window.__CS249R_CHAPTERS__["vol2/13_security_privacy"] = {"key": "vol2/13_security_privacy", "sections": [{"id": "sec-1", "level": 2, "title": "Purpose"}, {"id": "sec-2", "level": 2, "title": "The Expanded Attack Surface"}, {"id": "sec-3", "level": 2, "title": "Security and Privacy Foundations"}, {"id": "sec-4", "level": 3, "title": "Security defined"}, {"id": "sec-5", "level": 3, "title": "Privacy defined"}, {"id": "sec-6", "level": 3, "title": "Security versus privacy"}, {"id": "sec-7", "level": 2, "title": "Learning from Security Breaches"}, {"id": "sec-8", "level": 3, "title": "Stuxnet and supply chain compromise"}, {"id": "sec-9", "level": 3, "title": "Insufficient isolation in the Jeep Cherokee hack"}, {"id": "sec-10", "level": 3, "title": "Weaponized endpoints and the Mirai botnet"}, {"id": "sec-11", "level": 2, "title": "Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment"}, {"id": "sec-12", "level": 3, "title": "Security threat modeling for ML systems"}, {"id": "sec-13", "level": 3, "title": "Threat prioritization framework"}, {"id": "sec-14", "level": 2, "title": "Model-Specific Attack Vectors"}, {"id": "sec-15", "level": 3, "title": "Model theft"}, {"id": "sec-16", "level": 3, "title": "Leakage through authorized outputs"}, {"id": "sec-17", "level": 3, "title": "Data poisoning"}, {"id": "sec-18", "level": 3, "title": "Adversarial attacks"}, {"id": "sec-19", "level": 3, "title": "LLM-specific attack vectors"}, {"id": "sec-20", "level": 3, "title": "Availability abuse and denial of wallet"}, {"id": "sec-21", "level": 2, "title": "Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries"}, {"id": "sec-22", "level": 3, "title": "Gradient synchronization integrity"}, {"id": "sec-23", "level": 3, "title": "Collective traffic confidentiality"}, {"id": "sec-24", "level": 3, "title": "Shared accelerators and caches"}, {"id": "sec-25", "level": 3, "title": "Attestation and key custody at fleet scale"}, {"id": "sec-26", "level": 2, "title": "Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities"}, {"id": "sec-27", "level": 3, "title": "Hardware bugs"}, {"id": "sec-28", "level": 3, "title": "Physical attacks"}, {"id": "sec-29", "level": 3, "title": "Fault injection attacks"}, {"id": "sec-30", "level": 3, "title": "Side-channel attacks"}, {"id": "sec-31", "level": 3, "title": "Leaky interfaces"}, {"id": "sec-32", "level": 3, "title": "Supply chain risks"}, {"id": "sec-33", "level": 2, "title": "Comprehensive Defense Architectures"}, {"id": "sec-34", "level": 3, "title": "Hardware security foundations"}, {"id": "sec-35", "level": 3, "title": "Secure model deployment"}, {"id": "sec-36", "level": 3, "title": "Runtime system monitoring"}, {"id": "sec-37", "level": 3, "title": "Secure model design"}, {"id": "sec-38", "level": 3, "title": "Privacy-preserving data techniques"}, {"id": "sec-39", "level": 2, "title": "Differential Privacy"}, {"id": "sec-40", "level": 3, "title": "Mathematical foundations and privacy parameters"}], "html": "<h1 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h1\">Security &amp; Privacy</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sec-1\" class=\"cs-h2\">Purpose</h2>\n<p><em>Why can a correctly functioning machine learning service still disclose sensitive information or enable an attack?</em></p>\n<p>Many useful machine learning systems depend on personal data, institutional knowledge, or behavioral patterns, creating tension between utility and protection. Models can encode sensitive information and later reveal it through their outputs, extending risk beyond transient data processing. An authorized request can expose a private training example, while accepted training data can be manipulated to alter future behavior. In both cases, the software may execute as designed while the service violates its intended boundaries. Protecting a single server therefore leaves questions about what the model learned, who supplied it, and what callers can induce it to do. Scaling introduces more participants, copies, credentials, and handoffs, often across organizations with different responsibilities. A control applied at one boundary may leave another exposed, and a compromised participant can affect work shared across the fleet. The engineering decision is which actors to trust with which information and authority, then how to enforce those choices throughout training, release, and operation. Protection also consumes resources: restricting access can complicate coordination, checking requests adds work, and limiting information release can reduce model utility. Those costs must fit the service's operating budget without making safeguards optional when demand rises. A high-performing model may still be undeployable if sensitive information or control escapes the intended boundary. In C³ terms, Compute, Communication, and Coordination must preserve confidentiality, integrity, and accountable control as well as throughput.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Learning Objectives</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Distinguish security from privacy in ML systems through formal definitions, threat models, and quantitative trade-offs</li>\n<li>Explain how a correctly executing model can disclose memorized training data through authorized outputs</li>\n<li>Analyze ML-specific attack vectors across model theft, data poisoning, adversarial examples, fleet-internal gradient exchange, and hardware vulnerabilities</li>\n<li>Apply lessons from historical breaches and a layered maturity model to prioritize controls for a deployment's threat model</li>\n<li>Design layered defenses across data, model, runtime, and hardware, weighing each control by the trust boundary it protects and the compute, communication, or coordination cost it imposes</li>\n<li>Specify privacy-accounting inputs and assess reported budgets against sampling assumptions, composition, and measured utility</li>\n<li>Select rollback and forensic responses for a suspect model by tracing its provenance through the release record</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"sec-2\" class=\"cs-h2\">The Expanded Attack Surface</h2>\n<p>A serving fleet can meet its latency target while an authenticated client collects enough responses to copy model behavior. A federated training job can converge while a participating device submits poisoned updates. The release gates and hierarchical monitoring that govern the fleet can report both systems healthy, because a copied model and a planted backdoor need not move any availability, latency, or aggregate-quality signal. Operational health alone therefore cannot establish security. In the fleet stack (@fig-fleet-stack), the governance band begins with the Assurance row, which sits directly above Operations and which this chapter shares with @sec-robust-ai. That row must preserve the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of the fleet across the same interfaces that make distributed training and serving possible.</p>\n<p>Selecting a defense starts by identifying the protected asset, the actor who threatens it, the access that actor has, and the boundary that access must not cross. An API client can observe responses; a training participant can submit updates; a host administrator may inspect runtime state. Those capabilities call for different controls. The breach cases make these boundary failures concrete, the threat model turns them into explicit assumptions, and the later mechanisms enforce those assumptions at measurable compute, communication, and coordination cost.</p>\n<p>In conventional data systems, those boundary failures reduce to stealing database records, altering them, or disrupting access to them. Machine learning adds a learned representation through which those failures can propagate. An adversary may probe a model for memorized training examples or poison its training data to introduce a backdoor that activates on a chosen trigger. Protecting the stored records therefore does not fully protect the behavior learned from them or the information exposed through predictions.</p>\n<p>Learned-information exposure follows from the difference between <em>transient processing</em> and <em>persistent learning</em>. Many applications process sensitive data without encoding it into learned parameters; machine learning systems can preserve patterns from training data in their model parameters. Sensitive information may then remain in a model after the original processing has ended and become accessible through outputs or systematic interrogation. Queries to a healthcare model may expose memorized patient information, while queries to a proprietary model may reveal enough behavior to train a substitute. The first threatens training-data privacy (@sec-security-privacy-leakage-authorized-outputs); the second threatens model confidentiality.</p>\n<p>Operational platforms manage hundreds of models across distributed infrastructure, extending the attack surface across workers, devices, and administrative domains. In synchronous data-parallel training, one compromised worker contributes directly to the gradient that every replica applies, and the collective traffic carries that contribution across the training fabric. Its confidentiality depends on the fabric's isolation and encryption controls. Federated aggregation creates another observation boundary because model updates can reveal information even when raw records remain on devices. Accelerators and caches shared among tenants introduce extraction and leakage risks that also exist on a single shared machine. @Sec-security-privacy-fleet-trust-boundaries develops these fleet-internal boundaries. Scaling the system requires identifying which actors can observe or alter each exchange.</p>\n<p>↩ Multi-tenant platforms and the isolation they require are established in @sec-ml-operations-scale-multitenancy-isolation-c3ff.</p>\n<p>Architectural complexity compounds these challenges. A contemporary ML deployment spans data ingestion pipelines, distributed training infrastructure, model serving systems, and continuous monitoring frameworks, each introducing distinct vulnerabilities as @fig-attack-surface maps across the ML lifecycle. Continuous adaptation at edge nodes and federated coordination protocols add further entry points, each with its own trust assumptions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-3\" class=\"cs-h2\">Security and Privacy Foundations</h2>\n<p>Defending that expanded attack surface starts by separating two concerns that often share the same vocabulary. Security asks how an adversary can steal, manipulate, or disable the fleet; privacy asks how sensitive information can leak or be inferred even when the system behaves as designed. Keeping those questions separate determines which controls belong at each layer: authentication and isolation around adversarial pathways, provenance and monitoring across the release chain, and privacy accounting wherever learned parameters carry traces of the data that produced them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-4\" class=\"cs-h3\">Security defined</h3>\n<p>The first half of that separation is security, which defends model parameters, training pipelines, deployment infrastructure, and inference interfaces against intentional adversarial behavior. An attacker who crafts an adversarial patch on a stop sign, steals model weights from an unauthenticated bucket, or floods a generative endpoint with cost-maximizing queries compromises the system's integrity, confidentiality, or availability.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Security</strong></em> is the set of system properties (confidentiality, integrity, and availability) that protect an ML system's data, model weights, and inference pipeline from intentional adversarial actions, spanning both the infrastructure layer (network intrusion, credential theft) and the algorithmic layer (model extraction, prompt injection, adversarial examples).</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Security failures operate on both surfaces simultaneously. At the infrastructure layer, theft of a large proprietary model represents direct IP loss. At the algorithmic layer, model extraction via black-box queries can recover decision boundaries or distill a functional approximation of a deployed model at a small fraction of its training cost (@sec-security-privacy-model-theft-1879 quantifies the query budgets), bypassing the investment and competitive moat. Either failure transfers the value of the original training investment to the attacker without the attacker paying its compute cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Security specifies an intentional adversary and the boundaries that actor can cross. Robustness evaluates how model behavior changes under specified perturbations, including both accidental shifts and adversarial inputs; the two concerns overlap when the perturbation comes from an attacker.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that traditional IT security (firewalls, access controls, encryption) adequately secures ML systems. ML introduces an algorithmic attack surface orthogonal to infrastructure: a properly authenticated API request containing an adversarial input or a prompt injection bypasses all network-layer defenses and manipulates model behavior through the model's own learned functions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A facial recognition system deployed in public transit infrastructure, for example, may be targeted with adversarial inputs that cause it to misidentify individuals or fail entirely, representing a runtime security vulnerability that threatens both accuracy and system availability. The confidentiality-integrity-availability triad matters because each property breaks a different system contract. Confidentiality protects data and model access. Integrity protects the correctness of training and inference behavior. Availability protects the service from being disabled or degraded when it is needed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-5\" class=\"cs-h3\">Privacy defined</h3>\n<p>Whereas security addresses adversarial manipulation and unauthorized access, privacy limits the exposure and inference of sensitive information across training data, inference inputs, and model outputs. An authorized user querying a clinical classifier or a language model can extract a patient's sensitive diagnosis or verbatim personal record without ever breaching a firewall or stealing a password. Privacy protections must therefore account for unintended disclosure during ordinary operation. They must also account for active adversaries who choose queries, combine outputs with auxiliary information, or exploit repeated releases to infer sensitive facts.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Privacy</strong></em> is the protection of sensitive information from unauthorized disclosure, inference, and misuse across the ML lifecycle.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: It limits the exposure risk of training data and user inputs. Differentially private training introduces a utility-privacy trade-off: tighter privacy bounds generally require more noise or data, which can increase the training compute, the $\\IronLawComputeTime$ term of the Fleet Law (@eq-fleet-law), required to reach a target utility.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike confidentiality (which focuses on access control), privacy in ML focuses on inference risks: the ability of a user, operator, or adversary to infer sensitive facts or reconstruct training samples from model outputs, weights, or repeated releases.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that removing names (de-identification) is sufficient for privacy. In reality, neural networks are correlation engines that can inadvertently memorize and leak unique snippets of sensitive data through high-dimensional patterns.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Privacy failures are not limited to raw records or explicit identifiers. Aggregated behavioral traces can reveal sensitive routines when the surrounding geography makes individuals and sites easy to infer. Differential privacy (DP) bounds how much the distribution of released results can change when one protected contribution changes. Mechanisms can achieve this guarantee through calibrated randomization, such as adding noise to a query result. The protected contribution may be one record or all records from one person; the guarantee must specify which. Its utility cost depends on the task, data, mechanism, and training procedure.</p>\n<p>Differential privacy provides one mechanism-level guarantee; it does not provide lifecycle privacy by itself. A system must still minimize collection, establish an appropriate basis for use, enforce access controls, limit retention, honor deletion obligations, and prevent raw inputs or sensitive outputs from leaking through logs and caches. These controls answer questions that a formal privacy bound does not: whether the data should have been collected, who may use them, how long derived artifacts remain, and which copies must be removed. Production privacy therefore composes formal accounting with data-governance controls across collection, training, serving, telemetry, and retirement.</p>\n<p>Before the formal treatment of differential privacy, the chapter needs only one knob, the privacy budget $\\PrivacyBudget$. When one protected contribution changes, the probability of any released outcome changes by at most a factor of $e^{\\PrivacyBudget}$, apart from a small additive slack the formal definition allows, so smaller values give a tighter bound, and $\\PrivacyBudget$ is not an accuracy percentage. Because additional data-dependent DP releases about the same data spend more of that budget, the budget belongs to the history of releases rather than to any one server. Using a fixed DP-trained model does not spend additional training privacy budget. @Sec-security-privacy-differential-privacy-8c2b states the formal definition, its additive slack term, and the accounting that tracks spending across releases, once the defense architectures that surround it are in place.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-6\" class=\"cs-h3\">Security versus privacy</h3>\n<p>Security and privacy intersect in controls such as encrypted storage, yet they differ in their objectives, threat models, and mitigation strategies. Encrypting a hospital's training bucket stops an external attacker from stealing files, whereas the model trained on that bucket can still reveal whether a specific patient was in the cohort through an ordinary, authorized prediction query. These security-privacy distinctions matter because the two domains optimize for different failure modes. Their distinct goals carry through to typical concerns, example attacks, the defenses practitioners must consider, and regulatory standing (@tbl-security-privacy-comparison).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Aspect</strong></th><th><strong>Security</strong></th><th><strong>Privacy</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Primary Goal</strong></td><td>Prevent unauthorized access or disruption</td><td>Limit exposure of sensitive information</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Threat Model</strong></td><td>Adversarial actors (external or internal)</td><td>Passive exposure and active inference with auxiliary information</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Typical Concerns</strong></td><td>Model theft, poisoning, evasion attacks</td><td>Data leakage, re-identification, memorization</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Example Attack</strong></td><td>Adversarial inputs cause misclassification</td><td>Model inversion infers sensitive training-data properties</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Representative Defenses</strong></td><td>Access control, adversarial training</td><td>Differential privacy; federation with accounted privacy controls</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Relevance to Regulation</strong></td><td>Emphasized in cybersecurity standards</td><td>Central to data protection laws (for example, GDPR)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Security-Privacy Distinctions</strong>: Machine learning systems require distinct approaches to security and privacy; security mitigates adversarial threats targeting system functionality, while privacy protects sensitive information from both intentional and unintentional exposure through data leakage or re-identification. This table clarifies how differing goals and threat models shape the specific concerns and mitigation strategies for each domain. {#tbl-security-privacy-comparison}</p>\n<p>Security and privacy are deeply interrelated but not interchangeable. A secure system helps maintain privacy by restricting unauthorized access to models and data. Privacy-preserving designs can improve security by reducing the attack surface; minimizing the retention of sensitive data reduces the risk of exposure if a system is compromised.</p>\n<p>However, their controls can impose competing requirements. Differential privacy bounds information disclosed by a release but may lower model utility. Encryption restricts who can inspect protected data, so an audit that needs those data requires an authorized inspection path and appropriate key custody. The design must preserve useful evidence while limiting what that evidence reveals.</p>\n<p>Security and privacy therefore constrain the same system through different boundaries. Access control, cryptography, trusted execution, and differential privacy address different failure modes; their combination must protect model assets, limit data exposure, and retain the evidence needed to investigate failures. The breach histories make those boundaries concrete by tracing how aggregate releases, compromised components, and excessive access can expose information or alter system behavior.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-7\" class=\"cs-h2\">Learning from Security Breaches</h2>\n<p>A public heatmap built for runners became a military-intelligence leak when sparse GPS traces outlined movement around sensitive sites. That privacy failure belongs beside three landmark security breaches because each case turns an abstract control objective into an operational constraint, showing how behavioral telemetry can identify people, supply-chain compromise can alter physical systems, weak isolation can expose safety-critical control paths, and default credentials can turn cheap devices into attack infrastructure. Although most of these incidents did not target ML systems directly, every failure mode they demonstrated has a direct analog in training pipelines, inference APIs, edge deployments, and privacy-preserving data products. The 2018 Strava heatmap illustrates how an aggregate data release can fail on privacy without a single security intrusion, while the three security breaches that follow trace supply-chain, isolation, and endpoint failures.</p>\n<p><strong>Context</strong>: In late January 2018, public scrutiny of Strava's global activity heatmap, an aggregate built from users' uploaded GPS traces, revealed that it exposed activity at military bases [@russell2018strava]. The release showed patterns of movement without displaying a list of named participants.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong>: Aggregation hides individuals only where many people contribute. In sparsely populated regions, the few bright tracks came largely from personnel exercising on and around bases, so the heatmap traced base perimeters and the routes people ran, even where a site's location was already known.</p>\n<p><strong>Impact</strong>: The map exposed an operational-security risk in consumer telemetry. An aggregate intended to help people find exercise routes also told an observer where personnel moved routinely, prompting reviews of how fitness devices were used near sensitive facilities.</p>\n<p><strong>Design response</strong>: A system releasing location aggregates must assess what sparse regions reveal, limit sensitive contributions, and make participation controls understandable. These are design requirements suggested by the incident, not evidence that a particular product change eliminated the risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems lesson</strong>: Privacy failures can occur without names, passwords, or model weights leaking. Aggregation alone does not establish privacy when the released pattern and its surrounding context reveal sensitive behavior.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-8\" class=\"cs-h3\">Stuxnet and supply chain compromise</h3>\n<p>Discovered in 2010, the Stuxnet worm used four zero-day vulnerabilities (flaws unknown to the vendor and therefore unpatched) among its infection and propagation mechanisms, including spread through infected USB media. It targeted Siemens Step7 software and the programmable logic controllers (PLCs) it managed in industrial systems, including the air-gapped environment at Iran's Natanz nuclear facility [@farwell2011]. Altered control behavior accompanied by apparently normal telemetry demonstrated that operational health signals can conceal a compromise. An analogous ML failure occurs when poisoned training data or a backdoored model changes selected predictions while the inference server continues to meet its latency and availability targets. The common mechanism is a violation of behavioral integrity that ordinary service-health monitoring need not detect.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Air-gapped</strong>: Physical network separation does not eliminate transfer paths through removable media or authorized personnel. An isolated ML training cluster still needs a controlled process for admitting frameworks, datasets, and pretrained weights; each transfer must preserve the intended trust boundary.</p>\n<p>ML supply chains that ingest public packages, datasets, model weights, or firmware face four analogous vectors: compromised dependencies (malicious packages in PyPI and conda repositories), poisoned datasets on public platforms, backdoored model weights in model repositories, and tampered accelerator firmware. Each can introduce behavior through an artifact the pipeline accepts. Because a model's behavior depends on every contributor to its data, code, weights, and hardware, the model can be trusted only as far as a verifiable record of those contributors extends. That statement is the provenance bound on trust (principle \\ref{pri-provenance-bounds-trust}), and it carries the Stuxnet lesson to every artifact an ML pipeline admits. Artifact signatures (cryptographic checks that show which key holder produced an artifact and that it has not changed since) and immutable provenance logs establish origin and integrity evidence; controlled dependency admission limits which artifacts enter the pipeline. Behavioral testing addresses a different question, because an authentic, unchanged artifact can still contain a backdoor. Each stage of the Stuxnet attack chain has a counterpart in the ML supply chain (@fig-stuxnet).</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-9\" class=\"cs-h3\">Insufficient isolation in the Jeep Cherokee hack</h3>\n<p>Where Stuxnet entered through trusted supply-chain artifacts before execution, the 2015 Jeep Cherokee hack showed what happens when an already deployed component can reach across an internal boundary into safety-critical control. Security researchers remotely compromised a Jeep Cherokee's engine, transmission, and braking systems by exploiting a vulnerability in the vehicle's internet-connected Uconnect entertainment system, without physical access to the car [@miller2015remote; @miller2019]. Insufficient isolation allowed the compromised entertainment system to reach safety-critical controller area network bus controllers and manipulate vehicle functions.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; margin-video \"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MK0SrxBC1xs&amp;ab_channel=WIRED\" \"Jeep Cherokee Hack\" \"WIRED\" &gt;}}</p>\n<p>The ML lesson concerns authority across a trust boundary. Perception or diagnostic models may need to communicate with actuator controllers, but compromise of an externally reachable inference component must not grant unrestricted control authority. Segmentation, authenticated narrow gateways, sandboxed model execution, and minimal privileges constrain that path. Controllers must validate permitted commands and apply a system-specific safe response when inputs are invalid or communication fails; a network path makes the attack possible only if the controller accepts what arrives over it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-10\" class=\"cs-h3\">Weaponized endpoints and the Mirai botnet</h3>\n<p>The Jeep compromise moved inward from one exposed interface to a critical controller; the 2016 Mirai botnet reversed that direction by turning hundreds of thousands of weakly protected edge endpoints into a collective weapon. Mirai exploited weak credentials on Internet of Things (IoT) devices, including cameras, digital video recorders, and routers, to build infrastructure for distributed denial-of-service (DDoS) attacks. The longitudinal study of Mirai measured a peak of about 600,000 infections and documented attacks against services including Dyn, whose disruption affected other internet services [@antonakakis2017understanding]. The scaling lesson is that many individually limited devices can collectively threaten much larger services.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; margin-video \"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1pywzRTJDaY\" \"Mirai Botnet\" \"Vice News\" &gt;}}</p>\n<p>For ML edge deployments, a compromised endpoint may expose more than bandwidth. Its accessible records can include images or transcripts, and its enrolled federated-learning identity may let an attacker submit malicious updates. The defense must match those capabilities. Device-specific credentials limit shared-secret exposure, authenticated encrypted channels protect communication, and secure boot constrains which startup software is accepted. Hardware security modules (HSMs), tamper-resistant devices that hold keys and perform cryptographic operations without exposing those keys to host memory, can protect key custody where the platform supports them. Monitoring provides evidence of misuse, while update validation remains necessary because an authenticated device can still send harmful data.</p>\n<p>Together, the Strava, Stuxnet, Jeep, and Mirai incidents expose failures at four different boundaries: an aggregate release, accepted components, paths to safety-critical control, and endpoint credentials. The engineering task is to identify the asset and attacker capability at each boundary before choosing a control. A threat model makes those assumptions explicit, so defenses can be evaluated against the failures they are intended to prevent and the resources they consume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-11\" class=\"cs-h2\">Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment</h2>\n<p>An authenticated request can still probe a model for memorized data, and an accepted training sample can still carry a backdoor. Network security and user authentication therefore need a threat model that includes learned behavior. Training data can be manipulated to embed backdoors, input perturbations can exploit learned decision boundaries, and systematic API queries can expose enough behavior to train a substitute. Each attack uses an interface the system needs for ordinary work.</p>\n<p>Each of these vectors requires a formal <strong>threat model</strong>, written down as four fields: asset, boundary, adversary, and control. The asset defines what must remain confidential, intact, or available. The boundary defines where trust changes, such as between training data and model registry, user prompt and system instruction, or tenant workload and shared accelerator. The adversary field records the actor's capability (what it can access, observe, or modify) and its goal (what it seeks to compromise). The control identifies a permission, isolation mechanism, or observable signal that constrains the attack or supports a response. Without those fields, a risk matrix cannot explain why a particular defense deserves resources. Attack cost and expected benefit help assess feasibility, but the model must also cover actors whose objective is disruption rather than financial return.</p>\n<p>For a federated training round, suppose the coordinator may inspect messages but follows the aggregation protocol, while a compromised enrolled client may submit an arbitrary update. The same update crosses two boundaries. Hiding individual contributions from the coordinator addresses confidentiality; checking or limiting a client contribution addresses integrity. Authenticating the client establishes who sent an update, not whether the update is benign. A defense comparison is meaningful only after stating which of these actors, capabilities, and possible collusions it covers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-12\" class=\"cs-h3\">Security threat modeling for ML systems</h3>\n<p>Turning those four fields into a repeatable audit across a full ML system is the job of systematic threat modeling, which characterizes the attack surface and guides defensive investments before any control is selected. Because learned behavior couples data, parameters, and serving interfaces, that inventory must account for dependence on training data, statistical decision boundaries, and distributed deployment patterns across every layer of the stack.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h4\">Attack surface analysis</h4>\n<p>The attack surface of an ML system encompasses all points where an adversary can interact with or observe the system. It includes the lifecycle risks of ordinary software, such as compromised dependencies, as well as the data and learned state that determine model behavior. Decomposing this surface into data, model, interface, and infrastructure layers identifies where a control can constrain the attacker and what remains exposed beyond that control.</p>\n<p>The training data pipeline lets an adversary influence behavior before the model exists. At collection endpoints, raw data enters through application logs, sensors, forms, APIs, or uploads. Training corpora then reside in object stores, warehouses, feature stores, or dataset registries. Preprocessing pipelines normalize, filter, augment, and join these inputs, while human annotation, weak-labeling rules, or model-assisted labeling supply the targets used for learning. Dataset manifests and lineage systems identify which snapshot enters a release. A compromised source can affect successive model versions if the pipeline continues to ingest its records, and a backdoor may persist when training reuses an affected checkpoint. Retraining alone therefore does not establish that the compromised contribution has been removed.</p>\n<p>The model itself presents multiple attack surfaces spanning training infrastructure, model storage and versioning systems, model serialization and deserialization code, hyperparameter configuration management, and gradient computation and aggregation processes. Attacks at the model layer can compromise integrity by embedding trojans during training, extract intellectual property through parameter theft, or manipulate behavior through weight poisoning.</p>\n<p>Deployed models expose additional attack surfaces through inference API endpoints and load balancers, authentication and authorization systems, input validation and preprocessing logic, output formatting and response generation, and monitoring and logging infrastructure. At this interface layer, input checks enforce the accepted request contract, query limits constrain collection, and output controls restrict the information returned. An adversarial input can satisfy the request contract, and a permitted response can still reveal useful model behavior, so these controls need evaluation against the specified attack.</p>\n<p>The underlying computational infrastructure presents traditional attack surfaces amplified by ML-specific concerns, including accelerator firmware and drivers, container orchestration and scheduling systems, network communication between distributed training nodes, key management and secrets infrastructure, and supply chain for ML frameworks and dependencies. Infrastructure compromises can affect all systems running on shared resources, making this layer critical for multi-tenant ML platforms.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h4\">Threat vector classification</h4>\n<p>Threat-vector classification matters because the same model failure demands different defenses when the attacker has only API access, partial design knowledge, or full insider access. The three axes are access type, auxiliary knowledge, and attack timing.</p>\n<p>Access type specifies how the adversary can inspect or interact with the model. Under black-box access, the available interface returns outputs for submitted inputs. It can support model extraction [@tramer2016stealing] and query- or transfer-based adversarial attacks [@papernot2016transferability] without exposing the target's parameters. Gray-box access adds partial internal information, such as the architecture or training procedure. White-box access supplies the model's parameters and architecture, enabling direct gradient-based attacks (which follow the model's gradient with respect to its input to find a small change that alters the output) [@goodfellow2015explaining] and parameter copying when the files are accessible. These labels describe model visibility; they do not grant permission to alter training data, replace weights, or modify the runtime. Those write capabilities must be stated separately. Each access level, which the literature also calls a knowledge level, has a typical attack it enables and a typical deployment where it arises (@tbl-adversary-knowledge-spectrum).</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Adversary knowledge level</strong></th><th><strong>Model access</strong></th><th><strong>Training data access</strong></th><th><strong>Attack example</strong></th><th><strong>Common scenario</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>White-box</strong></td><td>Full access to architecture and parameters</td><td>Specified separately</td><td>Crafting adversarial examples using gradients</td><td>Insider threats, open-source model reuse</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Gray-box</strong></td><td>Partial access (e.g., architecture only)</td><td>Specified separately</td><td>Attacks based on surrogate model approximation</td><td>Known model family, unknown fine-tuning</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Black-box</strong></td><td>No internal access; only query-response view</td><td>Specified separately</td><td>Query-based surrogate model training and transfer attacks</td><td>Public APIs, model-as-a-service deployments</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Adversarial Knowledge Spectrum</strong>: Model access defines the white-box, gray-box, and black-box distinction used here; training-data access is specified independently. An open-weight model can expose its parameters while keeping its training records private. The table categorizes these models by access level, typical attack methods, and common deployment scenarios, clarifying the practical challenges of securing machine learning systems. {#tbl-adversary-knowledge-spectrum}</p>\n<p>Auxiliary knowledge adds the background information available beyond the exposed interface. A black-box client may know the application domain, public architecture documentation, or examples resembling the training distribution. An insider may know the deployment configuration without being able to read every training record. Model visibility and auxiliary knowledge therefore overlap, but neither replaces an explicit inventory of what the adversary knows. Claims of full knowledge must identify whether they include training records, preprocessing, and defense settings as well as model parameters.</p>\n<p>Attack timing determines where the defense can still intervene across the system lifecycle. Training-time attacks, such as data poisoning or backdoor injection, manipulate the learning process before the model artifact is produced. Deployment-time attacks compromise model distribution, serialization, or container installation before serving begins. Once in production, inference-time attacks exploit the deployed model through adversarial inputs or extraction queries. Finally, postdeployment attacks target online model updates, monitoring telemetry, or operational feedback loops after the model is already in service. The defense surface therefore moves in lockstep with the model lifecycle, from data controls to artifact integrity to serving-time containment.</p>\n<p>Combining these dimensions defines a testable threat scenario. A public API adversary might query a deployed classifier, know its task and output format, and lack access to its parameters or training records. A training insider might inspect the model and alter a specified subset of examples before fitting begins. The second scenario permits poisoning because it grants a write capability, not merely because the attacker knows more about the model.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h4\">Defense strategy framework</h4>\n<p>Effective defense against the threat vectors identified by the threat model requires a layered strategy that addresses each attack surface while accounting for adversary capabilities. The defense framework operates on three principles: defense in depth, minimal attack surface, and fail-safe defaults.</p>\n<p>No single defensive mechanism provides complete protection. The principle of <strong>defense in depth</strong> requires multiple independent layers such that compromising one layer does not grant full system access. For ML systems, this means combining data validation with model robustness techniques, access controls with output perturbation, and software defenses with hardware security mechanisms.</p>\n<p>The principle of <strong>minimal attack surface</strong> treats every exposed interface, stored artifact, and network endpoint as a potential entry or observation point. An API should expose only the functionality and output precision its users need, because detailed scores provide additional observations of model behavior. Encryption at rest protects stored models and training corpora when the attacker cannot also obtain their keys. Separating training clusters from inference endpoints constrains lateral movement, while protected access logs support investigation of sensitive operations. Each control reduces a particular exposure; none removes the need to examine the paths that remain.</p>\n<p>The principle of <strong>fail-safe defaults</strong> makes access depend on established permission rather than the absence of a detected threat. A request that cannot be authorized must not acquire privileges because a service failed. Behavioral anomalies require a separate response policy because they can also arise from legitimate traffic or distribution shift. Depending on the application, the system may reject a request, defer to a verified fallback, suspend a release, or isolate an affected component while preserving essential service. The safe response follows from the consequences of both continued operation and interruption; it cannot be inferred from an anomaly score alone.</p>\n<p>Each layer of the attack surface calls for its own defensive mechanisms and detection methods (@tbl-defense-mapping). Data defenses validate provenance, model defenses protect learned behavior and weights, API defenses govern query exposure, and infrastructure defenses anchor runtime trust.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Attack surface</strong></th><th><strong>Primary threats</strong></th><th><strong>Defensive mechanisms</strong></th><th><strong>Detection methods</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Data Layer</strong></td><td>Poisoning, label manipulation, supply chain compromise</td><td>Input validation, provenance tracking, secure data pipelines</td><td>Statistical anomaly detection, data quality monitoring</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Model Layer</strong></td><td>Backdoor injection, parameter theft, trojan insertion</td><td>Secure training environments, encrypted model storage, access controls</td><td>Model behavior analysis, weight distribution monitoring</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>API/Interface Layer</strong></td><td>Adversarial examples, model extraction, membership inference</td><td>Input sanitization, rate limiting, output perturbation, differential privacy</td><td>Query pattern analysis, confidence distribution monitoring</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Infrastructure Layer</strong></td><td>Side-channel attacks, firmware compromise, supply chain attacks</td><td>Trusted execution environments (TEEs), secure boot, network segmentation, dependency scanning</td><td>Hardware performance monitoring, integrity verification</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Defense Mapping by Attack Surface</strong>: Each layer of the ML system attack surface requires specific defensive mechanisms and detection methods. Effective security integrates protections across all layers while maintaining detection capabilities that can identify attacks that bypass preventive controls. {#tbl-defense-mapping}</p>\n<p>Attack surfaces, threat vectors, and the defense mapping together enumerate what could go wrong and which control addresses each failure. They do not say which failure to address first, and no deployment can fund every control at once.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-13\" class=\"cs-h3\">Threat prioritization framework</h3>\n<p>A team that spends months hardening against physical laser fault injection while leaving a public inference API without query budgets or loading untrusted pickle checkpoints (a Python file format that can run arbitrary code when loaded) from a public hub leaves its highest-probability attack paths wide open. Prioritization ranks the enumerated threats by how likely each is in the deployment and how much damage it would cause. Impact is not purely technical. Regulatory obligations raise the cost of some failures, as when an erasure request under the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) obliges the operator to trace personal data through derived datasets, logs, and deployed artifacts. A prioritization matrix based on likelihood and impact turns the threat model into an allocation decision. Teams automate defenses for threats that are both likely and damaging, prepare for rare but severe failures, and avoid spending scarce engineering time on low-value controls.</p>\n<p>: <strong>GDPR (General Data Protection Regulation)</strong>: Article 17 establishes a right to erasure under specified grounds and exceptions. For an ML pipeline, responding to an applicable request requires tracing personal data through source records, derived datasets, logs, and deployed artifacts. Whether a trained model requires remediation depends on the data it retains and the applicable obligation; deleting a source row does not establish that every derived copy or learned disclosure has been removed. Retraining and machine unlearning are possible technical responses whose effectiveness requires separate validation.</p>\n<p>Penalties make the regulatory part of impact concrete. In healthcare, where ML systems routinely handle patient data, a breach can violate the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), with significant legal and ethical consequences. Globally, for personal data within its territorial and material scope, GDPR authorizes fines up to 2 percent of total worldwide annual turnover when an organization fails to implement appropriate security measures, and up to 4 percent for violations of its core processing principles or data-subject rights [@gdpr2016].</p>\n<p>The following placements illustrate a threat model rather than universal attack frequencies. Poisoning can fall in the high-likelihood/high-impact quadrant in a federated deployment that admits untrusted contributors and suffers severe consequences from manipulated predictions. Such a deployment needs controls on update integrity and a response path for suspected compromise. High-likelihood/low-impact extraction describes a different scenario in which public queries are accessible but behavioral copying creates limited competitive loss. Output restrictions and query budgets may then be proportionate controls, although their effectiveness still requires evaluation.</p>\n<p>The lower-likelihood quadrants receive different treatment. Low-likelihood/high-impact hardware side-channel attacks describe a deployment in which obtaining the necessary observations is difficult but a successful attack could reveal valuable secrets. The required access depends on the channel; some attacks use a physical probe, while others exploit shared resources or remotely observable timing. In the public API scenario used for this matrix, membership inference attacks, which test whether a particular record was part of the training set (@sec-security-privacy-membership-inference), may sit in a lower-likelihood/lower-impact quadrant than direct integrity failures. That placement must be reassessed when the training data is sensitive or the exposed outputs make membership easier to infer. The matrix records those deployment assumptions rather than assigning a permanent rank to each attack family.</p>\n<p>The framework guides resource allocation, as @fig-threat-matrix summarizes. The example prioritizes accessible model and data threats before more specialized hardware attacks. A deployment with sensitive co-tenants or exposed edge hardware may require a different order. The allocation decision depends on the expected harm, the control's effectiveness, and its engineering cost, not on the attack category alone.</p>\n<p>A team detects a high volume of API queries from a single IP address that are systematically exploring the decision boundary of a fraud detection model.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Lifecycle stage</strong>: identify whether the suspicious activity occurs during training, serving, or infrastructure operation.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Attack surface</strong>: identify the interface the attacker is exercising and infer what the query pattern reveals.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Defense mapping</strong>: select the rate-limiting, monitoring, or output-control mechanisms that should activate first.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An unusual query pattern is evidence to investigate, not proof of extraction. The threat model connects that observation to an actor's capabilities and the asset at risk. Specific attack mechanisms then explain how access to data, artifacts, or predictions can compromise the model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-14\" class=\"cs-h2\">Model-Specific Attack Vectors</h2>\n<p>A stop sign with strategically placed black and white stickers can remain recognizable to a human while an evaluated image classifier labels it as a speed limit sign. The attacker changes the observed input rather than the classifier's code or stored weights. This adversarial example illustrates why the lifecycle entry point matters. Training data, model artifacts, and runtime inputs give an attacker different ways to influence the system and require controls at different boundaries.</p>\n<p>The lifecycle entry point separates five objectives. Model theft targets confidentiality by obtaining artifacts or reproducing behavior from queries. Output leakage targets the training data, which the same queries can expose through the predictions the service is authorized to return. Poisoning targets training integrity by changing what the model learns. Adversarial examples target deployed behavior by changing the inputs a model receives. Availability abuse uses the same serving interface to exhaust the service's compute and cost budget rather than to change any prediction. Data poisoning therefore requires an intervention in the learning pipeline, whereas adversarial inputs and availability abuse call for controls on the deployed service.</p>\n<p>Written as the four-field threat model of @sec-security-privacy-systematic-threat-analysis-risk-assessment-3ef1, the objectives differ mainly in asset and adversary capability. Theft targets weights or behavior and needs either artifact access or query access, and output leakage targets the training data with nothing more than query access. Poisoning targets training integrity and needs write access to something the model learns from. Adversarial inputs target deployed decisions and need control over an input, while availability abuse targets the serving budget and needs nothing beyond an account. These objectives connect the attack surface analysis in @sec-security-privacy-security-threat-modeling-ml-systems-f60d to the controls available before training, during artifact handling, and at inference, while @sec-security-privacy-llmspecific-threat-vectors-65f2 examines how token-stream architectures fuse instructions, untrusted content, and retrieved data across those objectives.</p>\n<p>These entry points sit at different stages of the ML lifecycle (@fig-ml-lifecycle-threats). Upstream, an attacker may inject samples or alter labels in a data source the pipeline accepts. A backdoor can then remain inactive on ordinary evaluation inputs and activate on a chosen trigger. Downstream, deployed files expose an artifact-access boundary, while prediction APIs expose a query boundary. An adversarial input crosses the latter without requiring the attacker to alter the training pipeline.</p>\n<p>Controls must act at the relevant entry point and preserve their assumptions through later stages. Data validation limits what enters training, artifact controls authenticate what deployment loads, and serving controls constrain requests and returned information. None establishes behavioral correctness by itself; the threat model determines which attack each control must withstand.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-15\" class=\"cs-h3\">Model theft</h3>\n<p>A competitor spending a few thousand dollars on public API queries to train a surrogate model, or an insider copying unencrypted weights from a bucket, can replicate a model that cost millions of dollars to train [@oliynyk2023]. Model theft, the first of those five objectives, targets confidentiality by recovering a trained model's parameters, architecture, or output behavior, undermining its economic value or exposing private information encoded in its weights.</p>\n<p>Such threats arise across a range of deployment settings, including public APIs, cloud-hosted services, on-device inference engines, and shared model repositories. Prediction interfaces can leak enough signal to recover model functionality [@tramer2016stealing; @oliynyk2023], while classifier outputs can reveal meaningful information about training sets or learned structure [@ateniese2015]. Artifact handling introduces separate risks. Unsafe deserialization can execute attacker-supplied code, while insufficient access controls can expose model files or deployment packages directly.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Model serialization</strong>: Python's pickle format, common in PyTorch checkpoint workflows, can execute arbitrary code during deserialization, so loading an untrusted checkpoint is equivalent to running untrusted code. Formats such as SafeTensors store only tensor data and remove that execution path [@huggingface_safetensors].</p>\n<p>Model theft pursues two distinct objectives, and each requires different controls (@fig-model-theft-types). <strong>Exact artifact theft</strong> obtains the model file or deployment artifact through storage, registry, or device access. The attacker can reuse the recovered weights without repeating their training. <strong>Approximate behavioral theft</strong> uses API responses, such as labels, logits, embeddings, or confidence scores, to train a surrogate. It can exploit permitted queries without breaking authentication. The literature names this query-based branch in several ways. <em>Model extraction</em> and <em>model stealing</em> are synonyms for the attack, while <em>model distillation</em> names its usual training step, in which a surrogate learns from the victim's outputs. Its usual product is a behavioral surrogate, but the same query access can also recover selected internal parameters, a third outcome that the OpenAI results below demonstrate. Encryption, access control, and protected deployment paths restrict artifact access; signatures separately authenticate artifact integrity. Output limits, query budgets, auditing, and pricing constrain behavioral extraction, while watermarking can support attribution after copying.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Model extraction</strong></em> is the class of attacks in which an adversary, with only black-box query access to a deployed model's prediction interface, recovers the model's parameters, decision boundaries, or behavior by analyzing observable outputs.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: Successful extraction often costs orders of magnitude less than the original training. Published demonstrations have recovered logistic regression, neural-network, and decision-tree models from public machine-learning APIs with high fidelity and modest query cost [@tramer2016stealing]. Later work has reconstructed components of large language models, including OpenAI deployments studied by @carlini2024stealing, using only public query access. The BERT extraction example below illustrates the same dynamic on a transformer model, using only synthetic queries to train the copy.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Extraction differs from direct theft of model files (which requires intrusion into the storage layer) and from model inversion (which uses model outputs to infer training-data properties or reconstruct representative inputs rather than the model itself). Its attack surface is the model's query interface, so every deployed ML service faces it by construction whenever the model is reachable.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that rate limiting alone is a sufficient defense. An attacker willing to issue queries slowly, distribute requests across accounts, or accept a multi-month attack window may still extract useful behavior. Reducing output information, detecting systematic probing, and pricing queries can raise the cost or expose the attempt, but their combination does not guarantee prevention.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Model theft can also enable attacks on information encoded by the model. Extraction studies demonstrate recovery of useful behavior or selected internal parameters, although those outcomes do not imply complete replication of a large model or its training pipeline [@tramer2016stealing; @carlini2024stealing]. A competitor holding a stolen Archetype B (DLRM at Scale) recommendation model obtains its embedding tables, which store a learned vector for each value of each categorical feature. In a recommender those features commonly include user and item IDs, so the stolen tables hand the competitor a per-user vector shaped by that user's history, a natural starting point for inferring the user's attributes. Whether this exposes particular customer attributes depends on the model, accessible features, and auxiliary information. Model inversion attacks investigate that boundary by attempting to infer sensitive input properties from the learned model (@sec-security-privacy-model-inversion).</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario</strong>: Proprietary language models deployed behind public inference APIs return full probability vectors and confidence scores to client queries [@krishna2020thieves].</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnosis</strong>: The attacker needed no real training data. Because both the victim and the copy were fine-tuned from the same publicly released pretrained BERT, queries made of random word sequences shaped by simple task-specific heuristics supplied enough labeled outputs to train a student. That student recovered much of the victim's task performance on tasks including natural language inference and question answering [@krishna2020thieves]. With a shared public starting point, the API responses had to supply only the task-specific fine-tuning signal. Retaining task accuracy is distinct from agreeing with the target on each input; neither implies recovery of its exact parameters.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems lesson</strong>: API endpoints expose intellectual property when outputs carry raw decision-boundary logits. Serving platforms combine rate-limiting, query auditing, and confidence-score truncation to make model extraction more costly and detectable.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h4\">Exact artifact theft</h4>\n<p>Exact artifact theft targets the model's stored structure and learned parameters. The exposed path may be an artifact-download API, an on-device inference package, or a model repository. This differs from querying a prediction API: the attacker obtains the artifact itself. Weak access control or unprotected packaging can expose that artifact without requiring full control of the underlying infrastructure.</p>\n<p>The value of a stolen artifact depends on what it contains and what the attacker wants to reproduce. Learned parameters and architecture details can support inference when the necessary preprocessing and runtime are available. Training hyperparameters, including learning rate schedules, batch sizes, and regularization settings, help reproduce or extend training, but are not required merely to execute an existing checkpoint. Side-channel attacks, reverse engineering, or analysis of observable outputs may reveal additional structure when the artifact is incomplete. The threat model should therefore distinguish running a copied model from rebuilding the pipeline that produced it.</p>\n<p>: <strong>ML side-channel attacks</strong>: Timing or power observations can reveal properties of inference even when model files and query outputs are restricted. Recovering useful structure depends on the implementation, measurement access, and signal quality; physical proximity is a precondition for some channels, not a guarantee of success.</p>\n<p>System designers must account for these risks by separating artifact security from interface security. Artifact controls secure model files, registry entries, and deployment packages; interface controls restrict APIs, reduce output precision, audit query patterns, and price or rate-limit access so behavioral extraction becomes harder to perform economically [@tramer2016stealing; @oliynyk2023].</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h4\">Insider theft of engineering assets</h4>\n<p>Insider access is often the shortest path to artifact theft, because an insider needs no exploit to reach files that the development environment already grants. The two cases below involve documents and source code rather than model weights, and that difference is their lesson. The protected asset extends beyond the serialized model to the code and data that produced it. Former Google engineer Anthony Levandowski was accused of taking proprietary Waymo materials before founding a competing startup [@nytimes2017levandowski], a case about engineering intellectual property rather than demonstrated extraction of model weights. In March 2019, Tesla filed two trade-secret lawsuits involving former employees who left for autonomous-vehicle competitors [@korosec2019tesla]. One complaint alleged that employees who joined Zoox copied proprietary warehousing, logistics, and inventory-control documents. A separate complaint alleged that former Autopilot engineer Guangzhi Cao copied Tesla Autopilot source-code repositories before joining XPeng/XMotors.</p>\n<p>These allegations matter for ML systems because training code, feature pipelines, labeling workflows, deployment scripts, simulation assets, and operational playbooks can reveal enough about a system to accelerate a competitor's replication effort or expose downstream security weaknesses. API-level extraction defenses do not reach this boundary. Restricting prediction queries cannot prevent a developer from copying repositories that the developer can already access. Development access, artifact handling, and release provenance therefore belong in the same threat model as inference interfaces, with controls matched to the sensitivity of each asset.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h4\">Query-based extraction</h4>\n<p>Where artifact controls close direct file and repository access, approximate behavioral theft uses observed input-output pairs from those inference interfaces to train a substitute without copying the target's parameters. This application of model distillation, or knockoff modeling, can reproduce useful functionality from public API responses [@orekondy2019].</p>\n<p>Evaluation must distinguish task performance from agreement with the victim. A substitute can achieve similar benchmark accuracy while making different predictions; reproducing the victim's correct answers and mistakes measures behavioral fidelity. The service therefore has to limit accumulated observations while preserving the outputs legitimate users need.</p>\n<p>Query access can also expose internal parameters rather than only supply examples for a surrogate. In their paper, @carlini2024stealing show how to reconstruct the final embedding projection matrix of OpenAI's <code>ada</code> and <code>babbage</code> models, and to recover the hidden dimensionality (and estimate the cost of full matrix recovery) for <code>gpt-3.5-turbo</code>, using only public API access. That final projection maps a hidden state vector to a much larger token vocabulary, so every output logit vector lies in a linear subspace whose rank equals the model's hidden dimension. Collecting logit vectors across enough varied prompts until new responses become linearly dependent on earlier ones reveals where the rank saturates and recovers the hidden dimensionality. For the smaller models, solving for a basis of that subspace replicates the weight matrix up to affine transformations, because the logits alone cannot distinguish the true hidden basis from any invertible transformation of it. This partial parameter extraction is distinct from both copying a complete artifact and training a behavioral substitute. It stops short of recovering the full model, yet it shows that an API's exposed scores and query controls can reveal internal structure that its operator intended to keep private.</p>\n<p>The empirical results in @tbl-openai-theft demonstrate extraction of smaller models' output-projection parameters with root mean square errors on the order of $10^{-4}$, while the larger GPT-3.5 rows show dimension recovery and estimated recovery costs rather than implemented weight-matrix extraction. The production-interface attack used features such as top-$\\TopClassCount$ token log-probabilities together with logit-bias controls. The logit-bias control adds a caller-chosen offset to the logits of named tokens. Because a production API returns only the top-$\\TopClassCount$ log-probabilities rather than the full vocabulary vector, the caller can use that control to push selected tokens into the top-$\\TopClassCount$ set and read their relative logit values across prompts. The interaction between returned information and permitted query modifications therefore belongs in the threat model, even when neither feature exposes weights directly.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Model</strong></th><th><strong>Size</strong> <strong>(dimension extraction)</strong></th><th><strong>Number of queries</strong></th><th><strong>RMS</strong> <strong>(weight matrix extraction)</strong></th><th><strong>Cost</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>OpenAI ada</strong></td><td>1024 \\ding{51}</td><td>$&lt; 2 \\times 10^6$</td><td>$5 \\times 10^{-4}$</td><td>\\$1 / \\$4</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>OpenAI babbage</strong></td><td>2048 \\ding{51}</td><td>$&lt; 4 \\times 10^6$</td><td>$7 \\times 10^{-4}$</td><td>\\$2 / \\$12</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>OpenAI babbage-002</strong></td><td>1536 \\ding{51}</td><td>$&lt; 4 \\times 10^6$</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>\\$2 / \\$12</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>OpenAI gpt-3.5-turbo-instruct</strong></td><td>Not disclosed</td><td>$&lt; 4 \\times 10^7$</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>\\$200 / \\$2,000 (estimated)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>OpenAI gpt-3.5-turbo-1106</strong></td><td>Not disclosed</td><td>$&lt; 4 \\times 10^7$</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>\\$800 / \\$8,000 (estimated)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Model Stealing Costs</strong>: Attackers can recover internal model information with publicly available APIs. The Cost column gives two figures: the dimension-recovery cost and the full weight-matrix extraction cost. For OpenAI's ada and babbage models, implemented output-projection weight extraction achieves low root-mean-square error with fewer than $4 \\times 10^6$ queries at estimated costs of \\$1 to \\$12. The GPT-3.5 rows report dimension-recovery results and estimated full-recovery costs, not implemented weight-matrix extraction. {#tbl-openai-theft}</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h4\">Defenses against model extraction</h4>\n<p>Unlike data poisoning and adversarial perturbations, whose algorithmic defenses are developed in @sec-robust-ai, and training-data leakage, whose formal privacy bounds are developed in @sec-security-privacy-differential-privacy-8c2b, model extraction is countered directly at the serving API boundary. Its quantitative defenses therefore belong beside the attack, while @sec-security-privacy-secure-model-deployment-e08c covers artifact custody, cryptographic packaging, and endpoint authentication infrastructure.</p>\n<p>Model extraction defenses must account for what an adversary can accumulate through the API across four operational levers. Reducing output precision or returning fewer classes restricts the observations supplied by each response. Query quotas constrain collection per credential, and pricing increases its cost. Behavioral monitoring supplies evidence of systematic probing that can trigger investigation or enforcement. A daily rate limit only slows collection without bounding its lifetime total, and an attacker can combine observations from several accounts. The defense must therefore connect output design to cumulative budgets and the identities over which those budgets can actually be enforced.</p>\n<p>Monitoring supplies evidence for adjusting those controls. High query volume is one possible signal, although legitimate batch jobs can produce it and an extractor can deliberately slow collection. A high-traffic, general-purpose prediction API might begin with an illustrative volume rule:</p>\n<p>$$ \\text{alert}(\\text{user}) = \\begin{cases} 1 &amp; \\text{if } \\DailyAccountQueryCount&gt; 10{,}000 \\text{ or } \\HourlyAccountQueryCount&gt; 2{,}000 \\\\ 0 &amp; \\text{otherwise} \\end{cases} $$</p>\n<p>where $\\DailyAccountQueryCount$ and $\\HourlyAccountQueryCount$ represent query counts. Such thresholds are only the first signal. They must be calibrated by service tier and by the shape of normal use, because batch customers and attackers can both generate high volume.</p>\n<p>The next signal compares the inputs with expected use. Synthetic or systematically varied queries may reveal probing, but an extractor can also submit ordinary examples. A detector can compare the user's query distribution with a baseline chosen for the service and customer workload:</p>\n<p>$$ \\KlDivergence(\\AccountQueryDistribution\\lVert \\ExpectedQueryDistribution) &gt; \\ResponseThreshold $$</p>\n<p>where $\\KlDivergence$ is Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence, a measure of how far one probability distribution departs from another, and $\\ResponseThreshold$ is an alert threshold. A practical detector needs a tractable representation of inputs and enough observations to estimate those distributions. For a language model API, unusual token distributions or repetitive prompt templates may provide useful features. Regular inter-query intervals can add evidence of automation, but legitimate applications also automate requests and attackers can vary their timing.</p>\n<p>An anomaly detector combines these signals into a score that can drive enforcement:</p>\n<p>$$ \\text{score}_{\\text{anomaly}}(\\ApiAccount) = \\ExtractionAnomalyClassifier(\\text{qps}(\\ApiAccount), \\KlDivergence(\\ApiAccount), \\InterQueryIntervalDeviation(\\ApiAccount), \\ldots) $$</p>\n<p>where $\\ExtractionAnomalyClassifier$ is a trained classifier and the features capture query volume, distribution, and temporal regularity. The score supports a response policy rather than establishing that theft occurred. A service can tighten quotas, request additional verification, or refer activity for review, with thresholds calibrated against legitimate workload variation. Free, paid, and enterprise tiers can carry different limits, but account-level enforcement constrains an adversary only to the extent that collection across credentials is also controlled.</p>\n<p>A concrete quota ladder for the same general-purpose API makes that information cap visible. A free tier might allow <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.free_queries_per_day_str]</code> with a <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.free_burst_qps_str]</code> burst, a basic paid tier might allow <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.basic_queries_per_day_str]</code> with <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.basic_burst_qps_str]</code>, and an enterprise tier might allow <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.enterprise_queries_per_day_str]</code> with custom burst limits and contractual monitoring. The exact numbers depend on product needs, but the ladder matters because extraction cost scales with accumulated responses.</p>\n<p>An illustrative adaptive policy connects the anomaly score to a request limit. Higher scores reduce the quota, so false positives translate directly into lost capacity for legitimate workloads:</p>\n<p>$$ \\text{limit}<em>{\\text{effective}}(\\ApiAccount) = \\text{limit}</em>{\\text{base}} \\times \\exp(-\\QuotaReductionSensitivity\\cdot \\text{score}_{\\text{anomaly}}(\\ApiAccount)) $$</p>\n<p>where $\\QuotaReductionSensitivity$ controls sensitivity. With $\\QuotaReductionSensitivity=1$, a user with anomaly score <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.anomaly_score_str]</code> keeps about <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.capacity_remaining_pct_str]</code> of the base quota, a <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.rate_reduction_pct_str]</code> rate reduction. The calculation specifies enforcement strength, not detection accuracy. Its security effect also depends on the response: full logits, hidden states, or detailed confidence scores can make each permitted query more useful to an extractor. Quota design therefore needs an explicit output contract.</p>\n<p>Output shaping reduces the leakage per response while preserving the part of the answer legitimate users usually need. <strong>Confidence rounding</strong> returns coarser probabilities instead of full-precision logits or probabilities:</p>\n<p>$$ \\RoundedClassProbability_i = \\text{round}(\\PredictedClassProbability_i, \\RoundingDecimalCount) $$</p>\n<p>where $\\RoundingDecimalCount$ is the number of decimal places. For a <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.class_count_value_str]</code>-class ImageNet classifier, rounding from <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.from_decimals_str]</code> decimals to <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.to_decimals_str]</code> decimals removes <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.decimal_digits_removed_str]</code> decimal digits, about <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.bits_removed_str]</code> per class. The count uses a decimal-bin model, which treats each decimal digit as selecting one of ten equally likely levels, so every dropped digit removes $\\log_2 10 \\approx 3.3$ bits. This counts representational precision, not measured information about the model. Rounding can also create score ties, so the service must evaluate the effect on ranking and downstream decisions.</p>\n<p>The <strong>top-$\\TopClassCount$ truncation</strong> defense returns only the top-$\\TopClassCount$ predicted classes rather than the full probability distribution:</p>\n<p>$$ \\text{output} = \\{(\\ReturnedClassLabel_i, \\PredictedClassProbability_i) : \\LabelCategoryIndex\\in \\text{top-}\\TopClassCount\\} $$</p>\n<p>For $\\TopClassCount=$ <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.top_k_neighbors_str]</code> on a <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.class_count_value_str]</code>-class problem, the API returns only those top scores instead of the full softmax vector, eliminating <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.topk_eliminated_pct_str]</code> of the distribution entries an extractor could otherwise observe per query. Legitimate users often need only the top few predictions, while an extractor benefits from the full distribution.</p>\n<p>When rounded or truncated outputs still leak too much, calibrated noise can make repeated measurements less useful. Adding Gaussian noise to the score vector $\\PredictedOutput$ of a $\\Glyph{K}$-class model (uppercase $\\ClassCount$ for the class count, distinct from the $\\Glyph{k}$ of top-$\\TopClassCount$) can push some scores below zero, so the noisy vector is projected back onto a valid probability distribution:</p>\n<p>$$ \\NoisyClassProbabilityVector=\\SimplexProjection\\!\\left(\\PredictedOutput+\\ScoreNoiseVector\\right), \\qquad \\ScoreNoiseVector\\sim\\GaussianDistribution(\\ZeroVector,\\ScoreNoiseStandardDeviation^2 \\IdentityMatrix) $$</p>\n<p>where $\\SimplexProjection$ returns the closest valid distribution to the noisy vector, one whose scores are all nonnegative and sum to one $(\\NoisyClassProbability_i\\geq 0,\\ \\sum_i\\NoisyClassProbability_i=1)$. The set of such vectors is the probability simplex $\\ProbabilitySimplex^{\\ClassCount-1}$. The noise scale $\\ScoreNoiseStandardDeviation$ must balance extraction defense with utility preservation. Too little noise leaves the confidence surface measurable; too much noise changes decisions for legitimate users. This randomized response shaping is not, by itself, a differential privacy guarantee: such a claim also requires an adjacency relation, a sensitivity bound (the most one protected contribution can change the result), calibrated noise, and composition accounting.</p>\n<p>Differential privacy addresses a different objective when a response is computed from sensitive records. It bounds the change in the released distribution under a specified change to the protected data. @Sec-security-privacy-differential-privacy-8c2b develops the full $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ definition and the adjacent-dataset notation. Repeated interactive DP releases compose, but queries to a fixed DP-trained model are post-processing, computation on the released model that never reads the protected data again, and do not consume additional training privacy budget. Such queries may still enable model extraction, so output shaping and rate limits remain security controls.</p>\n<p>The final lever is economics. Consider a simplified comparison in which extraction and independent training produce equally useful substitutes and query charges represent the extraction cost. If training from scratch costs $\\TrainingCost$ and extraction requires $\\ExtractionQueryCount$ queries at price $\\QueryPrice$ per query, extraction is the cheaper option when:</p>\n<p>$$ \\ExtractionQueryCount\\cdot \\QueryPrice&lt; \\TrainingCost $$</p>\n<p>Setting $\\QueryPrice$ so that $\\ExtractionQueryCount\\cdot \\QueryPrice&gt; \\TrainingCost$ makes extraction costlier than independent training in this simplified equal-quality comparison. It does not remove incentives based on unavailable training data, competitive value, or objectives other than acquiring a substitute cheaply. For example, if training a competitive large model costs <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.training_cost_m_usd_str]</code> and extraction requires <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.extraction_queries_m_str]</code> queries, the break-even price is <code>[ExtractionDefenseReference.breakeven_usd_per_query_str]</code>; pricing above that makes extraction more expensive than retraining under those assumptions. The comparison must include the attacker's alternative costs of compute, data, and engineering. In practice, pricing, monitoring, quotas, and output shaping work together. Restricted outputs and coarse confidence bins reduce exposed detail, while quotas constrain access through the identities they cover. Neither control alone bounds all information an actor can accumulate across accounts, alternate endpoints, or other sources.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: For a production ResNet-50 image-classification API serving <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.daily_queries_str]</code> daily queries from <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.daily_users_str]</code>, how can the operator make model extraction economically unattractive without degrading normal service?</p>\n<p><strong>Scenario</strong>: The model costs about <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.training_cost_k_usd_str]</code> to train in this scenario. An extractor needs about <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.extraction_queries_m_str]</code> queries for <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.target_fidelity_pct_str]</code> fidelity, or <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.queries_per_class_str]</code> for a <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.class_count_value_str]</code>-class classifier. Without defenses, an attacker abusing a free tier can send <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.attacker_queries_per_day_str]</code> for <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.extraction_days_str]</code> and pay nothing, making extraction economically favorable. The service is far smaller than the general-purpose API above, so its tiers and alert thresholds are scaled to its own traffic, and its training cost sits three orders of magnitude below the large model of the break-even example.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism</strong>: The defense stack works as a sequence:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Cap collection</strong>: The free tier allows <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.free_tier_queries_per_day_str]</code>, the paid tier costs <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.paid_tier_price_usd_month_str]</code> for <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.paid_tier_queries_per_day_str]</code>, and enterprise access requires authentication plus monitoring.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitor probing</strong>: The monitoring path watches for systematic measurement behavior, including more than <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.alert_queries_per_day_str]</code> from one user or median inter-query gaps below <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.alert_interquery_ms_str]</code>; the lightweight detector adds <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.monitoring_ms_str]</code> per query.</li>\n<li><strong>Shape outputs</strong>: Output shaping rounds confidences to <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.to_decimals_str]</code> decimal places, retaining about <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.retained_bits_str]</code> from each rounded scalar probability, and returns only the top-<code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.top_k_neighbors_str]</code> classes, eliminating <code>[OutputRoundingLeakage.topk_eliminated_pct_str]</code> of distribution entries.</li>\n<li><strong>Price extraction</strong>: At pay-as-you-go <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.price_per_query_usd_str]</code> (above tier caps), <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.extraction_queries_m_str]</code> extraction queries cost <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.extraction_cost_k_usd_str]</code>, matching the training cost in this scenario. An attacker limited to the <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.paid_tier_price_usd_month_str]</code> tier (<code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.paid_tier_queries_per_day_str]</code>) would need <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.paid_tier_days_to_extract_str]</code> and about <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.paid_tier_extraction_cost_usd_str]</code> total, which is cheaper under these assumptions. A daily quota slows collection but does not cap its lifetime total.</li>\n<li><strong>Cap cumulative collection</strong>: A per-principal lifetime budget closes that route. If each principal's queries beyond an allowance well below <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.extraction_queries_m_str]</code> are billed at the pay-as-you-go <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.price_per_query_usd_str]</code>, assembling the full extraction set costs close to <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.extraction_cost_k_usd_str]</code> on any tier, the same as training the model.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Outcomes to measure</strong>: Whether the design works is decided by four measurements, namely the surrogate's accuracy, the accuracy legitimate users still receive, the latency the controls add, and the share of users flagged by mistake. The values below are constructed inputs that show the shape of that measurement, not results derived from the controls above. Suppose the surrogate's accuracy drops from <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.output_accuracy_no_defense_pct_str]</code> without defenses to <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.output_accuracy_with_defense_pct_str]</code> with defenses, an <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.fidelity_drop_pp_str]</code> reduction, while legitimate top-5 accuracy remains <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.top5_accuracy_pct_str]</code>, close to the <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.baseline_top5_accuracy_pct_str]</code> baseline. Monitoring and output shaping add <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.total_defense_ms_str]</code> on top of <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.base_inference_ms_str]</code> inference, or <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.overhead_pct_str]</code> overhead, while flagging <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.false_positive_pct_str]</code> of users for manual review.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Tier caps, monitoring, and output shaping slow an extractor; only the cumulative budget makes the full extraction set cost as much as retraining. Break-even is the design target rather than a weakness. At that price the query bill alone equals the cost of training an equivalent model. The extractor still pays for the surrogate's own training run and engineering on top of it while receiving a copy that the shaped outputs leave less faithful. Under the assumed equal-quality comparison, a lifetime price at or above break-even therefore removes the cost advantage of extraction over training an equivalent model independently. The residual route is to spread queries across many accounts, which account issuance controls and cross-account detection must bound, because a per-principal budget constrains an adversary only as far as principals map to real actors. The accuracy, latency, and false-positive figures are assumptions that the operator must measure before the design ships.</p>\n<p>Pay-as-you-go pricing alone would charge the full training cost, but an extractor can stay inside the flat-fee tier, and tracking the cumulative bill against the queries collected shows what that choice is worth (@fig-extraction-cost-regimes). A daily quota stretches collection over <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.paid_tier_days_to_extract_str]</code> but leaves the total near <code>[DefenseOverheadAnalysis.paid_tier_extraction_cost_usd_str]</code>. A lifetime budget that bills every query beyond an allowance of <code>[ExtractionBudgetRegimes.lifetime_allowance_str]</code> queries at the pay-as-you-go price instead makes the bill climb with the extraction set itself, reaching about <code>[ExtractionBudgetRegimes.full_set_lifetime_usd_str]</code> when the set is complete, close to the cost of training the model.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-16\" class=\"cs-h3\">Leakage through authorized outputs</h3>\n<p>Whereas model theft copies the model, a second confidentiality failure needs no copy at all, because a model that executes correctly can disclose its training data through the outputs it is authorized to return. Persistent learning (@sec-security-privacy-security-privacy-ml-systems-0b1e) carries sensitive information past the original processing, and the attacker reads nothing but permitted predictions, so storage encryption and caller authentication offer no protection. Three attacks exploit that leakage at increasing levels of detail. Membership inference learns a single bit indicating whether a given record was in the training set. Model inversion reconstructs properties of the training data, such as a representative input for a class or an identity. Training-data extraction recovers memorized records verbatim. All three need only query access or released weights, and all three grow more effective as the model fits its training data more closely than the population that data came from.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h4\">Membership inference</h4>\n<p><strong>Membership inference</strong> tests whether a particular record was part of a model's training set [@shokri2017membership]. The attack exploits a gap that training creates. The model has fitted its training members, so it usually assigns them lower loss and higher confidence than records it has never seen. The simplest test thresholds that signal and declares a record a member when the model's confidence on its true label exceeds a calibrated cutoff. A stronger test trains <em>shadow models</em> on data drawn from the same distribution with known membership. It learns from their outputs how member and nonmember responses differ, and it applies that classifier to the target model's outputs [@shokri2017membership]. Both tests succeed in proportion to the gap between training and held-out behavior, so the risk grows with overfitting and tends to concentrate on small datasets and on rare records that the model had to fit individually. A control added for another threat can widen that gap, so each one is rechecked against membership inference.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Robustness-privacy trade-off</strong>: Adversarial training can increase susceptibility to membership inference in the settings studied by @song2019privacy. The result means that a robustness intervention must be reevaluated against privacy invariants rather than assumed to improve every security property at once.</p>\n<p>For threat modeling, the relevant variable is what the adversary observes: confidence scores, labels only, or individual client updates. Attack success depends on the target model, data, outputs, and adversary knowledge. Confirming that an individual's data was in a training set can still create privacy and compliance risk. In healthcare or legal prediction, membership itself can disclose sensitive information about a person, such as a diagnosis or a case. Whether that disclosure violates GDPR, HIPAA, or another rule depends on the data, parties, and deployment context [@gdpr2016; @hhs2003hipaaprivacy].</p>\n<p>Membership inference is also the standard privacy audit, and the audit and differential privacy bound the same leakage from opposite sides. An audit runs the strongest available attack against the trained model and reports its success. It must state which attacker access it assumes, because some threat models permit only model queries while others grant parameters or auxiliary information. A successful attack proves leakage. A failed attack shows only that this attack, under these assumptions, found none, so an empirical audit is a lower bound on what the model reveals.</p>\n<p>Differential privacy (@sec-security-privacy-differential-privacy-8c2b) supplies the upper bound. A membership test is itself a computation on the released model, so the guarantee applies to its verdict directly and limits how much more often any test can flag a protected record when that record was in training than when it was absent. Under a guarantee with budget $\\PrivacyBudget$ and no additive slack, the rate at which a test flags true members can exceed the rate at which it falsely flags nonmembers by at most a factor of $e^{\\PrivacyBudget}$, which places a ceiling over every possible attack. The distance between the measured lower bound and the accounted upper bound is the uncertainty that a privacy claim carries, the band between the audit curve and the ceiling in @fig-mia-audit-dp-bound.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h4\">Model inversion</h4>\n<p>Beyond single-bit membership, model inversion infers sensitive properties of the training data, or a representative input for a class or identity, from the learned model [@fredrikson2015]. It can use either query access or exposed model internals. Confidence scores, for example, can guide a search for inputs that the model associates with a target identity or attribute. A reconstructed face may reveal recognizable features without reproducing an exact training photograph; the privacy claim must distinguish those outcomes. Gradient inversion applies the same idea to the updates that a federated client sends (@sec-security-privacy-gradient-inversion-risks-d2c1).</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h4\">Memorization and training-data extraction</h4>\n<p><strong>Memorization</strong> is the strongest form of the leak, in which a model retains enough of a specific training record to reproduce it. It is not confined to language models. Under one published extraction protocol, a diffusion model reproduced a training-set photograph almost exactly [@carlini2023diffusion]; @fig-diffusion-model-example pairs the original with the reconstruction. Evidence of extraction concerns that specific training instance; a similar-looking output of the same subject would not establish memorization. The exposure matters most for assistants trained or adapted on user logs, where the memorized strings are the users' own messages.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h4\">GPT-2 data extraction case study</h4>\n<p>The GPT-2 extraction case shows why storage access control is insufficient once a model has learned sensitive content. In 2020, researchers demonstrated that query access to OpenAI's GPT-2 model could reveal verbatim training examples [@carlini2021extracting]. The attack targeted information exposed through generation rather than direct access to the stored training dataset. The researchers generated hundreds of thousands of samples, some unconditioned and some seeded with short snippets of internet text. They then ranked the samples by how much more likely GPT-2 found them than a reference model or compression baseline did. This ranking allowed them to extract personal information such as names, phone numbers, and email addresses, along with source code, unique identifiers, and other verbatim snippets that the model had memorized from its training data.</p>\n<p>The technical approach exploited GPT-2's memorization of rare or repeated text sequences through three stages. In the sampling stage, the researchers generated 600,000 samples using top-$\\Glyph{k}$ sampling, sampling with a temperature that decays over the first generated tokens, and sampling conditioned on short prefixes drawn from internet text. Ranking then applied membership-inference metrics that compare GPT-2's perplexity on a sample (a measure of how unlikely the model finds the text) against a reference, namely the sample's zlib compression entropy, the perplexity a smaller GPT-2 model assigns it, and GPT-2's perplexity on the lowercased text. A sample that GPT-2 finds far more likely than the reference suggests memorization rather than fluent generalization. Manual verification then closed the pipeline as the researchers inspected 1,800 top-ranked candidates, checking them first by internet search and then against the training data with OpenAI's cooperation to confirm 604 of them as unique memorized training examples.</p>\n<p>The attack challenged the assumption that training data would be \"forgotten\" after optimization. Some training examples remained recoverable even though the model was designed to generate text rather than retrieve records. Across the GPT-2 family studied (124M to 1.5B parameters), larger models exposed more memorized training data under the evaluation. This comparison motivates testing memorization as models scale; it does not establish a universal relationship between parameter count and privacy loss across different datasets and training procedures. For Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3), the deployment must therefore assess its actual corpus, training procedure, and exposed outputs rather than infer leakage from parameter count alone.</p>\n<p>Filtering, deduplication, and manual review can reduce exposure, and output filters can screen known sensitive patterns, but empirical screening does not prove that no sensitive string remains recoverable. Differential privacy addresses a different requirement by bounding how much a protected contribution changes the distribution of released results. It does not prohibit every reproduction of text that appears in training, especially information shared across many records. Its contribution bound and the utility effects of filtering or private training must therefore be evaluated separately.</p>\n<p>The result demonstrates why training-data controls need layered mitigations rather than storage access control alone [@carlini2021extracting]. Those layers can include filtering of personally identifiable information (PII), deduplication, extraction-style memorization audits, membership-inference testing, and differential privacy when a formal bound is required. Machine unlearning and policy controls address related lifecycle questions, but their guarantees require separate evidence and validation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-17\" class=\"cs-h3\">Data poisoning</h3>\n<p>While model theft and output leakage compromise confidentiality through read access to a finished model, data poisoning compromises training integrity through write access to something the model will learn from, arriving earlier in the lifecycle than any serving control can act. Injecting a few hundred mislabeled or trigger-stamped examples into a web-scraped corpus or user-feedback stream can plant a targeted backdoor while overall validation accuracy looks completely normal [@biggio2012poisoning].</p>\n<p><em><strong>Data poisoning</strong></em> is the corruption of training data to compromise model behavior at inference time, either by injecting malicious samples or modifying existing labels.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: It undermines the foundational assumption of data integrity. Even a small fraction of poisoned samples can create backdoors or systematic biases that remain latent until triggered by specific inputs during serving.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike inference-time evasion attacks against a fixed model, data poisoning operates through data collection or training, contaminating the model's learned mapping from the source.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that poisoning can be \"fixed\" by more data. In reality, poisoning often exploits the aggregation property of training: adding more clean data may not \"wash out\" a carefully targeted backdoor that uses a unique trigger.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Poisoned data may cause a model to make incorrect predictions, degrade its generalization ability, or embed failure modes that remain dormant until triggered after deployment. The exposure is greatest in applications that retrain on data collected from external sources, including user interactions, crowdsourced annotations, and online scraping, because an attacker can then contribute training data without any access to the training pipeline itself.</p>\n<p>The access axes of @sec-security-privacy-threat-vector-classification-3042 apply with one change of emphasis. Model visibility still determines how precisely a poison can be crafted. A white-box attacker can optimize examples against the actual architecture and training procedure, while a black-box attacker relies on surrogates and open submission channels. What makes poisoning possible at all, however, is the write capability that the threat-vector scenarios separated from visibility. That capability can sit at collection, preprocessing, labeling, or storage, so the poisoning surface spans the whole data path. Its priority relative to other threats follows the deployment context analyzed in @sec-security-privacy-threat-prioritization-framework-f2d5.</p>\n<p>A poisoning attempt spans admission, learning, and exploitation. The attacker first places manipulated examples where the training pipeline may accept them. Training then determines whether those examples induce the intended behavior; merely inserting a record does not guarantee success. If the altered behavior survives evaluation and deployment, the adversary may exploit it to cause selected errors or degrade service. The attack must therefore be evaluated through the actual learning procedure and release gates.</p>\n<p>Data poisoning can be viewed as a bilevel optimization problem, because the attacker's choice affects a model through the defender's training procedure. In the outer problem, the attacker selects poisoning data $\\PoisoningDataset$ to increase loss on a validation or target dataset $\\PoisoningTargetDataset$, while the inner problem trains the model on the resulting union with the original training data $\\CleanTrainingDataset$. Subject to the attacker's permitted modifications and poisoning budget, an untargeted objective is:</p>\n<p>$$ \\max_{\\PoisoningDataset} \\ \\LossFunction(\\PoisonedModel, \\PoisoningTargetDataset) $$ {#eq-untargeted-poisoning}</p>\n<p>where $\\PoisonedModel$ represents the model trained on the combined dataset of original and poisoned data. For targeted attacks, this objective can be refined to focus on specific inputs $\\ModelInput_t$ and target labels $\\ExampleLabel_t$: $$ \\min_{\\PoisoningDataset} \\ \\LossFunction(\\PoisonedModel, \\ModelInput_t, \\ExampleLabel_t) $$ {#eq-targeted-poisoning}</p>\n<p>In the training system, the nested objectives become two coupled loops (@fig-data-poisoning-loop). The outer loop is the attacker's proposal step, which optimizes the attack objective, either raising loss on the test set (@eq-untargeted-poisoning) or, for a targeted attack, lowering loss on the chosen label $\\ExampleLabel_t$ (@eq-targeted-poisoning). The inner loop is the defender's training process, which minimizes loss on the combined data. The attacker evaluates or approximates this inner effect on training, and uses the resulting target loss to refine the proposal. An attacker without access to the defender's training process must instead rely on a surrogate or other available feedback.</p>\n<p>This formulation captures the adversary's goal of introducing carefully crafted data points to manipulate the model's decision boundaries. Consider an email spam filter that retrains on messages users report as legitimate. An attacker who controls a set of accounts can report a batch of messages written in the style of an upcoming campaign as \"not spam.\" The goal is to shift the filter's decision boundary so that the campaign's future messages reach inboxes. In this case, the poisoning data $\\PoisoningDataset$ consists of mislabeled campaign-style messages, and the attacker's objective is to have the campaign messages $\\ModelInput_t$ classified as legitimate mail $\\ExampleLabel_t$, following the targeted attack formulation in @eq-targeted-poisoning. If the mislabeled messages influence training enough to induce that behavior, aggregate performance on other traffic may still appear normal. The contrast with the stop-sign stickers that opened the discussion of model-specific attacks is the lifecycle stage. Poisoning changes the model before deployment, whereas the stickers change only the input that an unmodified deployed model receives.</p>\n<p>Data poisoning attacks differ in their objective and scope [@biggio2012poisoning]. Availability attacks seek broad degradation of useful performance. Targeted attacks seek errors on selected inputs or classes and may leave aggregate accuracy largely unchanged [@shafahi2018poison]. The targeted objective of @eq-targeted-poisoning rewards errors only on the attacker's chosen inputs, while the inner training loop still fits the combined data, which remain mostly clean, so the objective itself does not push aggregate accuracy down. Backdoor attacks associate an attacker-chosen trigger with unwanted behavior; the trigger can be visible, as in a sticker, even when its significance is hidden [@gu2017badnets]. Subpopulation attacks instead target a group defined by shared features [@jagielski2021subpopulation]. These categories can overlap. Their objectives guide which evaluations are needed beyond aggregate accuracy, including targeted, trigger-specific, and subgroup tests.</p>\n<p>The objective classes say what an attacker wants; the attack <em>mechanism</em> depends on the attacker's access to the system and knowledge of the data pipeline. Every mechanism shares the shape traced in @fig-poisoning-impact, in which a poisoned record enters the training pipeline alongside clean data and attempts to alter the resulting model's behavior. The most direct mechanism is label modification, where an attacker selects a subset of training samples and alters their labels, flipping $\\ExampleLabel= 1$ to $\\ExampleLabel= 0$ or reassigning categories in multi-class settings. Even small-scale label corruption can shift decision boundaries. At web scale the same mechanism mislabels multimodal pretraining pairs, as in the dirty-label sample of @fig-dirty-label-example, which pairs cat images with misleading \"dog\" captions to induce false associations.</p>\n<p>Beyond label modification, the attacker can keep the label intact and corrupt the features instead, the strategy known as <strong>clean-label poisoning</strong>. In the bilevel formulation, only the attacker's permitted modifications change, from labels to features. Because every label is correct, label audits cannot flag these records. Imperceptible image perturbations, subtle shifts in structured fields, and fixed trigger patterns aim for the same outcome. In each case the training example still appears legitimate, but it bends the learned decision boundary toward a future failure. Generative methods and data-synthesis tools raise the same risk at larger scale because they can create natural-looking examples whose only purpose is to distort what the model learns.</p>\n<p>Clean-label poisoning is not always malicious in intent; researchers have begun exploring it as a defensive tool, particularly for protecting creative work from unauthorized use by generative AI models. Nightshade, developed by researchers at the University of Chicago, aims to deter unauthorized training by poisoning images that a scraper may collect [@shan2024]. It does not block scraping itself. Its perturbations preserve the apparent image content while changing how a model learns from it; the reported experiments show targeted concept corruption when the modified images enter the tested training pipelines.</p>\n<p>The dose-response matrix in @fig-poisoning charts this progressive corruption across four poison doses (0 to 300 samples) and eight prompt concepts. As the dose increases, Stable Diffusion XL outputs morph away from source concepts (generating cows for \"car\" or cats for \"dog\") under the tested training conditions. These outputs demonstrate changed behavior without, by themselves, identifying the internal representation that caused it. The experiment demonstrates concept poisoning, in which poisoned samples distort a model's semantic associations.</p>\n<p>The Nightshade experiments also report effects on concepts related to the poisoned target. This spillover extends the observed attack beyond the exact target prompt, but its reach depends on the model, poison construction, and training procedure. The same technique used to protect artistic content could also be repurposed to sabotage legitimate training pipelines, highlighting the dual-use dilemma at the heart of machine learning security.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Dual-use dilemma</strong>: The structural tension that defensive ML capabilities (adversarial training, data poisoning tools, red-teaming frameworks) are simultaneously offensive capabilities. This creates an arms race where defensive research publications become attack playbooks. For ML systems engineering, the consequence is architectural: robustness mechanisms must assume attacker knowledge of the defense, ruling out security-through-obscurity and motivating formal guarantees where feasible and adaptive empirical evaluation within the remaining threat model.</p>\n<p>Whether crafted examples become poisoning depends on the data boundary. Web scraping, social media feeds, crowdsourced annotation, and untrusted user submissions all allow poisoned records to enter upstream and pass through weak cleaning checks in a \"trusted\" form. Physical systems add a second path. A sticker on a road sign attacks a car at inference time, but photographs of that sign harvested into a training corpus poison the model only when they are crafted or labeled to serve the attacker. Correctly labeled examples can instead help the model resist the sticker. Online learning systems tighten the feedback loop further, allowing an attacker to introduce small increments of malicious data until model behavior drifts without a single obvious anomaly. In collaborative training the same boundary sits at aggregation, where one malicious client's update reaches every participant (@fig-poisoning-attack-example). @Sec-security-privacy-gradient-sync-integrity examines the same exposure among the workers of a synchronous data-parallel job.</p>\n<p>Mitigating poisoning requires controls from data admission through model evaluation, and @sec-robust-ai-data-poisoning-defenses-d070 develops those defenses and the evidence for them. Because targeted and backdoor attacks can preserve aggregate validation accuracy, clean evaluation cannot certify integrity. The provenance bound on trust (principle \\ref{pri-provenance-bounds-trust}) makes source verification and lineage necessary evidence about what entered training, but a verified source can still supply harmful records. Validation and anomaly detection examine those records, while robust training can limit their influence under a specified contamination model. Release evaluation must then test the targeted behaviors that ordinary accuracy metrics can miss.</p>\n<p>↪ Poisoning defenses, from data sanitization to robust training, are developed in @sec-robust-ai-data-poisoning-defenses-d070.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-18\" class=\"cs-h3\">Adversarial attacks</h3>\n<p>An attacker who can modify a sensor input or submit an API request can target the deployed model without changing its training data. The security question is which inputs the actor can control and which service decisions those inputs can influence. Behavioral robustness asks how much the model's predictions degrade under that specified manipulation. Here the threat model establishes the allowed attack; @sec-robust-ai-input-attack-detection-defense-19d3 develops the evaluation and training methods needed to measure and improve resistance.</p>\n<p>Adversarial inputs exploit the deployed model's decision behavior under attacker-chosen modifications. Some attacks use small, hard-to-perceive perturbations; others use visible patches or changes allowed by a physical or semantic constraint. The threat model must state those constraints before attack success or defense quality can be measured. This shifts attention from the integrity of upstream training data to the inputs and decisions exposed during inference.</p>\n<p>These attacks create real-world risks in domains such as autonomous driving, biometric authentication, and content moderation. Small, visually hard-to-distinguish perturbations can cause image classifiers to make high-confidence errors [@szegedy2013intriguing; @goodfellow2015explaining]. Physical-world work showed that printed adversarial images can survive camera capture and remain misclassified by an ImageNet classifier [@kurakin2018]. Road-sign attacks then showed that small stickers or perturbations can cause signs to be misclassified under realistic viewing conditions [@eykholt2018]. A deployed defense must be evaluated against attacks adapted to its mechanisms. Changing the attacker model or the deployment can invalidate earlier evidence, so each change requires renewed testing.</p>\n<p>The Perspective API case makes the inference-time distinction concrete. Researchers studying Google's toxicity detection API in 2017 showed that subtle misspellings, character substitutions, and added punctuation could cause explicitly toxic phrases to receive low toxicity scores [@hosseini2017deceiving]. The training pipeline had not been poisoned; the deployed decision surface was fragile to small input perturbations.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Perspective API</strong>: Google's toxicity model has been reported in use across many platforms, including <em>The New York Times</em> and Wikipedia. A shared classifier can create correlated exposure to an evasion technique, but success at each site also depends on its model version, preprocessing, thresholds, and additional moderation controls.</p>\n<p>The shared abstraction is a search over permitted input changes for an outcome the attacker wants. A norm bound constrains numerical distance, while a physical constraint may limit sticker placement or camera conditions. Neither automatically establishes that a modification is imperceptible or harmless to a human observer.</p>\n<p>The access types of @sec-security-privacy-threat-vector-classification-3042 determine how that search proceeds. White-box access lets the attacker compute input gradients directly, while black-box and gray-box attackers must estimate them from queries, partial architecture knowledge, or a surrogate model. Common attack strategies include surrogate model construction, transfer attacks exploiting adversarial transferability [@papernot2016transferability], and generative adversarial network (GAN)-based perturbation generation. The evaluation must also state the query budget and which inputs the actor can modify.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Adversarial transferability</strong>: Adversarial inputs crafted on one model can also fool another [@szegedy2013intriguing; @papernot2016transferability]. A surrogate can therefore support an attack without target-model parameters, but transfer success depends on the models, data, and allowed perturbations; it is not uniformly comparable to white-box access.</p>\n<p>↪ Gradient-based perturbation attacks and their white-box, black-box, and transfer variants are developed in @sec-robust-ai-inputlevel-attacks-model-robustness-d6ea.</p>\n<p>The threat model usually bounds the attacker's change to a ball around the clean input, $\\|\\AdversarialPerturbation\\| \\leq \\AdversarialPerturbationBudget$. A perturbation inside that radius succeeds when it carries the input across a decision boundary. The radius $\\AdversarialPerturbationBudget$ lives in input space and is unrelated to the privacy budget $\\PrivacyBudget$ of @sec-security-privacy-differential-privacy-8c2b. The stop-sign stickers that opened the discussion of model-specific attacks are the physical form of such a boundary crossing. @Sec-robust-ai-physical-world-attacks treats the case as a robustness problem and examines what its separate laboratory and field-test success rates imply for evaluation.</p>\n<p>Because these attacks succeed against models with high clean accuracy, defenses must act on the attack itself. They divide between runtime controls, such as input validation and anomaly monitoring during inference (@sec-security-privacy-runtime-system-monitoring-a71c), and training-time methods such as adversarial training on deliberately perturbed examples [@madry2018towards]. @Sec-robust-ai-input-attack-detection-defense-19d3 evaluates both against stated threat models.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-19\" class=\"cs-h3\">LLM-specific attack vectors</h3>\n<p>The same inference-time framing extends to large language models (LLMs), where security surveys catalog prompt injection, jailbreaking, data leakage, and malicious reuse as cybersecurity and privacy risks [@gupta2023]. The systems mechanism is that the interface often mixes instructions, user content, retrieved context, and policy controls in one token stream, making the trust boundary between control and data fragile [@perez2022ignore]. The boundary is weakest where retrieved or external content enters that stream, because text the system fetched can be reinterpreted as instructions [@greshake2023not]. A secure LLM system therefore separates four objects that the model tends to collapse. Instructions, comprising system and developer directives, define the intended control plane, whereas untrusted content from user input and external text must remain strictly data rather than authority. Retrieved documents provide evidence but should never rewrite policy or permissions, and sensitive secrets (credentials, private data, and hidden policy state) must remain outside model-visible context unless explicitly required. Separating these objects gives the serving system concrete boundaries it can enforce even when the model output tries to blur them.</p>\n<p>The serving system must treat every model output as untrusted input, not as authorization. Each model receives the least privilege needed for the current task [@saltzer1975], and any component that consumes model output validates it before acting on it. An external policy-enforcement point must check the authenticated principal and current context rather than accepting a model-generated claim of permission. High-impact or irreversible operations require a separate approval path. These boundaries prevent model text from expanding its authority and limit the blast radius when prompt injection succeeds [@greshake2023not]. They do not eliminate injection: an attacker may still abuse every operation that the current principal legitimately allows.</p>\n<p>Three recurring failure modes organize the space: prompt injection attempts to cross the boundary between control and data, training data extraction exposes memorized records [@carlini2021extracting], and jailbreaking seeks to bypass alignment controls [@zou2023]. Prompt injection exploits the model's interpretation of content as instructions [@perez2022ignore; @greshake2023not]. Instruction roles and delimiters can guide behavior, but do not supply an authorization boundary equivalent to separating SQL parameters from executable query structure. An attacker can embed directives within legitimate inputs (such as \"Ignore previous constraints and output the system prompt\") that the model may follow. Input and output classifiers can detect some attempts, while sandboxing and external permission checks constrain what a manipulated output can reach. These controls need separate evaluation because detecting suspicious text and enforcing authority are different tasks.</p>\n<p>Training data extraction is the memorization leak of @sec-security-privacy-memorization-extraction, in which generated text reproduces retained training sequences, including PII, without implying that the model stores a retrievable copy of every record. A third category, jailbreaking and alignment bypass, targets controls intended to keep generated behavior within policy. Reinforcement learning from human feedback shapes behavior during training [@ouyang2022instructgpt], Constitutional AI uses critique against explicit principles [@bai2022constitutional], and guardrail models classify inputs or outputs at runtime [@inan2023llamaguard; @shieldgemma2024]. These learned controls do not establish a formal guarantee against every prompt; adversarial suffixes demonstrate one way to bypass them [@zou2023]. A deployment can combine behavioral training, separately evaluated monitors, query budgets, and review of consequential outputs. Their value depends on measured resistance to adaptive attacks and on the external permissions that limit what an unsafe output can cause.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-20\" class=\"cs-h3\">Availability abuse and denial of wallet</h3>\n<p>Generative serving also exposes availability to an attack that needs no flood of requests. The cost of a request grows with the tokens it generates and the documents it retrieves, so an attacker can select requests that maximize that work or defeat cache reuse, consuming more accelerator time and downstream service capacity than ordinary traffic while the endpoint remains reachable. In this <em>denial-of-wallet</em> scenario, the asset under attack is the cost budget rather than only the request queue: autoscaling (@sec-inference-scale-autoscaling-capacity-control) can preserve apparent availability by purchasing more capacity while spend and tail latency exceed their operating limits.</p>\n<p>Availability defenses must therefore meter work, not merely request counts. The mechanisms are the overload controls that already protect a serving fleet; what security changes is the signal they act on, which becomes work and cost per authenticated principal rather than aggregate load. The serving boundary should assign budgets to input size, generated tokens, retrieval calls, concurrent work, and cumulative cost per authenticated principal. Admission control can reject or degrade requests before they reserve scarce accelerators; bounded generation, timeouts, circuit breakers, and per-request retrieval quotas limit the work admitted by any one request. Operators should monitor cost per successful result alongside latency and error rate because an attacker can preserve HTTP success while exhausting the service's economic budget. These controls also constrain model extraction, but their availability objective is distinct. They keep adversarially induced work from displacing legitimate service or producing unbounded spend.</p>\n<p>↩ Circuit breakers and backpressure, the serving fleet's overload controls, are established in @sec-inference-scale-circuit-breakers-backpressure-e61c.</p>\n<p>While LLM control-data entanglement cuts across multiple objectives inside a single token stream, the five primary lifecycle objectives span distinct stages of the ML pipeline and demand targeted defensive strategies. Grouping those five objectives by lifecycle stage and attack vector (@tbl-threats-models-summary) shows where each vulnerability appears and which mitigation it calls for.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Threat type</strong></th><th><strong>Lifecycle stage</strong></th><th><strong>Attack vector</strong></th><th><strong>Example impact</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Model Theft</strong></td><td>Deployment</td><td>API access, insider leaks</td><td>Stolen IP, behavioral clone</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Training-Data Leakage</strong></td><td>Inference</td><td>Authorized queries</td><td>Memorized PII, membership disclosure</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Data Poisoning</strong></td><td>Training</td><td>Label flipping, backdoors</td><td>Targeted misclassification, degraded accuracy</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Adversarial Attacks</strong></td><td>Inference</td><td>Input perturbation</td><td>Real-time misclassification, safety failure</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Availability Abuse</strong></td><td>Inference</td><td>Cost-maximizing requests</td><td>Exhausted serving budget, tail-latency breach</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Threat Landscape</strong>: Machine learning systems face diverse threats throughout their lifecycle, ranging from data manipulation during training to model theft postdeployment. The table categorizes these threats by lifecycle stage and attack vector, clarifying how vulnerabilities manifest and enabling targeted mitigation strategies. {#tbl-threats-models-summary}</p>\n<p>The appropriate defense depends on the attack vector, lifecycle stage, and available evidence. For each threat, assessment evidence and candidate controls play different roles (@fig-threat-mitigation-flow). Monitoring can reveal suspicious querying, while watermarking can support attribution after extraction; neither establishes that theft was prevented. Privacy audits measure attack success under tested conditions, whereas an accounted DP mechanism provides a formal bound under its stated assumptions. Each row identifies a design starting point, not a guaranteed detection-to-defense pipeline.</p>\n<p>An autonomous vehicle's vision system misclassifies a stop sign as a speed limit sign because of a specially crafted sticker placed on the sign. This attack happens at inference time without access to the training data.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Attack timing</strong>: distinguish this inference-time failure from a training-time compromise.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Input mechanism</strong>: explain how the attacker changed the model's observed input without changing the model itself.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Defense choice</strong>: select the detection or robustness control that belongs closest to the serving path.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Model theft, output leakage, poisoning, and adversarial inputs all reach the model through an interface the fleet exposes on purpose: a query endpoint, a data feed, or a sensor. A fleet also has interfaces it never exposes to users, between its own workers, tenants, and nodes, and those interfaces carry the next set of threats.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-21\" class=\"cs-h2\">Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries</h2>\n<p>A training job on the reference cluster of <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.total_gpus_str]</code> GPUs (<code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.node_count_str]</code> nodes with <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.gpus_per_node_str]</code> GPUs each) depends on contributions from many participants. Each optimizer step of the Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3) reference model reduces <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.grad_total_gb_str]</code> of FP16 gradients across the job, and each checkpoint interval produces a <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.ckpt_total_tb_str]</code> artifact containing valuable learned state. Public inference controls do not govern these internal exchanges. Four boundaries determine how a node compromise can propagate: the gradient each worker contributes, the fabric that carries it, the accelerators and caches tenants share, and the evidence used to admit nodes into the job.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-22\" class=\"cs-h3\">Gradient synchronization integrity</h3>\n<p>Because standard ring AllReduce averages worker contributions, a single compromised node submitting an arbitrarily large gradient vector can corrupt the global step for all <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.total_gpus_str]</code> GPUs. The <strong>breakdown point</strong> of an aggregation rule measures this weakness as the smallest fraction of corrupted inputs that can drive the aggregate arbitrarily far from the honest value. Weighting each of the $\\DataParallelDegree$ contributions (one per model replica) by $1/\\DataParallelDegree$ does not bound the corruption when the contribution's magnitude is unconstrained, so one input out of $\\DataParallelDegree$ suffices, and the mean's breakdown point falls to zero as the job grows. @Sec-fault-tolerance-reliability-reliability-byzantine-failures-8a24 introduces aggregation rules such as Krum and coordinate-wise trimmed mean, whose guarantees require assumptions about the number of faulty workers and the honest updates [@blanchard2017machine; @yin2018byzantine]. Under those assumptions they keep a positive breakdown point, so a bounded number of corrupted workers cannot drive the step arbitrarily far. An intentional adversary can adapt its update to the deployed norm or distance checks and repeat the attempt across steps. Evaluation must therefore test attacks against the active control rather than rely only on evidence from random faults.</p>\n<p>Robust aggregation also changes the collective's information requirements. Ring AllReduce computes a sum with about $2(\\DataParallelDegree-1)/\\DataParallelDegree$ times the gradient size $\\CollectivePayloadSize$ in per-worker communication as $\\DataParallelDegree$ grows; its result does not provide a separate gradient for every worker. A direct implementation of a median, trimmed mean, or distance-based rule can instead gather all individual gradients. Gathering them at every worker receives $(\\DataParallelDegree-1)\\CollectivePayloadSize$ bytes per worker, a factor of $\\DataParallelDegree/2$ relative to the ring traffic expression. Unlike the device count $\\DeviceCount$ of the Fleet Law, the data-parallel degree $\\DataParallelDegree$ counts replicas rather than accelerators. The comparison assumes a data-parallel degree of <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.dp_degree_str]</code> on the reference cluster, which leaves each replica two full nodes of <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.gpus_per_node_str]</code> GPUs (for example, tensor parallelism inside each node). At that degree $\\DataParallelDegree/2$ gives <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.robust_traffic_ratio_mult_str]</code> the ring traffic. Because the gradient synchronization is bandwidth-dominated, its share of $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$ grows by roughly that factor unless the extra traffic can be overlapped with backward computation. That ratio is the cost of the gather-based implementation, not a lower bound for every robust protocol.</p>\n<p>Worker admission through attestation (@sec-security-privacy-fleet-attestation) restricts which environments join the job, but does not prove their gradients are benign; update checks or redundant computation address different evidence. Federated deployments face heterogeneous attestation support and independently controlled clients. @Sec-edge-intelligence-federated-privacy-a1ed examines the additional conflict between inspecting individual updates and hiding them through secure aggregation.</p>\n<p>↩ Ring AllReduce and its $2(\\DataParallelDegree-1)/\\DataParallelDegree$ per-worker traffic bound are established in @sec-communication-collective-operations-collective-operations-ring-allreduce-ffce.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-23\" class=\"cs-h3\">Collective traffic confidentiality</h3>\n<p>Even when every admitted worker submits an honest update, the fabric that carries those updates introduces a second internal boundary around confidentiality. Moving <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.grad_total_gb_str]</code> of plaintext FP16 gradients per step over remote direct memory access (RDMA) bypasses host CPUs for speed, but exposes sensitive training updates to any adversary who can tap the fabric links, switches, or management plane. Gradient inversion, examined for federated clients in @sec-security-privacy-gradient-inversion-risks-d2c1, demonstrates that exposed gradients can reconstruct training examples under particular observation and training conditions. The exposure of a gradient shard, aggregate, or activation therefore needs its own analysis of what an observer on the fabric can recover. A dedicated facility can restrict physical access to links and switches without eliminating the fabric trust boundary.</p>\n<p>↩ RDMA and GPUDirect transports, which move payloads directly between accelerator memories, are established in @sec-network-fabrics-rdma.</p>\n<p>Encrypting collective traffic can add to the communication term of the Fleet Law (@eq-fleet-law). The size of that addition follows from the ring traffic just derived. For the large gradient buckets that dominate a step, the bandwidth term of the AllReduce, about $2(\\DataParallelDegree-1)\\CollectivePayloadSize/(\\DataParallelDegree\\LinkBandwidth)$ for link bandwidth $\\LinkBandwidth$, outweighs the per-message latency. A cipher that sustains only half the link rate therefore halves the effective $\\LinkBandwidth$ and roughly doubles that part of $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$. A cipher that keeps pace with the link adds latency but little transfer time, while a host-based path that stages accelerator data adds copies that GPUDirect RDMA avoids. Adapter or link-layer encryption can protect traffic without that staging path, subject to the endpoints and keys included in its trust boundary. The added latency and any lost overlap must be measured on the actual collective schedule, because an increase in $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$ recurs at every step and can raise the accelerator time the run requires.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-24\" class=\"cs-h3\">Shared accelerators and caches</h3>\n<p>Securing the network links between nodes still leaves the third internal boundary exposed whenever multiple workloads share the accelerators and memory at the ends of those links. Sharing a GPU across tenants without hardware memory partitioning or prefix-cache scoping lets one tenant infer another's prompt prefix from response-timing hits or read residual high-bandwidth memory (HBM) state. When a serving engine shares its key-value prefix cache across tenants, a request whose opening tokens match a cached prefix skips prefill computation and returns its first token noticeably faster, letting a co-tenant probe whether a target system prompt or document was recently processed. At the processor layer, Volta-era and later implementations of Multi-Process Service, which lets several processes run kernels on one GPU at once, provide separate client GPU address spaces, but processes still share compute units, caches, and memory controllers without hardware fault isolation, so address separation alone does not establish a boundary between mutually distrustful users. Multi-Instance GPU (MIG) partitions memory, cache, and compute resources in hardware [@nvidiaMigUserGuide], while sequential reuse introduces a separate requirement to scrub accessible residual memory before reassignment. Tenant-scoped prefix caches remove cross-tenant timing leakage at a potential hit-rate cost, leaving other timing channels and within-tenant access boundaries to be assessed.</p>\n<p>↩ The accelerator sharing options and the isolation strength each provides are established in @sec-fleet-orchestration-resource-isolation.</p>\n<p>Each of these controls spends capacity, through reserved partitions, fragmentation, scrubbing time, or lost cache reuse. Measuring that capacity effect requires a matched workload and resource budget; a smaller partition cannot be compared with a whole accelerator and labeled pure security overhead.</p>\n<p>Consider a hypothetical comparison of two serving configurations with the same allocated accelerator capacity, models, request mix, and latency target. Assume the baseline sustains <code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_peak_tokens_str]</code> per second and the configuration with additional security controls sustains <code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_secure_tokens_str]</code> per second. These are scenario inputs for a capacity calculation, not measured overheads for MIG or another isolation mechanism.</p>\n<p>For this matched comparison, the throughput loss is <code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_peak_tokens_str]</code> - <code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_secure_tokens_str]</code> = <code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_throughput_loss_str]</code> per second, or (<code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_throughput_loss_str]</code>/<code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_peak_tokens_str]</code>) = <code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_overhead_pct_str]</code>. Spatial partitioning does not inherently require tenant context switches. Its capacity cost may instead arise from fragmentation or idle reserved resources, which should be recorded separately from execution overhead.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: The assumed controls reduce useful throughput by <code>[MultiTenantIsolation.mt_overhead_pct_str]</code> under the stated comparison. In a measured deployment that loss would split among the mechanisms of this section. Hardware partitions strand capacity whenever a tenant's load falls below its slice, memory scrubbing between tenants takes time out of every reassignment, and tenant-scoped prefix caches give up the hits that cross-tenant reuse would have supplied. Each source has a different remedy. Right-sizing partitions recovers stranded capacity, while cache scoping is the price of closing a timing channel and cannot be tuned away without reopening it. Fleet planning must therefore record each source separately; none can be inferred from comparing a whole accelerator with a smaller partition.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-25\" class=\"cs-h3\">Attestation and key custody at fleet scale</h3>\n<p>When <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.node_count_str]</code> cluster nodes restart and update independently, releasing the decryption key for a <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.ckpt_total_tb_str]</code> checkpoint to a node whose boot firmware was silently modified exposes the entire model. Remote attestation lets a verifier appraise authenticated hardware claims about each node against an admission policy before delivering keys or data. The central claim is a set of measurements, cryptographic hashes of the firmware and software the node loaded, recorded and signed by the node's hardware so that compromised software cannot rewrite them. Before releasing the key for the <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.ckpt_total_tb_str]</code> checkpoint, the service can require fresh evidence covering the relevant firmware and workload measurements. Coverage depends on the platform; a valid measurement does not prove that approved code is free of vulnerabilities or that every runtime update is correct. The provenance bound on trust (principle \\ref{pri-provenance-bounds-trust}) links this evidence to the release path. Confidential computing adds runtime protection for code and data placed inside a hardware-enforced boundary, under the platform's stated adversary model. @Sec-security-privacy-hardware-security-foundations-f5e8 develops those mechanisms and their limits.</p>\n<p>The recovery sequence in @fig-attestation-key-release places key delivery between fresh appraisal and checkpoint restoration.</p>\n<p>Attestation and key management add coordination work when admission or recovery requires renewed evidence and key delivery. The policy must specify when evidence expires and which changes require another check. Key rotation also needs a transition plan, while revocation after a suspected compromise may require interrupting work that no longer has valid authority. Hardware security modules can protect the keys used to authorize or wrap secrets (wrapping encrypts one key under another), with node-level custody governed separately. When these operations delay participants or restart, their cost enters $\\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount)$ and the recovery path. A node that restarts after a failure must present fresh evidence and receive the checkpoint key again before it rejoins, so every failure costs that re-admission delay on top of the restart time and lost work that the checkpoint-interval calculation already charges.</p>\n<p>↩ The Young-Daly checkpoint interval, which balances checkpoint write cost against expected lost work, is established in @sec-fault-tolerance-young-daly.</p>\n<p>The four boundaries draw on all three C³ axes. Redundant computation and memory scrubbing add compute work, while tenant-scoped caches can reduce reuse. Robust aggregation and encrypted collectives can add communication cost; attestation and key rotation add coordination. These controls also depend on components beneath the job. A driver with access to plaintext state, a compromised memory path, or an observable power signal can undermine a protection enforced higher in the stack. The hardware threat model must identify which of those components remain trusted and which observations the attacker can obtain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-26\" class=\"cs-h2\">Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities</h2>\n<p>When a serving team moves model weights into production, it often treats encryption, access control, and API policy as the security boundary. Hardware attacks break that assumption. A processor can leak across isolation domains, a field device can be opened, a bus or power rail can expose signals, and a counterfeit component can enter the system before deployment begins. Hardware therefore belongs inside the threat model, as the substrate that can invalidate software controls after those controls appear to pass.</p>\n<p>The practical deployment question is which substrate layer can still violate trust. A cloud LLM endpoint is mostly an isolation problem, because one tenant's process, memory, and accelerator context must not reveal another tenant's prompts or weights. An embedded vision sensor is mostly a physical-access problem, because the attacker may probe power, attach to a debug port, or replace a module. A regulated procurement pipeline is mostly a provenance problem, because the organization must know which components were built, handled, patched, and retired. These cases use different defenses, but they share the same reasoning pattern.</p>\n<p>Hardware security planning therefore begins with four fundamental checks. Processor isolation must separate tenants, cryptographic keys, model weights, and intermediate activations from untrusted code and co-tenants. Physical-access controls must protect devices, sensors, memory, and debug interfaces when attackers can physically touch the hardware. Side-channel evaluation tests whether observable power, timing, electromagnetic, bus, or thermal signals expose sensitive computation despite those boundaries. Finally, supply-chain validation checks evidence that the deployed component matches the component the system was designed and certified to use. Each check needs an explicit attacker model; passing it does not establish protection against access or measurements outside that model.</p>\n<p>Grouping the hardware threat landscape by these four checks rather than by attack family ties each vulnerability to the deployment decision that controls it (@tbl-threat_types). The defensive strategies in @sec-security-privacy-comprehensive-defense-architectures-48ab return to the same decisions from the perspective of trusted execution, attestation, and hardware roots of trust.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Deployment decision</strong></th><th><strong>Trust failure to analyze</strong></th><th><strong>Threat families it organizes</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Processor isolation</strong></td><td>Cross-tenant or cross-process leakage after software access control appears to pass.</td><td>Hardware bugs, speculative execution flaws.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Physical access</strong></td><td>Direct manipulation of sensors, memory, firmware, or debug interfaces.</td><td>Physical attacks, fault injection, leaky ports.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Side-channel observability</strong></td><td>Observable power, timing, electromagnetic, bus, or thermal signals reveal state.</td><td>Side-channel attacks, leaky interconnects.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Provenance and lifecycle trust</strong></td><td>A component is counterfeit, trojaned, mishandled, or unsupported after deployment.</td><td>Counterfeit hardware, supply chain risks.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Hardware Threat Landscape</strong>: Hardware security decisions group diverse vulnerability families into isolation, physical-access, side-channel, and provenance questions that determine whether software protections remain meaningful in deployment. {#tbl-threat_types}</p>\n<p>Each threat family tests one of those decisions. Hardware bugs test processor isolation. Physical attacks, fault injection, and leaky interfaces test whether direct access can change the system after software controls pass. Side-channel attacks test whether computation leaks through observable signals. Supply chain risks, from a single counterfeit part to a compromised part number, test whether the deployed component is the one the architecture assumes. Because a fleet deploys many identical copies of a few parts, a weakness that fails one of these checks on one machine can fail it on every copy at once.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-27\" class=\"cs-h3\">Hardware bugs</h3>\n<p>When tenants share a cloud host, a transient-execution flaw such as Spectre or Meltdown in the CPU can leak decrypted keys, prompts, or feature batches across process boundaries after software access checks have already passed [@lipp2018meltdown; @kocher2019]. Hardware bugs therefore test the first deployment decision: whether processor isolation can be trusted as an empirical boundary rather than a default guarantee. Attackers can exploit these microarchitectural design flaws to bypass memory isolation and read the data of co-tenant applications and the operating system.</p>\n<p>Spectre exploits speculative execution, a performance optimization in which a CPU executes instructions along a predicted path before it knows whether that path is correct. An attacker influences the prediction so that a victim transiently accesses sensitive data. Meltdown instead exploits affected processors that transiently make data available before enforcing the access restriction. In both cases, discarding the unauthorized or mispredicted computation can leave observable traces in microarchitectural state, such as CPU caches. Software-visible execution can therefore respect a boundary while the traces of transient execution leak across it.</p>\n<p>Further research identified related transient-execution attacks, including Foreshadow, ZombieLoad, and RIDL, against different microarchitectural structures in affected processors. Their existence motivates examining each component's isolation assumptions without assuming that every processor shares the same vulnerability. ML systems pair CPUs with GPUs, Tensor Processing Units (TPUs), field-programmable gate arrays (FPGAs), or custom accelerators. A compromise can propagate across this heterogeneous stack when components share sensitive data, keys, or privileged access; the threat model must trace those relationships.</p>\n<p>For a fleet, the consequence can be cross-tenant exposure. Host processors stage data for accelerators, run tokenizers and preprocessing, and hold decrypted keys. When tenants share a vulnerable host and the required attack conditions hold, a transient-execution flaw can expose sensitive state before any accelerator is involved. Mitigations depend on the vulnerability and deployment: restricting simultaneous multithreading, in which two threads share one core's execution resources, or clearing affected microarchitectural state at isolation boundaries can reduce throughput on the data-loading and serving paths that keep accelerators fed. The fleet must remeasure those paths after applying the relevant protections rather than assume a fixed overhead.</p>\n<p>Hardware security therefore extends beyond physical tamper prevention to architectural isolation: preventing processors, accelerators, and memory systems from leaking across boundaries the software assumes are sealed. Mitigations often carry performance costs, especially for data-bound ML workloads, so confidential computing (@sec-security-privacy-fleet-attestation) and trusted execution environments (TEEs) must be evaluated as capacity and latency decisions as well as security decisions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-28\" class=\"cs-h3\">Physical attacks</h3>\n<p>An attacker who can physically touch an edge drone or biometric kiosk can swap a camera module, probe a memory bus, or attach to an unlocked debug port, bypassing every network firewall and API policy. Physical attacks test the second deployment decision: what an adversary can observe or modify by touching the device, sensor, memory, firmware, or debug interface. An exposed debug port may permit memory reads, a replacement sensor may supply false inputs, and a writable firmware interface may change execution. Encryption and authentication remain useful when their keys and enforcement mechanisms stay protected; the threat model must identify which physical paths bypass those protections.</p>\n<p>The failure mode depends on what the attacker can touch. Replacing a drone's navigation module, swapping the sensor of a biometric access system, or misaligning a vehicle's cameras changes system behavior without changing a single model weight. In edge fleets such as the phone-class devices of Archetype C (Federated MobileNet), the coordinator does not control clients' physical environments. Device ownership does not automatically defeat protected storage or execution, but the coordinator cannot infer the integrity of a client's data or updates from ownership or enrollment alone.</p>\n<p>Internal components create the same risk at a lower layer. A hardware trojan inserted during chip fabrication can remain dormant until a specific input or state triggers it, then leak outputs, corrupt computation, or degrade performance in ways that are difficult to diagnose after deployment. Memory chips and accelerator buses can expose model parameters or training data when an attacker has physical access. Data centers are not exempt. An implant installed by someone with sufficient facility access can monitor administrative credentials or data streams and provide a persistent path into training and inference pipelines.</p>\n<p>The defensive implication is that physical-access analysis belongs in the system architecture rather than solely in device packaging. Disabling production debug interfaces closes one access path; signed firmware and secure boot constrain which code can execute. Tamper detection can signal interference, while attestation and supply chain checks provide evidence about measured state and component provenance. These controls address different parts of the trust boundary, so none alone establishes that sensor inputs or runtime computations remain correct after deployment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-29\" class=\"cs-h3\">Fault injection attacks</h3>\n<p>A timed voltage glitch or laser pulse on a microcontroller can skip a single assembly jump instruction in a ReLU layer, zeroing out an activation and forcing a safety-critical misclassification without altering a single stored weight [@breier2018]. Fault injection is the active version of the physical-access decision: the attacker does not replace the model artifact but changes the computation while it runs. A precisely timed voltage drop, power spike, clock glitch, temperature shift, electromagnetic pulse, or laser strike can induce bit flips, skipped instructions, or corrupted memory states [@joye2012; @barenghi2010; @hutter2009; @amiel2006; @skorobogatov2009; @skorobogatov2002]. For ML systems, those faults can force incorrect outputs, bypass security checks, degrade reliability, or expose internal state for later reverse engineering.</p>\n<p>The ML-specific risk is that a small computation fault can become a semantic failure. An embedded classifier running on a microcontroller may misclassify a safety-important input, while a fault in memory or control logic may expose proprietary weights or architecture details. Unlike ordinary random faults, adversarially timed faults are chosen to land on computations the model or security protocol depends on.</p>\n<p>The experiments of @breier2018 targeted activation-function implementations on a microcontroller with timed laser faults. @Fig-injection identifies the vulnerable instruction in the observed ReLU implementation. For a positive accumulator, the copy instructions assign the activation to the output, and the following <code>jmp end</code> normally skips the zeroing block. A laser pulse timed to suppress that jump makes execution fall through into the clear instructions, overwriting the positive output with zero and forcing the neuron inactive. The fault changes executed control flow rather than any stored model weight.</p>\n<p>Fault injection attacks can also be combined with side-channel analysis, where attackers first observe power or timing characteristics to infer model structure or data flow. This reconnaissance helps them time faults against specific layers or operations, such as activation functions or final decision layers. The resulting effect still depends on the implementation, the induced fault, and any checks that detect or contain it.</p>\n<p>Embedded and edge ML systems can be vulnerable when exposed hardware combines with resource constraints that limit runtime defenses. Access to system buses, memory, or power supplies can give attackers opportunities to induce faults, even when software access controls remain intact. The relevant protection comes from the device and execution stack: a lightweight model does not itself establish whether memory has error correction, computations have redundant checks, or packaging resists probing.</p>\n<p>The defense must combine physical hardening with runtime evidence. Tamper-resistant packaging restricts access, while design obfuscation makes targets harder to locate without itself preventing interference. Error-correcting memory and redundant checks can detect or correct some induced errors; secure firmware constrains code changes but does not prevent a fault during execution. Model-output and sensor-consistency monitors can catch some fault-induced behavior that escapes lower layers; MAVFI demonstrates this pattern for micro-aerial-vehicle workloads with anomaly detection and recovery [@hsiao2023]. These protections are hardest in cost- and power-constrained edge systems, where extra cryptographic hardware or redundancy may be unaffordable, so resilience has to be designed across the electrical, firmware, software, and system layers.</p>\n<p>A data center fleet rarely faces a laser, but it faces the same class of failure in two other forms. Without any attacker, marginal processors corrupt computation silently at fleet scale. With an attacker, shared electrical resources can provide a path for interference. Co-tenants on a shared FPGA have induced timing faults remotely by drawing enough current to drop the supply voltage [@gnad2017]. The check-and-verify defenses of @sec-ft-sdc-verification can therefore serve security as well as reliability. Their security coverage requires separate evaluation, however, because an attacker may target both the computation and its check rather than induce the independent errors a reliability analysis assumes.</p>\n<p>↩ Silent data corruption from probabilistic hardware faults at fleet scale is established in @sec-fault-tolerance-reliability-physics-silent-corruption-5d5d.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-30\" class=\"cs-h3\">Side-channel attacks</h3>\n<p>Even when every network port and debug interface is locked down, variations in power draw, electromagnetic emissions, or cache timing during a forward pass can reveal secret keys, layer topologies, or user inputs [@kocher1999; @gandolfi2001]. Side-channel attacks test the third deployment decision: whether computation is observable through physical or shared-resource signals when explicit interfaces are sealed. Measuring and analyzing power, electromagnetic, timing, or acoustic signals lets an attacker infer internal state or recover secret data without exploiting any software or network vulnerability.</p>\n<p>ML accelerators executing inference are exposed to physical leakage as other computing devices are. A matrix multiplication on an edge accelerator can draw current in a pattern correlated with the weight values and input activations being processed. An attacker who can measure that signal may infer properties of the computation, but recovery depends on measurement quality, implementation details, and knowledge or control of inputs. Additional samples help only if the observable signal carries the relevant information. The side-channel surface for a deployed model therefore encompasses both the cryptographic operations protecting its keys and the inference computation itself.</p>\n<p>Studies of Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) implementations showed that an implementation can leak information about a key without an attacker breaking the cipher's mathematical construction. The same distinction between an algorithm and its implementation applies to the ReLU activations, softmax normalizations, and weight-load sequences that constitute a forward pass.</p>\n<p>Power analysis of a password check shows the mechanism in its simplest form [@kocher2011]. A device that compares a 5-byte password one byte at a time draws a stable power profile while the bytes match, and @fig-encryption2 shows the trace jump at the first wrong byte, where the device halts the comparison.</p>\n<p>The position of the jump reveals how many leading bytes are correct, so repeated guesses collapse a search over all $256^5$ strings into five searches of 256 values each. ML inference can expose analogous data-dependent behavior when a microcontroller branches on a ReLU input's sign or hardware skips zero values, producing signals correlated with activation sparsity. Recovering layer topology or parameters from such a signal requires a further inference step supported by the implementation and the available measurements.</p>\n<p>{{&lt; margin-video \"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iDLfuEBcs8\" \"Power Attack\" \"Colin O'Flynn\" &gt;}}</p>\n<p>The underlying lesson is physical. Computation emits signals, whether as the sound of a cipher machine [@wright1987spycatcher] or of a laptop running a cipher [@genkin2017acoustic], the clicks of a keyboard [@asonov2004], or the current drawn by a processor. For a fleet, the important variant needs no probe at all. On shared hardware, the shared resource itself becomes the measurement instrument. Researchers built a power monitor from logic in their own region of an FPGA shared among users and used it to recover a cryptographic key computed in another user's region [@zhao2018]. The sharing that makes multi-tenancy economical turns caches, memory bandwidth, and power delivery into channels between tenants.</p>\n<p>Machine learning systems inherit this risk when accelerators or edge devices process sensitive data near an observable signal. A local speech-recognition device may leak timing or power patterns correlated with commands. An accelerator may reveal layer structure, parameter access patterns, or batch shape through timing, power, or electromagnetic traces. Side-channel defense therefore asks which signals are observable in the deployment environment and whether shielding, constant-time execution, noise, monitoring, or physical access controls can reduce the signal enough to preserve the trust boundary.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-31\" class=\"cs-h4\">Learned side-channel analysis</h4>\n<p>Machine learning also changes the attacker's side of this analysis. Extracting a key from noisy power traces requires finding signal patterns associated with secret-dependent computations, and a convolutional neural network can learn those patterns from labeled traces, turning part of the analysis into a learned classification task. The attacker still needs suitable measurements and training data, but can reuse the trained model across repeated attempts under compatible conditions. The SCAAML framework (Side-Channel Attacks Assisted with Machine Learning), developed by researchers at Google, is a widely studied and reproducible demonstration of this learned pipeline [@bursztein2024; @burszteindc27].</p>\n<p>Power drawn during an AES S-box substitution depends on the data being processed (@fig-side-channel-curves). The left panel places a possible leakage interval within a longer trace; the right panel magnifies a schematic difference between processed values. Hamming weight counts the ones in a binary value: 1111 has weight 4, 0101 has weight 2, and 0000 has weight 0. The drawing orders amplitudes by that weight to illustrate a leakage model, not a measured law of CMOS switching. Actual leakage depends on circuit structure, state transitions, noise, and countermeasures. A classifier must learn a reproducible relationship in measured traces before these differences can support key recovery.</p>\n<p>Statistical methods use an explicit leakage model. A neural network instead learns features from labeled traces, and its predictions of an intermediate value are combined across traces to score candidate key bytes. This works only if the learned relationship generalizes to traces not used in training.</p>\n<p>In a SCAAML guide, @burszteindc27 used convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to recover AES keys from power traces collected on an STM32F415 microcontroller running the open-source TinyAES implementation. The models predicted intermediate values of the AES algorithm, such as the output of the S-box in the first round, from power traces. The demonstration recovered a full 128-bit key by combining byte-level predictions. Its target was an unprotected implementation, so the number of traces it needed sets no expectation for devices with masking, shuffling, or other countermeasures.</p>\n<p>: <strong>S-box (substitution box)</strong>: The nonlinear byte-substitution table in AES. In the first round, its input combines a plaintext byte with a key byte. Predicting its output is a 256-way classification problem; with the corresponding plaintext byte known, the attacker can map predictions back to candidate key bytes and combine evidence across traces.</p>\n<p>Learned side-channel models can reduce the analysis effort or trace count of an attack when suitable training data and measurable leakage are available. In a fleet built from a few part numbers, reusable attack models create correlated risk. Transfer still requires evaluation across devices, firmware, measurement conditions, and countermeasures; matching part numbers alone do not guarantee it. The defensive response must therefore address both the attacker's access to measurements and the leakage of the computation itself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-31\" class=\"cs-h3\">Leaky interfaces</h3>\n<p>Whereas side-channel attacks leak through unintended physical signals, leaky interfaces expose information or accept unverified inputs through diagnostic, update, and communication channels the system already provides. An exposed baseboard management controller in a data center rack or an active JTAG debug port on a field camera allows an attacker to read memory or rewrite firmware while bypassing every OS and model-layer defense, placing leaky interfaces directly between physical access and lifecycle trust.</p>\n<p>A leaky interface is any access point that reveals more information than intended or accepts commands from the wrong actor. The common causes are weak authentication, missing encryption, inadequate isolation, and development interfaces left exposed in production. Consumer, medical, and industrial systems have repeatedly shown the pattern: Wi-Fi-enabled baby monitors have exposed unsecured remote access ports, and pacemaker vulnerabilities have shown how wireless interfaces can become safety-critical control channels. Debug ports left enabled on production devices are the canonical hardware case: a consumer smart lightbulb, for example, exposed unencrypted Wi-Fi credentials through such a port, giving attackers a path into the home network without defeating any network defense.</p>\n<p>: <strong>IoT device vulnerabilities</strong>: Default credentials, unencrypted communications, and outdated firmware create access paths into connected devices. A compromised smart camera can supply manipulated inputs or participate in distributed attacks; if it also contributes training data or federated updates, that path can carry poisoning attempts. The consequences depend on the device's role and permissions.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Medical device security</strong>: Connected medical devices require secure maintenance over long operating lifetimes. In a device that uses ML, compromised inputs, weights, or preprocessing can alter clinical behavior without stopping the device. Traditional device software can also fail silently, so the additional ML requirement is to evaluate learned behavior alongside firmware integrity and physical safety controls.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Debug port vulnerabilities</strong>: JTAG and Serial Wire Debug can expose memory and execution control when debug access remains enabled and authorized checks are absent or bypassed. An attacker may then recover weights accessible in plaintext, but an exposed connector alone does not defeat correctly enforced debug locks or encryption whose keys remain unavailable.</p>\n<p>The data center has its own privileged maintenance interfaces. A server's baseboard management controller commonly supports power control, console access, and firmware management over an out-of-band network, independently of the host operating system. Compromising its credentials or firmware can therefore expose control paths that host access policies do not govern. The resulting reach depends on network segmentation, controller privileges, and firmware-update protections. The attestation checks of @sec-security-privacy-fleet-attestation help only when trusted measurements cover the affected components and the attacker cannot forge the evidence or alter the verifier's approved state. The same reasoning applies at the edge, where a maintenance interface that lacks authentication can expose user data, alter model weights, or disable security features.</p>\n<p>Securing interfaces is therefore inventory work as much as cryptography. Strong authentication, encrypted communication, anomaly detection, access-control policy, periodic audit, and zero-trust defaults all serve the same principle: every interface must justify what it exposes and who can use it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-32\" class=\"cs-h3\">Supply chain risks</h3>\n<p>Supply chain risks extend the provenance decision from exposed maintenance interfaces to every hardware and software component across every stage of its life. A counterfeit memory module, modified network interface card (NIC) firmware, or compromised open-source dependency replicated across <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.node_count_str]</code> cluster nodes introduces a backdoor before the first training job even starts. The smallest unit of this risk is the counterfeit part. A counterfeit processor, memory module, sensor, or network device may look and function like a genuine one while degrading faster, failing under load, or containing hidden circuitry, so procurement becomes part of the security boundary. Organizations that unknowingly deploy such parts may also violate safety, privacy, or cybersecurity obligations, which makes component provenance evidence that the deployed system matches the certified one.</p>\n<p>A component's life runs through design, fabrication, assembly, distribution, and deployment (@fig-hw-supply-chain), and each stage presents opportunities for tampering or substitution. Recycled electronic waste can be relabeled as new, distributors can mix cloned parts into legitimate shipments, and insiders at manufacturing facilities can embed hardware trojans that routine functional tests may miss. Micrography, X-ray screening, and functional testing provide different evidence, with coverage and cost determined by the suspected modification. Procurement therefore needs risk-based provenance controls as well as inspection; passing a functional test does not establish a component's origin or exclude dormant malicious behavior.</p>\n<p>Fleet procurement changes the shape of this risk. A training cluster is bought in bulk from a small number of part numbers, so each of the <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.node_count_str]</code> nodes of the reference cluster carries the same accelerators, network adapters, management controller, and firmware images as every other. A compromised shared design, firmware release, or production batch can therefore affect many nodes at once. Replication does not remove a vulnerability shared by the replicas, although one compromised unit is evidence about its part number, not proof that every unit carrying it is affected. The provenance bound on trust (principle \\ref{pri-provenance-bounds-trust}) applies to silicon and firmware as much as to data. Supplier, batch, and firmware records help identify the affected population; supplier concentration increases common dependencies even when those records are complete.</p>\n<p>Hardware compromise also changes the economics of response. Software vulnerabilities can often be patched remotely, but a hardware compromise that slips past every check to deployment (@fig-hw-supply-chain) may require physical replacement. That possibility adds replacement cost, service interruption, and revalidation to the expected loss and strengthens the case for preventive checks before deployment.</p>\n<p>A disputed 2018 report of surveillance chips implanted on server motherboards, which the named companies publicly denied [@TheBigHa77], shows why allegations must be separated from verified compromise. A claim about deployed components needs evidence from the relevant fabrication, assembly, distribution, and integration stages, and component validation, runtime monitoring, attestation, and hardware roots of trust each supply part of that evidence.</p>\n<p>The engineering takeaway is that hardware trust must be evidenced continuously across design, fabrication, procurement, deployment, and retirement. A trusted execution environment (TEE) isolates selected code and data from parts of the surrounding host. Attestation supplies cryptographic evidence about that protected environment before another component entrusts it with secrets. Neither mechanism establishes that a model is behaviorally correct, and both depend on an explicit set of trusted hardware and software components.</p>\n<p>Before moving from hardware threats to the defenses anchored in hardware, check that each vulnerability family is attached to the deployment decision it tests.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Processor isolation</strong>: explain how a transient-execution flaw can expose a co-tenant's prompts or keys even after software access control passes.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Physical access</strong>: distinguish what fault injection changes in a running computation from what a side-channel attack observes about it.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Interfaces</strong>: identify a maintenance or debug interface in a training node or edge device that host access policies do not govern.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Correlated risk</strong>: explain why a compromised part number threatens a fleet differently from a compromised individual device.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These hardware threats endanger the model, its data, and the execution environment that protects them. They also set the limit of every software control examined so far, because each of those controls assumes that some layer beneath it remains trustworthy. A defense architecture must therefore state which components it trusts and what each layer adds when another fails.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-33\" class=\"cs-h2\">Comprehensive Defense Architectures</h2>\n<p>An adversary who bypasses an API rate limit may still face authorization checks, restricted outputs, or an isolated execution environment. These controls protect different assets and constrain different actions. Secure ML systems use defense in depth to limit what follows from a failed mechanism. The benefit depends on whether the remaining controls retain their enforcement power; several checks that share the same compromised credential or runtime do not provide independent barriers.</p>\n<p>Countermeasures fall into four architectural tiers (@fig-defense-stack). Hardware mechanisms, defined in @sec-security-privacy-hardware-security-foundations-f5e8, include trusted execution environments, secure boot, HSM key custody, and physical unclonable function (PUF) device identities. Platform controls include container isolation, network segmentation, access control, and runtime telemetry. The model layer includes adversarial training, input validation, output monitoring, and rate limiting, while data-layer controls include differential privacy, encryption at rest, provenance tracking, and secure aggregation.</p>\n<p>These tiers partition defenses by the layer they protect, which differs from the data, model, interface, and infrastructure layers of @sec-security-privacy-attack-surface-analysis-b10b that partition where an adversary enters. The two maps line up except at the interface. Interface controls such as rate limiting, input validation, and output monitoring guard the model's query boundary, so the stack files them with the model tier. The serving platform beneath enforces them, which is why the runtime-monitoring discussion below treats them as platform mechanisms. Infrastructure threats fall to the hardware and platform tiers.</p>\n<p>The dependency arrow highlights the runtime support that many controls require. A compromised host can expose raw records or alter a privacy mechanism before release, but it does not retroactively invalidate the differential privacy guarantee of an artifact already produced by a correctly implemented mechanism. The dependency analysis must identify the protected asset and release, not assume that every control fails together.</p>\n<p>Layered defense (also known as defense in depth) makes these dependencies explicit. Differential privacy bounds what a specified release reveals about an individual's contribution; it does not protect a separate copy of raw training records from theft. Federated learning changes where training data reside but does not, by itself, bound what updates reveal. Hardware isolation can protect execution within its threat model without establishing that an application follows the intended data policy. Combining these mechanisms requires stating each guarantee and checking which assumptions the other layers preserve.</p>\n<p>The stack also orders the dependencies. A rate limit enforced by the serving platform is only as reliable as that platform, and the platform is only as reliable as the firmware and processor it runs on. Trust therefore builds from the bottom tier upward. Silicon anchors it, platform controls govern how a model is deployed and monitored, model-tier choices shape the learned function itself, and the data layer determines what a correctly computed release reveals.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-34\" class=\"cs-h3\">Hardware security foundations</h3>\n<p>Anchoring the bottom tier of that stack in silicon matters because software-only access controls and encrypted disks cannot protect plaintext model weights or keys in RAM once an attacker gains root access on a host OS or cloud hypervisor. Software checks enforced solely by a compromised operating system can be disabled or falsified, and an unlocked debug interface can similarly expose execution state that software policy was intended to protect. Controls enforced in a separate hardware trust domain remain effective even when the operating system, runtime, or application code is compromised, which motivates identifying exactly where each security decision is made and who can alter it.</p>\n<p>Hardware-based security creates a root of trust anchored in silicon, giving higher-layer defenses a foundation that host software cannot rewrite. Machine learning systems deployed in edge devices, embedded systems, and untrusted cloud infrastructure often depend on that anchor because the attacker may have physical access, co-tenant access, or administrative access to parts of the stack.</p>\n<p>The trust boundary becomes especially important when systems must satisfy regulatory requirements. Healthcare ML systems handling protected health information under HIPAA must implement technical safeguards such as access control, audit controls, integrity protection, and transmission security. Systems processing personal data within GDPR's territorial and material scope must also demonstrate appropriate technical and organizational measures, including data-protection-by-design principles. Hardware security does not satisfy these requirements alone, but it provides the boot, runtime, key-management, and device-identity guarantees that software controls often assume.</p>\n<p>Four hardware security mechanisms divide that foundation by separating startup integrity, runtime isolation, key custody, and device identity. Secure boot verifies whether the system started from authentic code, whereas trusted execution environments (TEEs) isolate sensitive computation while it runs on an untrusted host. Because both mechanisms rely on cryptographic keys and device identity, hardware security modules (HSMs) generate and use keys without exposing them to ordinary memory, and physical unclonable functions (PUFs) derive a hardware-bound identity that is harder to copy than a software credential. @Tbl-hw-security-comparison compares the four primitives after each has been examined. They do not map one-to-one onto the four deployment decisions of @tbl-threat_types, and they work best when composed across hardware, firmware, and software boundaries rather than selected as standalone products.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h4\">Trusted execution environments</h4>\n<p>A TEE is first a trust-boundary decision: which parts of the model, key material, input data, or attestation path must remain protected even if the surrounding host is compromised. A trusted execution environment (TEE) is the hardware-isolated processor region that implements that boundary. TEEs enforce confidentiality, integrity, and runtime isolation within a stated threat model. Their guarantee depends on the trusted hardware, firmware, and code inside the protected boundary.</p>\n<p>For machine learning, a TEE can keep model parameters and sensitive inputs confidential during inference and protect execution from host-level tampering. It can, for example, prevent malicious software on the same device from extracting model parameters, or keep computations on facial or fingerprint data inside the protected boundary. Correct execution of an approved model says nothing about whether its predictions are accurate or safe.</p>\n<p>Isolation of this kind appears at two scales. On consumer devices, a dedicated security coprocessor such as Apple's Secure Enclave keeps credentials and biometric features protected even if the application processor kernel is compromised [@apple2024secureenclave]. In the data center, the relevant form is the confidential virtual machine, which protects an entire guest, including its model weights and user inputs, from the hypervisor and host operators, and which the fleet admits into a job only after remote attestation (@sec-security-privacy-fleet-attestation).</p>\n<p>Remote attestation supports the distributed trust decision: before components exchange sensitive data or model updates, a verifier checks fresh evidence about measured code and its hardware-protected boundary against an admission policy. Binding a communication key to that evidence connects the accepted identity to the ensuing channel. Attestation alone does not establish the correctness of every runtime operation. For ML, protection follows the computation, so inputs, weights, and activations remain covered only while their processing and storage stay within the chosen boundary.</p>\n<p>TEE implementations combine execution isolation, protected memory, integrity mechanisms, and protected key storage in different ways. The isolation boundary may surround an application region, a secure processor mode, or an entire virtual machine. Access controls restrict which principals can read protected state, while memory encryption and authentication can extend protection beyond the processor boundary where the architecture supports them. Persistent storage needs its own protection when data leave protected memory. Within the execution boundary, computation uses plaintext directly. The deployment analysis must therefore trace where plaintext exists, which components can access it, and which integrity and freshness checks the platform actually supplies.</p>\n<p>Platforms differ mainly in the size of the protected boundary. ARM TrustZone splits a mobile or embedded processor into secure and normal worlds. Intel SGX isolates individual enclaves inside an application's address space on servers. Confidential virtual machines such as those built on AMD SEV-SNP or Intel TDX move the boundary out to a whole guest operating system, which lets unmodified ML serving stacks run inside it at the cost of a larger trusted code base.</p>\n<p>: <strong>ARM TrustZone</strong>: Introduced in 2004, TrustZone partitions an ARM processor into secure and normal \"worlds\" with hardware-enforced isolation. Its presence on a device does not by itself protect an ML deployment: the model, keys, and data path must actually be placed inside the secure world, which depends on whether the device vendor exposes secure-world applications beyond key storage.</p>\n<p>Apple's system-on-chip (SoC) Secure Enclave shows these boundaries in silicon (@fig-enclave). Its dedicated processor, true random number generator (TRNG), public key accelerator (PKA), and cryptographic engines operate separately from the application processor, which communicates through a mailbox interface. A memory protection engine encrypts and authenticates the enclave's external memory, while protected key paths support storage encryption [@apple2024secureenclave]. On consumer devices, Apple's Face ID applies this architecture by binding its biometric templates and comparison logic to the Secure Enclave rather than treating them as ordinary application data [@apple2024facialmatching]. This example demonstrates isolation of designated security operations and state; it does not imply that arbitrary application model weights or activations reside inside the Secure Enclave, because their protection depends on the execution and data paths the platform supports.</p>\n<p>Trusted execution environments carry costs in every C³ dimension. Protected memory, memory encryption, and transitions between trusted and untrusted modes can add compute and latency, and battery-powered devices also pay in energy for secure memory accesses and cryptographic work. Development cost rises because code inside the boundary must follow isolation, attestation, and update protocols, and some deployments must also pass formal certification such as Common Criteria, an international standard for evaluating security products. Distributed training and federated inference add coordination work when their admission policy requires attestation and protected key exchange. A central verifier can perform admission checks; direct mutual attestation between every pair is not inherently required. TEEs therefore fit deployments whose threat model, whether adversarial users, cloud co-tenants, or malicious insiders, justifies that investment.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h4\">Protected-memory and overhead budgets</h4>\n<p>Hardware security features are budgeted like any other resource. Each primitive spends time at its boundary crossings and capacity in its protected memory, and both costs must be sized before a design commits to a primitive. Hardware security overhead must be measured for the chosen implementation. A secure-world transition has a cost per transition, not automatically a cost per inference: the result depends on how often the inference path crosses the boundary and what data it copies. Encryption, cache effects, and paging add separate costs. Energy overhead requires power and elapsed-time measurements; it cannot be inferred from a transition-latency figure alone.</p>\n<p>Memory is the second budget. The protected working set competes for platform-specific memory capacity. Some implementations support protected paging beyond resident capacity, so overflow can impose substantial movement costs rather than an absolute model-size limit. Compression or partitioning changes those costs and may also change which values remain protected.</p>\n<p>Security features scale differently than computational resources. Protected memory capacity, not host memory, sets how much of a model can remain resident inside a TEE without paging. On an older Intel SGX configuration with a <code>[TEEMemoryFootprint.sgx_epc_mb_str]</code> enclave page cache (the protected resident memory), ResNet-50's FP32 weights alone would fill about <code>[TEEMemoryFootprint.resnet50_fp32_epc_pct_str]</code> of it. A ResNet-18 model is about <code>[TEEMemoryFootprint.resnet18_fp32_mb_str]</code> with FP32 weights or <code>[TEEMemoryFootprint.resnet18_int8_mb_str]</code> with INT8 weights, while ResNet-50 is about <code>[TEEMemoryFootprint.resnet50_fp32_mb_str]</code> with FP32 weights or <code>[TEEMemoryFootprint.resnet50_int8_mb_str]</code> with INT8 weights before activations and runtime buffers. The fleet's reference model sits far beyond that scale. The FP16 weights of the Archetype A (GPT-4/Llama-3) model occupy as many bytes as the <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.grad_total_gb_str]</code> gradient it reduces each step, orders of magnitude more than an enclave page cache. Fleet-scale confidential computing therefore draws its boundary around a whole confidential virtual machine, an entire guest protected from the host, rather than an application enclave.</p>\n<p>Beyond capacity, memory protection adds latency whose size depends on which threats are in scope. Encryption and authentication are distinct operations, and AES encryption alone does not verify integrity. An authenticated mode or a separate authentication mechanism is needed when tampering is in scope, and the latency must be measured for the actual data volume and hardware.</p>\n<p>TEE assurance depends on its protected initialization and attestation path, not merely on when the host boots. Some enclave designs explicitly tolerate an untrusted host operating system, while other trusted environments depend on a verified platform startup chain. Secure boot complements these boundaries by authenticating the components included in that chain; the threat model must identify which firmware and initialization code remain trusted.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h4\">Secure boot</h4>\n<p>To authenticate that initialization chain, secure boot ensures that a device boots only software components that are cryptographically verified under the platform's configured trust policy. At startup, each stage of the boot process, comprising the bootloader, kernel, and base operating system, is checked against a known-good digital signature. If any signature fails verification, the boot sequence is halted, blocking components that fail authentication. The chain rests on a trusted initial stage; signed but vulnerable software remains a risk.</p>\n<p>A compromised boot process, particularly on embedded or edge hardware, can load malicious software before the ML runtime begins, enabling attackers to intercept model weights, tamper with training data, or reroute inference results. Such breaches can lead to incorrect or manipulated predictions, unauthorized data access, or device repurposing for botnets or crypto-mining.</p>\n<p>For machine learning systems, secure boot authenticates the components included in its verification chain. Model artifacts receive this protection only when a verified loader checks their signatures or a signed manifest before loading them; boot verification alone does not authenticate weights read later from storage. Secure update flows must extend the same verification policy to replacement firmware, runtimes, and model artifacts.</p>\n<p>Secure boot frequently operates in tandem with hardware-based TEEs to establish an unbroken chain of trust prior to code execution. The chain is a stepped verification handshake that pairs each trusted execution stage with an explicit cryptographic authentication gate [@nist2018sp800193], as @fig-secure-boot traces. Execution proceeds from an immutable boot root through a verified bootloader and operating system, halting and rejecting the component if any signature check fails. For machine learning pipelines, Stage 3 extends this chain beyond conventional system software: the verified OS or loader authenticates the model manifest and artifact digest before launching the authorized ML workload. The terminal stage emphasizes that while boot-time authentication blocks unauthorized or tampered binaries, dynamic runtime controls remain necessary to guard against post-boot attacks and data-dependent exploits.</p>\n<p>Startup trust is what makes the runtime isolation described earlier meaningful, and it must survive updates. Apple's signed boot chain verifies the Secure Enclave firmware before any biometric data is processed and installs only software versions Apple is still signing, so a secure initial boot keeps its assumptions only through a controlled update path [@apple2021bootprocess; @apple2024secureenclave; @apple2021securesoftwareupdates].</p>\n<p>Secure boot also creates key-management and lifecycle obligations. Enterprises must protect signing keys, distribute trusted verification material, and support revocation or recovery when an authorized key is compromised. The affected trust chain depends on that key's authority and the platform's recovery design. Verification adds startup work, and every component covered by the policy must remain authenticated through updates, whether by an individual signature or a digest bound into an authenticated manifest. The deployment must identify which components the chain actually covers.</p>\n<p>At fleet scale, device identity and evidence of startup state support distinct admission decisions. The Device Identifier Composition Engine [@tcg2018dice] combines a device secret with software measurements to derive identities tied to the measured state. IEEE 802.1AR [@ieee8021ar2018] defines cryptographically bound device identities and their provisioning; a device identity alone does not establish which software is running. Fleet attestation (@sec-security-privacy-fleet-attestation) connects authenticated identity with acceptable state evidence before a node receives keys or joins a job.</p>\n<p>Secure boot, when extended through the model loader and complemented by controlled updates, can establish that the configured authority approved the loaded components. Correct model behavior and freedom from vulnerabilities in the authenticated software remain separate claims. Runtime isolation and monitoring address different parts of that assurance argument.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h4\">Hardware security modules</h4>\n<p>While TEEs and secure boot provide runtime isolation and integrity verification, hardware security modules (HSMs) specialize in the cryptographic operations that underpin these protections. An HSM is a tamper-resistant physical device designed to perform cryptographic operations and securely manage digital keys. HSMs are standard in finance, defense, and cloud infrastructure; in an ML pipeline they protect the keys on which model integrity and data confidentiality depend.</p>\n<p>HSMs provide an isolated, hardened environment for performing sensitive operations such as key generation, digital signing, encryption, and decryption. Unlike general-purpose processors, they are engineered to withstand physical tampering and side-channel attacks, and they typically include protected storage, cryptographic accelerators, and internal audit logging. HSMs may be implemented as standalone appliances, plug-in modules, or integrated chips embedded within broader systems.</p>\n<p>In machine learning systems, HSMs can protect signing keys and the key-encryption keys that wrap, or encrypt, data-encryption keys. The distinction matters because a root key may remain inside the HSM while a workload uses a released data key inside its authorized execution boundary. Two distributed workflows illustrate this role. An HSM can protect the certificate authority's signing key used to certify enrolled devices; each device then proves possession of its own private key. Certification establishes identity, not benign behavior; an authenticated but compromised client can still submit poisoned updates. An HSM can also wrap a model-decryption key for a TEE's attestation-bound public key after the verifier accepts the evidence. Keeping weights out of plaintext host memory then depends on the TEE's key handling, loader, and execution path, not on the wrapping operation alone.</p>\n<p>HSMs also play a role in maintaining the integrity of machine learning models. In many production pipelines, models must be signed before deployment to ensure that only verified versions are accepted into runtime environments. The signing keys used to authenticate models can be stored and managed within the HSM, providing cryptographic assurance that the deployed artifact is authentic and untampered. Similarly, secure firmware updates and configuration changes, regardless of whether they pertain to models, hyperparameters, or supporting infrastructure, can be validated using signatures produced by the HSM.</p>\n<p>During model training, data often originate from distributed and potentially untrusted sources. An HSM secures these training pipelines by holding the keys that sign training-data manifests and wrap data-encryption keys, which keeps encryption, integrity checks, and access control enforcement within a hardware-protected boundary. Regulated industries such as healthcare and finance rely on these protections to satisfy statutory safeguards. For instance, the HIPAA Security Rule requires technical safeguards such as access control, audit controls, integrity protections, and transmission security, while treating encryption and some integrity controls as addressable implementation specifications. GDPR similarly lists pseudonymization and encryption as examples of appropriate technical and organizational measures where they fit the risk.</p>\n<p>HSM deployment introduces a capacity and integration budget. Signing throughput, key-unwrapping latency, and network access to a remote HSM must be measured for the operations actually used, because the HSM sits on the signing and key-release path and its throughput, not the accelerator's, bounds how fast keys reach the fleet. The protected key boundary can also require changes to application interfaces and fleet provisioning. Certification and procurement requirements, including applicable cryptographic-module standards, constrain which devices and configurations can be used.</p>\n<p>HSMs combine key custody with protected cryptographic operations. Resource-constrained embedded systems may need a smaller mechanism for a narrower task: deriving a device-specific secret without storing a persistent copy of that secret. Physical unclonable functions use a chip's physical properties for this purpose. They can support key derivation and identity, but do not replace the HSM's full cryptographic processing and policy-enforcement role.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h4\">Physical unclonable functions</h4>\n<p>PUFs provide a hardware-intrinsic mechanism for cryptographic key generation and device authentication by exploiting physical randomness in semiconductor fabrication [@gassend2002]. Unlike traditional keys stored in memory, a PUF generates secret values based on microscopic variations in a chip's physical properties, which are inherent to manufacturing processes and difficult to clone or predict, even by the manufacturer. Because of variation such as doping concentration and line edge roughness, chips fabricated from the same design masks differ measurably in timing or voltage behavior, and a PUF circuit amplifies those differences into a device-dependent response to each input challenge. An attacker may still predict responses from a vulnerable design without physically cloning the chip.</p>\n<p>: <strong>PUF (physical unclonable function)</strong>: Manufacturing variation can support device-specific key derivation without storing a persistent copy of the key. Separate device keys limit the reach of a storage-key compromise, but they do not prevent extraction of plaintext model weights during execution or attacks that predict a vulnerable PUF's responses.</p>\n<p>This challenge-response mechanism allows PUFs to serve several cryptographic purposes. They can be used to derive device-specific keys that never need to be stored externally, reducing the attack surface for key exfiltration. The same mechanism also supports secure authentication and attestation, where devices must prove their identity to trusted servers or hardware gateways. These properties make PUFs a natural fit for machine learning systems deployed in embedded and distributed environments.</p>\n<p>For ML fleets, a PUF can reduce the need to inject and persistently store a unique secret in each device. The phone-class devices of Archetype C (Federated MobileNet) and the cameras and drones of edge deployments still need enrollment: the verifier must associate a device with trusted authentication evidence and manage its admission and revocation. A PUF-derived key can encrypt the on-device model at rest, so copying the stored file does not yield the device-bound key that decrypts it. Authentication can use enrolled challenge-response information or a credential backed by a derived key, depending on the design. Both protections have limits. Plaintext weights extracted during execution expose the shared model regardless of how the storage key was generated, and successful authentication establishes which device is speaking, not that its runtime software is uncompromised.</p>\n<p>Panel A of @fig-puf states the common principle, and panels B to D show three PUF architectures that draw on different physical sources of variation [@gao2020]. In each, two devices of the same design answer challenge $\\PufChallenge$ with different responses ($\\PufResponse_1 \\ne \\PufResponse_2$), whether the response is an optical speckle pattern, an arbiter's response bit $\\PufResponseBit$ set by racing signal paths, or the startup state of an SRAM cell. These responses are not perfectly repeatable under every operating condition, which motivates the error correction and calibration discussed next.</p>\n<p>PUFs also present engineering challenges. Their responses drift with temperature, voltage, and aging, so production designs add error correction or helper data and calibrate devices during fabrication, at some cost in layout area and test time. Managing large sets of challenge-response pairs raises questions of storage, consistency, and revocation, and the statistical structure of some PUF designs makes their responses predictable to machine learning-based modeling attacks if enough pairs are observed. Within those limits, PUFs reduce the key-management burden for large device fleets and supply a hardware-bound identity that complements secure boot, TEEs, and HSMs.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h4\">Mechanisms comparison</h4>\n<p>Trusted execution environments, secure boot, hardware security modules, and physical unclonable functions address distinct layers of the platform rather than substituting for one another. The design choice is which primitive owns which trust boundary, and @tbl-hw-security-comparison sets the four side by side by function, use in ML, and trade-offs.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Mechanism</strong></th><th><strong>Primary function</strong></th><th><strong>Common use in ML</strong></th><th><strong>Trade-offs</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Trusted Execution Environment (TEE)</strong></td><td>Isolated runtime environment for secure computation</td><td>Secure inference and on-device privacy for sensitive inputs and outputs</td><td>Added complexity, memory limits, performance cost; requires trusted code development</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Secure Boot</strong></td><td>Verified boot sequence and firmware validation</td><td>Authenticates boot components; model protection requires a verifying loader</td><td>Key management complexity, vendor lock-in; startup performance impact</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Hardware Security Module (HSM)</strong></td><td>Secure key generation, storage, and cryptographic processing</td><td>Signing ML models, securing training pipelines, verifying firmware</td><td>High cost, integration overhead, limited I/O; requires infrastructure-level provisioning</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Physical Unclonable Function (PUF)</strong></td><td>Hardware-bound identity and key derivation</td><td>Model binding, device authentication, protecting IP in embedded deployments</td><td>Environmental sensitivity, modeling attacks; needs error correction and calibration</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Hardware Security Primitives Compared</strong>: Machine learning systems use diverse hardware defenses (trusted execution environments, secure boot, hardware security modules, and physical unclonable functions) to establish trust and protect sensitive data across the system stack. Each mechanism addresses a different security challenge, from runtime isolation and integrity verification to key management and device identity, and each carries its own trade-offs in performance and complexity. {#tbl-hw-security-comparison}</p>\n<p>Together, these hardware primitives support a defense-in-depth strategy for securing ML systems in adversarial environments. Their integration needs an explicit assurance argument: which components remain trusted, which evidence supports that trust, and which attacks fall outside the protection. Safety-sensitive devices and distributed learning systems need this argument as much as they need the mechanisms themselves.</p>\n<p>Hardware primitives supply evidence and enforcement for selected platform properties. Deployment controls use those capabilities to decide which model artifacts may load and which callers may use them. The transition from platform assurance to application policy is therefore a composition problem: the loader and serving endpoint must enforce the intended permissions within the protected boundary.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-35\" class=\"cs-h3\">Secure model deployment</h3>\n<p>Training a robust model inside a locked cluster accomplishes nothing if the exported artifact is packaged as an untrusted pickle file, stored unencrypted, or served behind an API key with no per-principal quota. A trusted platform establishes where a model runs; deployment controls decide who can load it, who can call it, and whether its behavior survives contact with users, operators, and attackers. As models move into edge devices, public APIs, and multi-tenant platforms, serialization, packaging, and endpoint access policies determine whether the integrity, confidentiality, and availability established during training survive inference.</p>\n<p>Because @sec-security-privacy-model-extraction-defenses-8f3b already quantified the API-boundary query quotas, output perturbation, and pricing regimes that counter model extraction, deployment-stage controls here focus on artifact custody, cryptographic packaging, and endpoint authentication infrastructure, while the design-stage controls of @sec-security-privacy-secure-model-design-69a6 shape the model's behavior and ownership evidence. Deployment controls also have to survive ordinary optimization work such as quantization, pruning, and knowledge distillation, where performance improvements must not compromise artifact integrity, ownership evidence, or access-control boundaries.</p>\n<p>Once training is complete, the model must be securely packaged for deployment. Authenticated encryption protects stored artifacts when its keys remain inside the intended trust boundary; a signed manifest authenticates the expected artifact digest before loading. Obfuscation may raise reverse-engineering effort but does not provide the confidentiality contract of encryption. Runtime key custody and the loader's verification policy determine whether packaging protection survives execution.</p>\n<p>Deployment environments must enforce access policies at inference endpoints. OAuth supplies delegated authorization; mutual TLS authenticates certificate-bearing peers; API keys identify credentials that the service maps to permissions and quotas. Role-based access control (RBAC) can restrict those permissions by operational role. These mechanisms support the query controls in @sec-security-privacy-model-extraction-defenses-8f3b, but a credential is not proof of a unique human or organization. An attacker who can obtain many accounts can still distribute queries across identities, so account issuance, cumulative budgets, and abuse detection remain part of the boundary.</p>\n<p>An API key demonstrates possession of a credential and allows the backend to enforce its associated policies. Keeping keys out of source code reduces accidental exposure in version control, but environment variables alone do not prevent leaks through logs, process inspection, or a compromised runtime. Secret retrieval, scoped permissions, rotation, and redacted logging must be designed together. Model verification against an authenticated manifest, input limits, rate controls, and security-event logging complement credential management, while runtime monitoring (@sec-security-privacy-runtime-system-monitoring-a71c) extends these protections into active operation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-36\" class=\"cs-h3\">Runtime system monitoring</h3>\n<p>A model binary that passed cryptographic signature verification at boot can still receive adversarial prompts, drift into unsafe generations, or have its weights modified in memory during operation. Secure deployment controls who can load the model and call the endpoint, while runtime monitoring checks whether the live system is still behaving like the approved system across cloud services, edge devices, and embedded hardware.</p>\n<p>Runtime monitoring must decide whether an input should reach the model, whether the output is behaving normally, and whether the serving system is still the one originally deployed. The corresponding mechanisms are input validation, output monitoring, and system integrity checks.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-37\" class=\"cs-h4\">Input validation</h4>\n<p>Input validation checks incoming data before it reaches a machine learning model. Format and range checks enforce explicit input contracts, while statistical and semantic checks estimate whether an input fits expected conditions. These checks can reject malformed inputs that disrupt preprocessing or inference and some adversarial inputs crafted to trigger incorrect predictions. Passing validation establishes only that the implemented checks accepted the input, not that the model will handle it correctly.</p>\n<p>That gap arises because small, carefully chosen changes to input data can still cause models to make high-confidence but incorrect predictions [@goodfellow2015explaining]. Such inputs may satisfy ordinary type and range checks. Input validation therefore complements model robustness evaluation; it cannot replace testing how the learned function behaves on valid but adversarial inputs.</p>\n<p>Validation techniques range from low-level checks of size, type, and value range to semantic filters, such as a face detector on an uploaded image or a speech activity detector on a voice clip. These checks reject inputs that violate the declared contract, but an adversarial input that satisfies the contract still reaches the model, and a semantic filter's own error rates belong in the validation assessment.</p>\n<p>In generative systems, input validation can include prompt filtering against an application's content policy. A medical education application, for example, might reject requests to generate fabricated treatment claims. Filters may use keyword lists, regular expressions, or lightweight classifiers that estimate prompt intent. Their effectiveness depends on the policy, detector, and inputs tested, including paraphrases and adversarial prompts. They cannot establish that an accepted prompt will produce an acceptable output, which is why output monitoring remains necessary.</p>\n<p>In some applications, distributional checks are also used to assess whether incoming data statistically resembles what the model saw during training. A computer vision pipeline, for instance, might compare the color histogram of an input image to a baseline distribution and flag outliers for manual review or rejection. These validations can be lightweight (heuristics or threshold rules) or learned (small models trained to detect distribution shift or adversarial artifacts). Their value depends on which invalid or hostile inputs they reject and how often they reject legitimate inputs. Since an accepted input can still induce an incorrect result, the next boundary is the model's output.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-37\" class=\"cs-h4\">Output monitoring</h4>\n<p>Even when inputs pass validation, adversarial or unexpected behavior may still emerge at the model's output. Output monitoring observes confidence, prediction entropy, class distribution, or response patterns to flag deviations from expected behavior. A monitor can contain a detected failure when its fallback acts before the output causes harm. Missed detections, delayed alerts, and incorrect fallbacks limit that protection, so detection quality and response timing belong in the same evaluation.</p>\n<p>The signal a monitor watches depends on the model's modality. For a discriminative classifier, unexpected changes in confidence, class frequencies, or output entropy can trigger investigation, but do not identify their cause. A legitimate workload change, model defect, or attack can produce similar deviations. Content-moderation monitors can compare predicted \"safe\" labels with auxiliary reference signals; fraud-detection monitors can flag sudden score changes during high-volume periods. In each case, the system compares the live stream with an expected distribution and applies a chosen response, such as human review or a conservative baseline, while investigation distinguishes the possible causes.</p>\n<p>Generative text-to-image systems can produce high-fidelity imagery that violates content safety policies, platform guidelines, or user expectations. Downstream postgeneration classifiers mitigate these risks by assessing generated content for violence, nudity, or brand misuse and suppressing, blurring, or rejecting outputs that cross predefined thresholds. Some systems also inspect internal representations (for example, attention maps or latent embeddings) to anticipate potential misuse before content is rendered.</p>\n<p>Prompt filtering alone is nonetheless insufficient. Text-to-image systems can be manipulated through implicitly adversarial prompts, queries that appear benign but lead to policy-violating outputs, as the Adversarial Nibbler red-teaming project demonstrated on models such as Stable Diffusion [@quaye2024; @parrish2023adversarial]. Such prompts bypass filters because the risk arises from model behavior during generation rather than from lexical cues in the prompt.</p>\n<p>Prompts that pass a filter can still yield graphic outputs, such as the three blurred generations in @fig-adversarial-nibbler, so an output needs assessment against the application's policy even when the prompt appears acceptable. Diagnosing how a prompt bypassed a filter requires prompt, model, and filter records alongside the outputs, and the output classifiers that catch these cases need their own errors evaluated.</p>\n<p>For language generation, output monitoring targets toxicity, hallucinated claims, and off-distribution responses. For example, a customer support chatbot may be monitored for keyword presence, tonal alignment, or semantic coherence. If a response contains profanity, unsupported assertions, or syntactically malformed text, the system may trigger a rephrasing, initiate a fallback to scripted templates, or halt the response altogether.</p>\n<p>Output monitoring can combine rule-based heuristics with learned detectors trained on historical outputs. These detectors feed alerts into incident response pipelines or block outputs before release. Adversarial training aims to improve the model's behavior, while monitoring adds a separate opportunity to detect and contain failures during operation. Any claimed damage bound must account for missed detections, response latency, and the consequences of the selected fallback. Guardrail classifiers such as Llama Guard and ShieldGemma (@sec-security-privacy-llmspecific-threat-vectors-65f2) occupy the same position in the runtime stack, so their own error rates belong in the deployment's safety evaluation.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-37\" class=\"cs-h4\">Integrity checks</h4>\n<p>While input and output monitoring focus on model behavior, system integrity checks compare model files, execution environments, and serving infrastructure with approved state. Their coverage depends on what is measured, when it is measured, and whether the measurement path remains trusted. Verifying a file before loading detects covered artifact changes at that point; it does not establish that all subsequent execution remains untampered. One of the most common integrity mechanisms is cryptographic model verification: before a model is loaded into memory, the system computes a cryptographic hash (for example, SHA-256) of the model file and compares it against the expected digest in an authenticated, signed release manifest.</p>\n<p>Access control and audit logging complement cryptographic checks. ML systems should restrict access to model files using role-based permissions and monitor file access patterns. For instance, repeated attempts to read model checkpoints from a nonstandard path, or inference requests from unauthorized IP ranges, may indicate tampering, privilege escalation, or insider threats. In cloud environments, container- or VM-based isolation helps enforce process and memory boundaries, but these protections can erode over time because of misconfiguration or supply chain vulnerabilities.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Container vs. VM isolation</strong>: Containers share the host kernel, which keeps overhead low and model scale-out fast but leaves a kernel attack surface shared with co-tenants. VMs separate guest kernels and memory at additional resource cost, yet hypervisor flaws and shared-hardware side channels can still enable cross-tenant attacks on a multi-tenant serving fleet.</p>\n<p>For example, a regulated healthcare ML deployment can organize integrity evidence around three gates. The loader compares the model artifact's cryptographic hash with an authenticated, signed release manifest. Dependency validation checks approved package versions and authenticated artifact digests; a pinned version number names a package without proving that its contents are the approved ones. Platform attestation checks measured state against an approved configuration, with coverage determined by the attestation mechanism rather than an assumption of continuous execution verification. These gates supply evidence relevant to HIPAA and other applicable obligations, but the organization must assess the complete system and its operational use to establish compliance.</p>\n<p>: <strong>HIPAA ML requirements</strong>: For electronic protected health information, the HIPAA Security Rule treats encryption at rest and in transit as addressable specifications, which an organization implements when reasonable and appropriate or replaces with a documented equivalent [@hhs2005hipaasecurity]. Its audit-control standard requires mechanisms that record and examine activity in systems holding that information, scoped by risk analysis rather than a mandate to log every read. Cloud services handling it generally require Business Associate Agreements. In an ML pipeline, these duties become risk-based audit coverage, protected checkpoints, controlled access, and evidence that the safeguards are operating.</p>\n<p>Some systems also implement runtime memory verification, such as scanning for unexpected model parameter changes or checking that memory-mapped model weights remain unaltered during execution. While more common in high-assurance systems, such checks are becoming more feasible with the adoption of secure enclaves and trusted runtimes. Together, system integrity checks and input/output monitoring provide complementary evidence: integrity checks can identify changes to measured artifacts even when predictions appear normal, while behavioral monitors can flag failures even when every artifact matches its approved version. Their combined evidence guides investigation and containment without equating artifact authenticity with correct model behavior.</p>\n<p>Integrity checks become ML supply-chain controls only when they connect every deployable artifact. A signed model file is insufficient if the training dataset, dependency lockfile, preprocessing code, registry promotion event, container image, and deployment attestation cannot be tied to the same release record. That release record makes the provenance bound on trust (principle \\ref{pri-provenance-bounds-trust}) operational, and it gives rollback and forensic analysis a concrete object. Operators can ask which data snapshot produced the checkpoint, which dependencies built the serving image, which registry principal promoted it, and which production endpoints loaded it.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-37\" class=\"cs-h4\">Response and rollback</h4>\n<p>A detected breach, anomaly, or performance degradation must lead to a defined response. The immediate task is containment; recovery then restores a trustworthy service and addresses the cause. ML systems add model checkpoints, training-data lineage, and learned behavior to the software versions, credentials, and runtime state that incident responders already need to examine.</p>\n<p>Detection thresholds and automated rollback triggers work here as they do for any production model; security changes the signal set and the order of operations. Escalation can come from input validation (a rise in invalid inputs), output monitoring (a drop in prediction confidence), or integrity checks (a failed model signature verification). A security trigger must also preserve evidence before the automated response changes the system's state, because a rollback that overwrites the suspect artifact destroys the record an investigation needs.</p>\n<p>↩ Automated rollback triggers for production models are established in @sec-ml-operations-scale-automated-rollback-triggers-ffa3.</p>\n<p>One common strategy is model rollback, where the system reverts to a previously verified release. If a newly deployed fraud detector misclassifies transactions, an earlier checkpoint can restore service only if it remains compatible with the current data path and does not share the defect or compromise. Rollback therefore requires an authenticated release record covering weights, preprocessing, dependencies, and configuration, together with criteria for accepting the fallback under current conditions.</p>\n<p>In high-availability environments, model isolation can help contain failures. Removing a suspect instance from load balancers stops new serving traffic, while restricting its network and credential access limits other actions. Preserving an isolated copy supports forensic analysis as unaffected replicas serve requests. The canary stages of @sec-ml-operations-scale-canary-deployment-3d8b serve a different purpose, evaluating a candidate release with limited live exposure. They do not substitute for quarantining a known compromised instance, because a small traffic share does not make continued use of a compromised model safe, and a rare trigger may never appear during the canary window.</p>\n<p>Traffic throttling is another immediate response tool. If an adversarial actor is probing a public inference API at high volume, the system can rate-limit or temporarily block offending IP ranges while continuing to serve trusted clients. This containment technique helps prevent abuse without requiring full system shutdown.</p>\n<p>Once immediate containment is in place, investigation and recovery can begin. Evidence may include protected input logs, parameter deltas between model versions, or memory snapshots from inference containers. Generative systems add prompts, retrieval context, guardrail decisions, and secret-access events. These records help reconstruct what instructions and data the model received, what it produced, and which controls intervened; a guardrail log records the detector's decision rather than proving that it caught every violation. Evidence collection must also protect the sensitive content it captures through scoped access, redaction, retention limits, and integrity controls. The aim is enough trustworthy context to distinguish privacy breaches, model-integrity failures, and ordinary model errors without creating an uncontrolled second repository of private data. Applicable incident-notification obligations remain part of the response process.</p>\n<p>Recovery must repair the boundary that failed. A stolen credential requires revocation and controlled replacement; a compromised loader requires a trusted deployment path; poisoned training data may require dataset repair and retraining. Changes must pass through a secure update process using authenticated artifacts, trusted build pipelines, and validated data. For a prompt injection incident, recovery should examine how untrusted content gained authority over instructions or data access; additional training and filtering may help, but cannot replace enforcing the affected authorization boundary. Postincident review then follows the platform practice of @sec-ml-operations-scale-postincident-reviews-a8f5, with a security review adding three records to it: which boundary failed, which credentials and artifacts were revoked or rebuilt, and which attack cases now enter the evaluation suite.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-37\" class=\"cs-h3\">Secure model design</h3>\n<p>Hardening the serving container does not stop an attacker from querying a high-confidence prediction on an out-of-distribution input or stripping ownership metadata from a stolen weight file. While deployment and runtime controls act on the platform around the model, the model tier above them acts on the learned function itself. Many of the vulnerabilities that platform controls try to catch at serving time can be reduced before deployment by choosing what the model can learn, how it behaves under uncertainty, and what evidence of ownership it carries.</p>\n<p>One design strategy is to limit when the system acts on a model prediction. Confidence calibration makes reported confidence more meaningful under the evaluated conditions, while abstention permits the system to defer uncertain predictions. Neither mechanism establishes robustness by construction: an adversarial input may still receive high confidence. Output smoothing and other regularization methods likewise require evaluation under the specified attack. These design choices support behavioral robustness testing rather than replacing it.</p>\n<p>Another design-stage consideration is the use of model watermarking, a technique for embedding verifiable ownership signatures directly into the model's parameters or output behavior [@adi2018turning]. A watermark might be implemented, for example, as a hidden response pattern triggered by specific inputs, or as a parameter-space perturbation that does not affect accuracy but is statistically identifiable. Abstention and watermarking therefore serve different purposes: abstention controls when the system acts, while watermarking supplies evidence about model origin, and each needs its own evaluation.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Model watermarking</strong>: In trigger-set watermarking, the owner's secret inputs reliably identify the model while normal test accuracy is preserved [@adi2018turning]. The systems challenge is robustness, because a watermark must survive fine-tuning, pruning, and distillation, the same operations an attacker uses to strip ownership marks from a stolen model.</p>\n<p>Smaller or compressed models can reduce the protected working set and make an isolation boundary less costly to implement. That resource benefit buys no resistance to extraction, memorization, or side channels. A simpler model may be easier to approximate from queries, and leakage depends on the implementation and exposed outputs rather than parameter count alone.</p>\n<p>↪ Where explanations belong in a deployed system, and what they cost, is developed in @sec-responsible-ai-architecting-explainability-transparency-75e8.</p>\n<p>In high-risk applications, designers may also prefer models whose decisions reviewers can inspect, which supports investigation of vulnerabilities and biases without establishing their absence. All of these model design choices reflect trade-offs between accuracy, robustness, transparency, and system complexity, and making them before deployment gives engineers an opportunity to evaluate the resulting behavior and resource cost together. Ordinary accuracy evaluation can still miss one risk entirely. A model can retain training examples and later expose them through its outputs, the leakage of @sec-security-privacy-leakage-authorized-outputs, which design choices at this tier do not remove.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-38\" class=\"cs-h3\">Privacy-preserving data techniques</h3>\n<p>Addressing that training-data exposure moves the defense to the top tier of the stack, where the data layer forces an explicit decision about who may observe raw records, individual updates, and released results. Leaving raw typing logs on phones via federated learning can still leak private text through unmasked gradients, just as replacing patient records with a GAN-generated synthetic dataset can still leak records that the generator memorized. Keeping records centralized, leaving them on client devices, computing over encrypted values, and generating synthetic substitutes therefore change different parts of that exposure. That decision is architectural before it is algorithmic because it fixes the system variables tracked throughout the defense stack: information leakage, model utility, runtime cost, and the strength of the guarantee. ML workflows often depend on raw, high-fidelity data to train effective models, so privacy-preserving techniques should be read as different ways to move or bound that exposure rather than as interchangeable tools.</p>\n<p>Each technique excludes a different observer. Federated learning keeps raw records away from the central server, secure aggregation hides individual updates from the coordinator, homomorphic encryption and secure multiparty computation hide inputs from the parties that compute on them, and synthetic data withholds the source records from the people who use the substitute. De-identification and k-anonymity, which strip or coarsen identifying fields, by contrast change only the shape of a released table.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-39\" class=\"cs-h4\">Federated learning</h4>\n<p>A release guarantee and a data-location requirement answer different privacy questions. Differential privacy bounds the effect of a protected contribution on released results; federated learning keeps raw training records on participating clients. Policy, consent, or data-use agreements may forbid centralizing those records, making federated architectures a natural fit. The updates exchanged during training still require their own exposure analysis.</p>\n<p>Federated learning restructures the learning process so that client data remain decentralized while model updates are communicated. Data locality alone does not establish privacy, however, because the gradient updates that clients transmit can leak sensitive information about the examples that produced them. Decentralization is necessary when the deployment contract requires local records, but a trusted curator applying differential privacy to centralized data can provide a guarantee under a different trust model.</p>\n<p>↩ Federated coordination protocols, weighted averaging, and client update cycles are established in @sec-edge-intelligence-federated-learning-6e7e.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-39\" class=\"cs-h4\">Gradient leakage and privacy-preserving computation</h4>\n<p>Gradient inversion attacks (such as Deep Leakage from Gradients) demonstrate reconstruction of training examples from exposed gradients under the studied conditions [@zhu2019deep; @nasr2019]. Recovery depends on the model, batch, update processing, and attacker's knowledge; it is not guaranteed for every client update. The gradient nevertheless provides an information channel even when raw data never leave the device. One defense is the secure aggregation protocol, which limits the server to an aggregate under specified participation and collusion assumptions.</p>\n<p>↩ Secure aggregation and its pairwise masks, which reveal only the sum of client updates to the coordinator, are established in @sec-edge-intelligence-federated-privacy-a1ed.</p>\n<p>Each client's update carries pairwise masks that cancel in the server's sum, so the coordinator recovers the aggregate without receiving any unmasked update. That protection holds only under stated conditions. Enrollment must be authenticated, the participating clients must complete the round, and at least two noncolluding clients must keep their shared masks from the coordinator. A production protocol must therefore formalize its collusion threshold and its dropout recovery, because a client that departs mid-round strands masks that no longer cancel. The aggregate itself still discloses information, potentially including an individual contribution when combined with other known contributions, so hiding the messages does not by itself provide differential privacy.</p>\n<p>Gradient leakage motivates both secure aggregation protocols [@bonawitz2017] and differential privacy, but the trust boundary determines which version of DP applies. Central differential privacy assumes a trusted curator or aggregation service can see raw records or updates before releasing a randomized result. Local differential privacy randomizes each client's protected contribution before the server sees it, reducing trust in the server but often paying a larger utility cost. Secure aggregation limits the server to an aggregate rather than individual updates; a separate, accounted DP mechanism can bound what the released result reveals. Noise added after the server sees the exact aggregate protects the release audience, not that server. Protecting against an untrusted coordinator requires randomization or a trusted computation boundary before it observes the protected result. Compression can change the information in an update, but communication savings alone establish no privacy bound. Each of these protections also carries a C$^3$ price. Masking adds client compute, key agreement and masked uploads add communication, and dropout recovery adds protocol-specific coordination governed by participation thresholds and deadlines.</p>\n<p>↩ Gradient compression as a lever against bandwidth scarcity is established in @sec-communication-collective-operations-collective-operations-gradient-compression-7a5c.</p>\n<p>For the Archetype C mobile/federated workload, Google's Gboard keyboard (whose on-device next-word suggestion and autocomplete inference interfaces appear in @fig-ondevice-gboard) provides a concrete production baseline for learning from data retained on edge devices. The original keyboard study demonstrates federated training, and the systems report describes the production orchestration challenges [@hard2018federated; @bonawitz2019towards]. Those reports do not by themselves establish that every described model combines secure aggregation with a formal DP guarantee. The engineering pattern separates three contracts: FL keeps raw typing data local, secure aggregation can hide individual messages under its protocol assumptions, and an accounted DP mechanism can bound what the released model reveals. A deployment claim must identify which contracts its actual training run satisfies. For this archetype the threat model therefore starts after the raw data remain on the device, with what each device's update reveals and who can observe it.</p>\n<p>Federated learning changes where raw data reside, while differential privacy bounds the effect of a protected contribution on released results. Cryptographic computation addresses a third boundary: who can observe inputs and intermediate values during execution. Homomorphic encryption (HE) and secure multiparty computation (SMPC) support computation over inputs represented as ciphertexts or protocol-specific shares [@gentry2009; @yao1982]. Their costs depend on the chosen protocol and representation. Many HE schemes pack multiple values into one ciphertext so that each operation acts on all of them, and rotations move values between those packed positions. Quantization can help some encrypted computations, but its effect must be assessed together with packing, rotations, nonlinear-function approximations, and required accuracy. Hardware acceleration changes execution cost without removing those mathematical constraints.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Homomorphic encryption</strong>: Operations on ciphertexts correspond to operations on the underlying plaintexts. Gentry's 2009 work supplied the first fully homomorphic construction; it did not make arbitrary ML workloads immediately practical. Inference cost depends on circuit depth, packing, rotations, numerical representation, and the chosen security parameters.</p>\n<p>In the case of HE, evaluating a supported function on a ciphertext produces a ciphertext that decrypts to the corresponding plaintext result: $$ \\HomomorphicCiphertext\\leftarrow \\operatorname{Enc}(\\HomomorphicPublicKey,\\PlaintextInput), \\qquad \\operatorname{Dec}\\!\\left(\\HomomorphicSecretKey,\\operatorname{Eval}(\\HomomorphicPublicKey,\\HomomorphicFunction,\\HomomorphicCiphertext)\\right)=\\HomomorphicFunction(\\PlaintextInput) $$</p>\n<p>This property supports inference over encrypted inputs in an untrusted computing environment. Its confidentiality guarantee depends on the scheme and key custody; it does not by itself prove that the server evaluated the intended function correctly. The deployment must also measure the cost of representing that function as supported encrypted operations, including any approximation that changes prediction quality.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: A team deploying a health-monitoring model compares three security levels:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Plaintext</strong>: Standard inference.</li>\n<li><strong>Encrypted transport (AES)</strong>: Model and data are encrypted at rest and in transit.</li>\n<li><strong>Encrypted compute (fully homomorphic encryption [FHE])</strong>: Inference performed on encrypted data.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Treat the following overheads as assumed scenario inputs, not universal AES or FHE performance ratios. The comparison is whether these configurations satisfy the application's latency budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Inference latency scales by the complexity of the security protocol.</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Plaintext</strong>: <code>[EncryptionOverhead.eo_base_ms_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>AES-256</strong>: <code>[EncryptionOverhead.eo_base_ms_str]</code> + <code>[EncryptionOverhead.eo_aes_overhead_ms_str]</code> = <code>[EncryptionOverhead.eo_aes_ms_str]</code> (negligible overhead).</li>\n<li><strong>FHE</strong>: <code>[EncryptionOverhead.eo_base_ms_str]</code> $\\times$ <code>[EncryptionOverhead.eo_fhe_mult_str]</code> = <code>[EncryptionOverhead.eo_fhe_sec_str]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Under the assumed overheads, protected computation takes longer than encryption around plaintext execution. Whether FHE fits an application depends on its latency budget and the measured encrypted circuit; the multiplier here does not generalize across models or implementations. Hardware isolation provides a different trust boundary in which computation uses protected plaintext. Comparing that option requires its own measured overhead and an explicit hardware trust assumption.</p>\n<p>Homomorphic computation costs depend on the supported operations, circuit depth, numerical representation, and chosen security parameters. Supporting arbitrary computation does not by itself determine the cost of a particular inference circuit. Feasibility requires measuring the protected workload against its latency, throughput, and utility requirements. The hardware-enforced isolation of @sec-security-privacy-trusted-execution-environments-80ed offers a different trade-off: plaintext computation inside a trusted boundary, with security dependent on that boundary's hardware and software assumptions.</p>\n<p>SMPC, by contrast, distributes computation across parties while protecting their private inputs under the protocol's stated corruption and collusion assumptions. In secret-sharing protocols, for example, each input is split into random shares that reveal nothing on their own, each party computes on the shares it holds, and only the recombined shares disclose the result. The protocol specifies which parties receive the output; privacy hides additional information beyond what those outputs and a party's own inputs reveal. This is particularly useful in joint training across institutions with strict data-use policies, such as hospitals or banks.</p>\n<p>: <strong>SMPC performance overhead</strong>: Cryptographic computation adds protocol-specific communication and local work. The bottleneck depends on round count, network latency, arithmetic representation, and the adversary model; a single slowdown factor does not characterize all deployments. Moving some layers outside protection changes the information exposed and therefore requires a new privacy analysis.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-39\" class=\"cs-h4\">De-identification and k-anonymity</h4>\n<p>When an organization releases a dataset instead of computing over encrypted shares, removing names and account numbers is the oldest privacy technique, and on its own it fails. The fields that remain act as quasi-identifiers, which an attacker can join against auxiliary data to single out a person. Linkage of the Netflix Prize dataset showed the failure at scale. Researchers inferred individual movie preferences from records released without names by matching them to public ratings, not by querying a trained model [@narayanan2006break]. The Strava heatmap of @sec-security-privacy-learning-security-breaches-6719 showed the aggregate form of the same failure, in which sparse regions singled out the few people behind them.</p>\n<p>: <strong>Netflix deanonymization</strong>: As few as 8 movie ratings with approximate dates uniquely identified 99 percent of records in the anonymized Netflix Prize dataset, and correlation with public IMDb ratings re-identified users [@narayanan2008]. For ML training data, removing direct identifiers does not establish privacy when retained records can be linked to auxiliary information.</p>\n<p>A release satisfies <strong>$\\Glyph{k}$-anonymity</strong> when every combination of its designated quasi-identifiers occurs in at least $\\AnonymitySetSize$ records, so no record can be singled out from fewer than $\\AnonymitySetSize$ candidates. The property belongs to a released table, not to a single query, so transforming one request does not establish it. It also constrains only the shape of the released table. When every record in a group shares the same sensitive value, or the attacker holds auxiliary knowledge about a person, knowing which group holds that person still discloses the attribute. The property therefore prevents an observer of the release only from singling out a record, and it bounds nothing about what a model trained on the data reveals. Differential privacy (@sec-security-privacy-differential-privacy-8c2b) closes that gap by bounding how much one record changes what is released rather than how the released table looks.</p>\n<p>: <strong>$\\Glyph{k}$-anonymity</strong>: A property of a released dataset [@sweeney2002]. Generalization, such as coarsening dates or postal codes, and suppression of rare rows can achieve the property at the cost of information loss. It does not bound inference from homogeneous sensitive attributes or auxiliary knowledge and is not a per-request privacy guarantee.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-39\" class=\"cs-h4\">Synthetic data generation</h4>\n<p>Synthetic data generation changes the privacy decision from hiding raw records to replacing them with generated surrogates. Generated data can let downstream researchers train models without receiving the source records, but the generator and its outputs must still be evaluated for leakage.</p>\n<p>Synthetic data generation works by training a generative model (such as a GAN, variational autoencoder (VAE), or diffusion model) on the original sensitive dataset, then using this trained generator to produce new artificial samples. The intended result preserves useful population patterns without reproducing individual records. Learning a distribution does not ensure that separation, however; a generator can memorize its training data, so synthetic sampling alone cannot establish privacy.</p>\n<p>Synthetic data generation typically advances through three ordered stages. First, in distribution learning, a generative model $\\SyntheticDataGenerator$ trains on real data $\\RealDataset= \\{\\ModelInput_1, \\ModelInput_2,\\ldots, \\ModelInput_n\\}$ to approximate the underlying data distribution $\\ProbabilityFunction(\\ModelInput)$. Next, synthetic sampling produces new records $\\SyntheticDataset= \\{\\SyntheticDataGenerator(\\GeneratorLatentVector_1), \\SyntheticDataGenerator(\\GeneratorLatentVector_2),\\ldots, \\SyntheticDataGenerator(\\FinalGeneratorLatentVector)\\}$ by sampling latent vectors from random noise $\\GeneratorLatentVector_i \\sim \\GaussianDistribution(\\ZeroVector,\\IdentityMatrix)$. Finally, empirical validation measures selected aspects of fidelity to $\\RealDataset$ and tests for memorization or linkage under the chosen attack model. Sampling can preserve useful statistical properties without distributing the source table [@goncalves2020], but passing those tests does not prove that every training record is protected. A formal release guarantee requires an additional mechanism, and validation that accesses sensitive source data must itself remain within the intended privacy boundary.</p>\n<p>Synthetic data generation still faces both utility and privacy limits. On the utility side, generative models can suffer from mode collapse, failing to capture rare patterns in the original data. On the privacy side, adversaries can extract information about the original training data through generative model inversion or membership inference attacks. Because actual protection depends on the generator's architecture, training procedure, and hyperparameters, synthetic data alone cannot provide a formal privacy guarantee without an additional mechanism such as differential privacy.</p>\n<p>Consider a hospital that wants to share synthetic data for ML research. It trains a GAN on 10,000 patient records containing demographics, lab results, and diagnoses, seeking to preserve useful relationships among those features. The hospital applies differential privacy during training and uses a tested accountant, specifying whether the protected contribution is one record or all records from one patient. Before release, it evaluates clinical fidelity and subgroup utility, since an acceptable overall score can hide poor performance for rare conditions. Memorization tests supply additional empirical evidence, while the formal guarantee comes from the accounted mechanism. DP limits the effect of a protected contribution; it does not prevent learning population patterns or all sensitive inferences based on them.</p>\n<p>Across federated learning, differential privacy, homomorphic encryption, secure multiparty computation, and synthetic data, the privacy-preserving approaches differ in the guarantees they offer and in their system-level implications. Production designs often combine them, for example by applying differential privacy inside a federated setup or by reserving homomorphic encryption for the few inference stages whose inputs justify its cost. The choice of mechanism therefore depends on computational constraints, deployment architecture, and regulatory requirements. Once the formal machinery of differential privacy is in place, these methods can be weighed on privacy guarantee, runtime overhead, operational maturity, and use case fit together rather than as independent properties (@tbl-privacy-technique-comparison).</p>\n<p>The defense stack now covers each tier, from hardware roots of trust through deployment, runtime, and model-design controls to the data path, where federation, secure aggregation, encrypted computation, and synthetic data each move raw records or individual updates away from a particular observer. Data locality, message hiding, encrypted computation, and synthesis alone do not bound what the released model reveals. A secure training cluster and confidential links can still publish a model that exposes memorized examples, as the GPT-2 case showed, because the leak travels through an authorized output rather than an unauthorized access. Closing that gap requires a guarantee stated about the release itself, with the protected contribution, the randomization, and the accumulated loss across releases all made explicit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-39\" class=\"cs-h2\">Differential Privacy</h2>\n<p>Suppose an adversary queries a medical diagnosis model using the exact attributes of a known patient and compares the answers with what a model trained without that patient would return. Encryption and access control cannot help here. The adversary is an authorized caller, and the query reaches only the trained model, never the stored records. A difference in one pair of predictions alone does not prove a privacy violation. The relevant question is how much the distribution of released outcomes permits an adversary to distinguish datasets with and without that patient, relative to a stated privacy bound.</p>\n<p>Differential privacy constrains a randomized training or analysis mechanism so that its output distributions remain close on adjacent datasets. The adjacency definition specifies the protected contribution, such as one record or all records from one person. The information leakage invariant (principle \\ref{pri-information-leakage}) holds that any observable output can carry information about a protected training record, so a privacy claim must name the protected unit and the observations available to an adversary. Differential privacy answers that requirement by bounding the additional evidence a release supplies about the named contribution. For a fixed mechanism and workload, tighter privacy bounds require more noise or fewer data-dependent releases, and the first cost appears as lost utility. Recovering that utility with more data or more training steps raises the training compute, the $\\IronLawComputeTime$ term of the Fleet Law, and per-example gradient clipping in differentially private stochastic gradient descent (DP-SGD) lowers throughput on its own. Whether a tighter bound costs accuracy, compute, or both is therefore a design choice, which places DP in the deployment architecture rather than only in a legal checklist.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Differential privacy</strong></em> is a mathematical guarantee that bounds changes in the output distribution of a randomized algorithm under a specified change to one protected contribution. The neighboring relation defines that contribution and the permitted change. Approximate differential privacy permits a multiplicative bound and additive slack, formalized by the $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ bound $\\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset) \\in \\MechanismOutputSet] \\leq e^{\\PrivacyBudget} \\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset) \\in \\MechanismOutputSet] + \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ for adjacent datasets $\\NeighboringDataset$ and $\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset$ and any measurable output set $\\MechanismOutputSet$ [@dwork2006calibrating; @dwork2014].</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Significance</strong>: The privacy budget $\\PrivacyBudget$ converts an algorithmic property into an engineering currency that can be allocated, composed, and depleted. Production reports and benchmark studies show that privacy budgets are deployment-specific: Apple describes local-DP telemetry deployments with per-event $\\PrivacyBudget$ values chosen by use case, while DP-SGD studies show accuracy and compute trade-offs across benchmark tasks [@apple2017learningprivacy; @abadi2016deep; @bu2020gaussian; @de2022dp]. Smaller $\\PrivacyBudget$ raises the noise floor and degrades classification accuracy along the empirical frontier plotted in @fig-privacy-utility-frontier, where CIFAR-10 suffers double-digit accuracy drops even at $\\PrivacyBudget= 8$. DP-SGD can also reduce training throughput because per-sample gradient clipping works against the batch-level parallelism that makes accelerator training efficient.</li>\n<li><strong>Distinction</strong>: Unlike de-identification, which removes identifiers from records and fails against re-identification through auxiliary data, and unlike statistical disclosure control, which depends on the attacker's model, differential privacy is a property of the algorithm, not of the data. Its guarantee holds against any adversary, including one with arbitrary side information about other records in the dataset.</li>\n<li><strong>Common pitfall</strong>: A frequent misconception is that an $\\PrivacyBudget$ label applies independently to every data access. Sequential composition of $\\ComposedMechanismCount$ DP mechanisms, each with privacy loss $\\PrivacyBudget_0$, yields total loss at most $\\ComposedMechanismCount\\PrivacyBudget_0$ under basic composition, with tighter bounds available from mechanism-specific accountants (@sec-security-privacy-privacy-budget-composition-edbe). A system that runs DP-SGD steps or answers interactive DP queries against sensitive data must therefore track cumulative loss and stop or redesign the release when the allocated budget is exhausted. Rotating cryptographic keys does not reset privacy loss, while querying a fixed DP-trained model is post-processing and incurs no additional training privacy loss.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A randomized mechanism $\\RandomizedMechanism$ run on adjacent datasets $\\NeighboringDataset$ and $\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset$ produces two output densities that overlap without coinciding (@fig-differential-privacy, blue and orange curves). Overlap means that either dataset can produce outcomes in the same region; it does not make the distributions identical. For a discrete outcome $\\MechanismOutcome$ in $\\text{Range}(\\RandomizedMechanism)$, relative likelihood can be expressed as $\\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset)=\\MechanismOutcome] / \\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset)=\\MechanismOutcome]$ when the denominator is positive. Continuous curves instead depict densities, whose ratio is not their vertical difference. The $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ guarantee applies to probabilities of measurable output sets: it bounds one probability by $e^{\\PrivacyBudget}$ times the other plus $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$. For a continuous output, those probabilities are areas under the curves over the selected set. The definition therefore constrains distributional change across all such sets, rather than declaring an overlapping region private or treating additive slack as a pointwise density-ratio allowance.</p>\n<p>A randomized algorithm $\\RandomizedMechanism$ is said to be $\\PrivacyBudget$-differentially private if, for all adjacent datasets $\\NeighboringDataset$ and $\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset$ differing in one record, and for all measurable output sets $\\MechanismOutputSet \\subseteq \\text{Range}(\\RandomizedMechanism)$, the following holds [@dwork2006calibrating; @dwork2014]: $$ \\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset) \\in \\MechanismOutputSet] \\leq e^{\\PrivacyBudget} \\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset) \\in \\MechanismOutputSet] $$</p>\n<p>The parameter $\\PrivacyBudget$ quantifies the privacy budget. In the pure-DP definition above, smaller values of $\\PrivacyBudget$ impose a tighter likelihood-ratio bound; achieving that bound may require more noise or a different mechanism. No universal $\\PrivacyBudget$ value denotes \"strong,\" \"moderate,\" or acceptable privacy: interpretation depends on adjacency, the additive slack $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ in approximate DP, composition, and the consequences of inference. Under pure DP, the multiplicative factor $e^{\\PrivacyBudget}$ bounds ratios of probabilities for the same output event on adjacent datasets. Approximate DP relaxes that event-probability inequality with additive slack, as developed below.</p>\n<p>This bound limits how distinguishable the output distributions can be when a protected contribution changes; it does not make them identical or prevent inference of population-level patterns. Common DP mechanisms add calibrated noise to model updates or query responses, using distributions such as Laplace or Gaussian. Training techniques like DP-SGD combine contribution bounds with calibrated noise and composition accounting. Their guarantee applies to the specified contribution and release, which must be identified before selecting the mechanism.</p>\n<p>: <strong>DP-SGD (differentially private stochastic gradient descent)</strong>: Introduced by @abadi2016deep, DP-SGD clips each per-sample gradient and adds calibrated Gaussian noise. Per-example clipping adds memory, recomputation, and reduction work that ordinary minibatch training avoids; vectorized clipping implementations recover much of the parallelism, so the throughput cost must be measured for the chosen model and implementation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-40\" class=\"cs-h3\">Mathematical foundations and privacy parameters</h3>\n<p>Attaching an $\\PrivacyBudget$ label to a model without stating whether adjacency protects one record or one person, how many training steps composed, and what $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ slack was allowed makes the privacy claim meaningless. Repeated training steps, retraining runs, or queries that access protected data may compose, while post-processing a fixed DP release incurs no additional privacy loss for that data. The mathematical foundations specify the guarantee, noise calibration, and accounting for those data-dependent operations so teams can define a valid accounting boundary before attaching an $\\PrivacyBudget$ value to a deployed release.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-41\" class=\"cs-h4\">The $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-differential privacy formulation</h4>\n<p>The pure $\\PrivacyBudget$-DP definition provides strong guarantees but can be overly restrictive for practical ML applications. The relaxed $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-differential privacy of the definition callout adds slack $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ to the probability bound controlled by $\\PrivacyBudget$, and it is the starting point for noise calibration. Approximate DP is widely used with Gaussian perturbations in deep learning. Its role follows from the probability bound and noise calibration, not from unbounded support alone; Laplace noise also has unbounded support.</p>\n<p>Formally, a randomized algorithm $\\RandomizedMechanism$ satisfies $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-differential privacy if for all adjacent datasets $\\NeighboringDataset, \\AdjacentNeighboringDataset$ and all measurable output sets $\\MechanismOutputSet$: $$ \\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset) \\in \\MechanismOutputSet] \\leq e^{\\PrivacyBudget} \\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset) \\in \\MechanismOutputSet] + \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$$</p>\n<p>The parameter $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ is additive slack in this event-probability inequality, not generally the probability of a catastrophic disclosure. In practice, $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ is commonly chosen smaller than the inverse dataset size, often $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack&lt; 1/\\DatasetSize$ or smaller depending on policy and threat model; smaller $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ tightens the guarantee at additional utility cost. For a dataset with 1 million records, $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack= 10^{-12}$ specifies a small additive slack, but does not by itself assign a probability to every possible privacy failure.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-41\" class=\"cs-h4\">Noise mechanisms for achieving differential privacy</h4>\n<p>ML systems implement differential privacy through carefully calibrated noise injection. The two primary mechanisms used in ML systems differ in their noise distributions and applicability. The first is the Laplace mechanism. For pure $\\PrivacyBudget$-DP, it adds noise drawn from $\\text{Lap}(\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery/\\PrivacyBudget)$, where $\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery$ is the global sensitivity of the function (the maximum change in output when one record changes). For a query $\\DatasetQuery$, the privatized output is: $$ \\PrivatizedQuery(\\NeighboringDataset) = \\DatasetQuery(\\NeighboringDataset) + \\text{Lap}\\left(\\frac{\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery}{\\PrivacyBudget}\\right) $$</p>\n<p>The Laplace distribution has density $\\ProbabilityFunction(\\LaplaceNoiseSample) = \\frac{\\PrivacyBudget}{2\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{\\PrivacyBudget|\\LaplaceNoiseSample|}{\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery}\\right)$, with scale parameter $\\LaplaceNoiseScale= \\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery/\\PrivacyBudget$. For a fixed query and adjacency relation, tightening the bound from $\\PrivacyBudget= 1.0$ to $\\PrivacyBudget= 0.1$ increases the noise scale by 10$\\times$. Individual noise draws remain random; the scale controls their distribution rather than fixing their magnitude.</p>\n<p>The Laplace derivation shows why the noise scale must match sensitivity. To prove that the Laplace mechanism achieves $\\PrivacyBudget$-DP, consider the ratio of output densities on adjacent datasets $\\NeighboringDataset$ and $\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset$. Let $\\DatasetQuery(\\NeighboringDataset) = \\DatasetQueryResult$ and $\\DatasetQuery(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset) = \\AdjacentQueryResult$, where $|\\DatasetQueryResult- \\AdjacentQueryResult| \\leq \\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery$ by the sensitivity bound. For any output $\\MechanismOutcome$, the density ratio is: $$ \\frac{\\ProbabilityFunction(\\MechanismOutcome | \\NeighboringDataset)}{\\ProbabilityFunction(\\MechanismOutcome | \\AdjacentNeighboringDataset)} = \\frac{\\exp(-\\PrivacyBudget|\\MechanismOutcome - \\DatasetQueryResult|/\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery)}{\\exp(-\\PrivacyBudget|\\MechanismOutcome - \\AdjacentQueryResult|/\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery)} = \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\PrivacyBudget(|\\MechanismOutcome - \\AdjacentQueryResult| - |\\MechanismOutcome - \\DatasetQueryResult|)}{\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery}\\right) $$</p>\n<p>Using the triangle inequality, $|\\MechanismOutcome - \\AdjacentQueryResult| - |\\MechanismOutcome - \\DatasetQueryResult| \\leq |\\DatasetQueryResult- \\AdjacentQueryResult| \\leq \\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery$. Therefore: $$ \\frac{\\ProbabilityFunction(\\MechanismOutcome | \\NeighboringDataset)}{\\ProbabilityFunction(\\MechanismOutcome | \\AdjacentNeighboringDataset)} \\leq \\exp\\left(\\frac{\\PrivacyBudget\\cdot \\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery}{\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery}\\right) = e^{\\PrivacyBudget} $$</p>\n<p>This establishes that the Laplace mechanism satisfies $\\PrivacyBudget$-differential privacy. The derivation reveals why sensitivity calibration is necessary, because the noise scale must match the maximum possible change in the query output to mask individual contributions.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Consider computing the average salary of <code>[DPCostAnalysis.n_employees_str]</code> employees while guaranteeing privacy budget $\\PrivacyBudget=$ <code>[DPCostAnalysis.epsilon_str]</code>. The salaries range from \\$0 to <code>[DPCostAnalysis.sensitivity_usd_str]</code>. Assume a fixed, public employee roster and neighboring datasets that differ in one employee's salary, with both values inside these bounds. How much noise must the mechanism add?</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>:</p>\n<ol class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>Sensitivity</strong> $(\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery)$: The maximum one person can change the sum is <code>[DPCostAnalysis.sensitivity_usd_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Privacy Budget</strong> $(\\PrivacyBudget)$: <code>[DPCostAnalysis.epsilon_str]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Laplace Noise Scale</strong> $(\\LaplaceNoiseScale)$: the Laplace mechanism sets its noise scale to the sensitivity divided by the privacy budget, so $\\LaplaceNoiseScale= \\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery/ \\PrivacyBudget=$ <code>[DPCostAnalysis.sensitivity_usd_str]</code> / <code>[DPCostAnalysis.epsilon_str]</code> = <code>[DPCostAnalysis.noise_scale_usd_str]</code>.</li>\n<li>Impact on Mean: The mean-estimation noise comes from noise added to the sum with magnitude approximately <code>[DPCostAnalysis.noise_scale_usd_str]</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Error in the mean = <code>[DPCostAnalysis.noise_scale_usd_str]</code> / <code>[DPCostAnalysis.n_employees_str]</code> = <code>[DPCostAnalysis.error_in_mean_usd_str]</code>. The mechanism draws noise once, for the sum; dividing by the group size spreads that single draw across the mean.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: Under this fixed-roster contract, the published mean receives zero-mean Laplace noise with expected absolute magnitude <code>[DPCostAnalysis.error_in_mean_usd_str]</code>; individual draws can be smaller or larger. For a dataset of $\\DatasetSize=$ <code>[DPCostAnalysis.n_small_str]</code>, the expected absolute error grows to <code>[DPCostAnalysis.error_small_usd_str]</code>. The noise needed to hide one person is fixed by the sensitivity, while the signal grows with the number of people, so small groups pay the largest relative cost and large datasets dilute it through the factor $1/\\DatasetSize$.</p>\n<p>The salary-average notebook above uses the central model of differential privacy. Each person supplies a bounded salary to a trusted analyst; the analyst computes the sum, adds one Laplace draw calibrated to the sum's global sensitivity, and divides by the fixed, public group size. The example protects a salary replacement within a public roster. If participation itself is private, adjacency must be add/remove, meaning adjacent datasets differ by the presence or absence of one person's records rather than by a change to one value. Under that relation the count becomes data dependent and needs its own privacy treatment, so division by that count is not automatically safe post-processing. This differs from local differential privacy, in which each person randomizes a value before submission and the analyst aggregates the randomized reports. Both models compare output distributions on adjacent inputs, but their mechanisms, trust assumptions, and utility are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p>The Gaussian mechanism serves the ML case where the relaxed $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ guarantee is acceptable. It adds noise with standard deviation $\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation$ calibrated based on $\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$, and the $\\ell_2$ sensitivity $\\QuerySensitivity_2 \\DatasetQuery$: $$ \\PrivatizedQuery(\\NeighboringDataset) = \\DatasetQuery(\\NeighboringDataset) + \\GaussianDistribution\\left(\\ZeroVector, \\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation^2 \\IdentityMatrix\\right) $$</p>\n<p>Unlike the Laplace mechanism, which achieves pure $\\PrivacyBudget$-DP, the Gaussian mechanism generally requires the relaxed $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-DP formulation because shifted Gaussian densities can have arbitrarily large likelihood ratios in the tails. Bounding those tail events gives a sufficient way to establish the $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ slack. The derivation works with the privacy loss, the log of the ratio between the two output densities at the observed output. Because the output is random, so is this log ratio, which is why it is called the privacy loss random variable.</p>\n<p>For adjacent datasets with $\\ell_2$ sensitivity $\\QuerySensitivity_2 \\DatasetQuery$, the privacy loss at output $\\MechanismOutputVector$ follows: $$ \\PrivacyLossVariable(\\MechanismOutputVector) = \\ln\\frac{\\ProbabilityFunction(\\MechanismOutputVector|\\NeighboringDataset)}{\\ProbabilityFunction(\\MechanismOutputVector|\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset)} = \\frac{\\|\\MechanismOutputVector- \\DatasetQuery(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset)\\|_2^2 - \\|\\MechanismOutputVector- \\DatasetQuery(\\NeighboringDataset)\\|_2^2}{2\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation^2} $$</p>\n<p>When $\\MechanismOutputVector= \\DatasetQuery(\\NeighboringDataset) + \\GaussianMechanismNoiseVector$ for noise $\\GaussianMechanismNoiseVector\\sim \\GaussianDistribution(\\ZeroVector, \\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation^2 \\IdentityMatrix)$, expanding the difference of squared norms cancels the quadratic term $\\|\\GaussianMechanismNoiseVector\\|_2^2$ and leaves a constant shift plus a linear inner product between $\\GaussianMechanismNoiseVector$ and the dataset difference $\\DatasetQuery(\\NeighboringDataset) - \\DatasetQuery(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset)$. Unlike the Laplace log ratio, which the triangle inequality caps at a constant across the entire output space, this linear function of $\\GaussianMechanismNoiseVector$ grows without bound along the direction of the dataset difference. For an adjacent pair attaining the sensitivity bound, the privacy loss is therefore a shifted Gaussian random variable with mean $\\frac{\\QuerySensitivity_2 \\DatasetQuery^2}{2\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation^2}$ and variance $\\frac{\\QuerySensitivity_2 \\DatasetQuery^2}{\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation^2}$, and $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ bounds the probability that a noise draw lands far enough in the tail to push $\\PrivacyLossVariable(\\MechanismOutputVector)$ above $\\PrivacyBudget$. For one application of the mechanism and $0&lt;\\PrivacyBudget&lt;1$, a classic sufficient calibration is: $$ \\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation\\geq \\frac{\\QuerySensitivity_2 \\DatasetQuery}{\\PrivacyBudget}\\sqrt{2\\ln\\left(\\frac{1.25}{\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack}\\right)} $$</p>\n<p>This sufficient bound is a one-shot Gaussian-mechanism result within that parameter domain. Neither it nor the one-shot Laplace ratio $\\QuerySensitivity \\DatasetQuery/\\PrivacyBudget$ of the salary example can set a DP-SGD noise multiplier, the ratio of the noise standard deviation to the clipping norm, by substituting the final training budget. DP-SGD clips sampled per-example gradients, adds Gaussian noise to their sum, and composes many such steps, so its budget requires a sampled-Gaussian accountant. Smaller $\\PrivacyBudget$ or $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$ generally requires more noise, but the resulting accuracy change must be measured for the specific task and training procedure.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem</strong>: Determine a sufficient noise standard deviation $\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation$ for the Gaussian mechanism to achieve $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-differential privacy for a single vector-valued query.</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong>: $\\ell_2$ sensitivity $\\QuerySensitivity_2 \\DatasetQuery=$ <code>[GaussianNoiseScale.sensitivity_str]</code>, target $\\PrivacyBudget=$ <code>[GaussianNoiseScale.epsilon_str]</code>, $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack=$ <code>[GaussianNoiseScale.delta_math]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Math</strong>: Classic sufficient bound requires $\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation\\geq$ <code>[GaussianNoiseScale.sigma_min_str]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Systems insight</strong>: This calculation applies to a single Gaussian release. DP-SGD repeats a Gaussian release at every optimization step, so its budget must be composed across thousands of steps, and the accountants of @sec-security-privacy-privacy-loss-random-variable-moments-accountant-cf17 bound that composed total more tightly than adding up the per-step bounds would.</p>\n<p>Gaussian noise fits the clipped-gradient mechanism because clipping explicitly bounds gradient norms in $\\ell_2$ space, making sensitivity analysis tractable. Rényi differential privacy accounting can retain mechanism-specific information to improve on generic composition bounds; that advantage belongs to mechanisms like clipped-gradient training and does not make Gaussian noise the right choice for every private computation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-41\" class=\"cs-h3\">Privacy budget composition</h3>\n<p>Releasing 10 models trained on the same patient cohort at $\\PrivacyBudget=1$ each does not maintain $\\PrivacyBudget=1$ protection; under basic composition the cumulative bound reaches $\\PrivacyBudget=10$. Because a full training run or a sequence of model releases applies a mechanism many times, privacy loss accumulates across every additional data-dependent DP release about the same protected population. Basic composition bounds the total by adding the per-release bounds, which is valid but often loose, while mechanism-specific accountants retain more information about each mechanism to obtain tighter bounds across noisy stochastic gradient descent (SGD) steps before expressing the result as an $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ budget. Repeated use of a fixed released DP model is post-processing and does not add to its training privacy loss, because a computation that reads only the release, never the protected data, cannot weaken the guarantee the release already satisfies.</p>\n<p>Privacy accounting depends on three primary composition tools. Simple composition bounds the cumulative privacy loss of $\\ComposedMechanismCount$ pure-DP mechanisms with bounds $\\PrivacyBudget_i$ by $\\sum \\PrivacyBudget_i$; for approximate DP, their additive slack parameters also accumulate. In suitable parameter regimes, advanced (strong) composition improves the bound using square-root and linear terms while allocating additional slack in the probability inequality. For iterative mechanisms like DP-SGD, the moments accountant and Rényi differential privacy developed next track the loss across steps before converting it to an explicit $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ guarantee, and no accountant is uniformly tighter than every alternative. Organizations must maintain a privacy accounting log for the protected population and halt or redesign further data-dependent releases when the allocated bound would be exceeded.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-42\" class=\"cs-h4\">Privacy loss random variable and moments accountant</h4>\n<p>A more refined approach to privacy analysis tracks the privacy loss random variable, which measures how strongly an observation favors one adjacent dataset over the other through a log-likelihood ratio. For adjacent datasets $\\NeighboringDataset, \\AdjacentNeighboringDataset$ and algorithm output $\\MechanismOutcome$, the discrete-output expression is: $$ \\OutcomePairPrivacyLoss = \\ln\\frac{\\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset) = \\MechanismOutcome]}{\\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset) = \\MechanismOutcome]} $$</p>\n<p>The privacy loss random variable characterizes the log-likelihood ratio between outputs on adjacent datasets; for continuous outputs, the ratio uses densities rather than point probabilities. A sufficient condition for $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-DP is that every ordered adjacent pair satisfies $\\Pr[\\PrivacyLossVariable&gt; \\PrivacyBudget] \\leq \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$, with the output sampled from the numerator distribution. This tail condition is stronger than the general definition, but it enables composition analysis through moment-generating functions.</p>\n<p>The moments accountant technique, introduced for DP-SGD by @abadi2016deep, bounds exponential moments of the privacy loss distribution. It retains more information about the mechanism than a single per-step privacy bound while still taking the worst case over adjacent datasets. For mechanism $\\RandomizedMechanism$ with privacy loss $\\PrivacyLossVariable$, the moments accountant computes: $$ \\MechanismLogMoment(\\MomentOrder) = \\max_{\\NeighboringDataset,\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset} \\ln \\mathbb{E}_{\\MechanismOutcome\\sim \\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset)}\\left[\\left(\\frac{\\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset) = \\MechanismOutcome]}{\\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset) = \\MechanismOutcome]}\\right)^{\\MomentOrder}\\right] $$</p>\n<p>for moment order $\\MomentOrder$. Because the total privacy loss across steps with fresh noise draws is the sum of the per-step privacy losses, its exponential moment factors into the product of the per-step moments, and its logarithm adds across steps. If each of $\\ComposedMechanismCount$ steps satisfies the same log-moment bound, composition gives an upper bound of $\\ComposedMechanismCount\\cdot \\MechanismLogMoment(\\MomentOrder)$. Instead of converting every step into a separate tail cutoff and summing those cutoffs, the accountant accumulates the log-moments first and applies the Markov inequality once at the end to yield $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ bounds: $$ \\PrivacyBudget(\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack) = \\min_{\\MomentOrder&gt;0} \\left[\\frac{\\ComposedMechanismCount\\cdot \\MechanismLogMoment(\\MomentOrder) + \\ln(1/\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)}{\\MomentOrder}\\right] $$</p>\n<p>The numerical result depends on the sampling rule, sampling rate, noise multiplier, step count, adjacency relation, and accountant. In DP-SGD, random subsampling at rate $\\LotSamplingProbability$ also shrinks $\\MechanismLogMoment(\\MomentOrder)$ at each step because a given record falls, on average, into only a $\\LotSamplingProbability$ fraction of lots, the randomly sampled groups of examples that DP-SGD processes at each step. For example, @abadi2016deep report that with sampling rate $\\LotSamplingProbability=0.01$, noise multiplier $\\PrivacyNoiseMultiplier=4$, and $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack=10^{-5}$, their moments accountant gives $\\PrivacyBudget=1.26$ after 100 epochs and $\\PrivacyBudget=2.55$ after 400 epochs, whereas the corresponding advanced-composition bounds are 9.34 and 24.22. These values belong to that experiment and are not reusable constants for another DP-SGD run.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-42\" class=\"cs-h4\">Renyi differential privacy and composition</h4>\n<p>Rényi differential privacy (RDP), introduced by @mironov2017, provides a convenient representation for composition by tracking Rényi divergence. A mechanism $\\RandomizedMechanism$ satisfies $(\\RenyiOrder, \\RenyiPrivacyBudget)\\text{-RDP}$ if for all adjacent $\\NeighboringDataset, \\AdjacentNeighboringDataset$: $$ \\RenyiDivergence(\\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset) \\lVert \\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset)) = \\frac{1}{\\RenyiOrder-1}\\ln \\mathbb{E}_{\\MechanismOutcome \\sim \\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset)}\\left[\\left(\\frac{\\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\NeighboringDataset) = \\MechanismOutcome]}{\\Pr[\\RandomizedMechanism(\\AdjacentNeighboringDataset) = \\MechanismOutcome]}\\right)^{\\RenyiOrder}\\right] \\leq \\RenyiPrivacyBudget$$</p>\n<p>where $\\RenyiOrder&gt; 1$ is the Rényi order. RDP composition is simple. If mechanism $\\RandomizedMechanism_i$ satisfies $(\\RenyiOrder, \\MechanismRenyiPrivacyBudget)\\text{-RDP}$, then their composition satisfies $(\\RenyiOrder, \\sum_i \\MechanismRenyiPrivacyBudget)\\text{-RDP}$. This representation avoids repeatedly applying a generic $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ composition theorem to every training step.</p>\n<p>For an unsampled Gaussian mechanism with unit $\\ell_2$ sensitivity and noise standard deviation $\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation$, the RDP guarantee is: $$ \\RenyiPrivacyBudget(\\RenyiOrder) = \\frac{\\RenyiOrder}{2\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation^2} $$</p>\n<p>Subsampling changes this mechanism. In particular, $\\LotSamplingProbability^2\\RenyiOrder/(2\\GaussianMechanismStandardDeviation^2)$ is only a leading-order approximation in restricted regimes, not a privacy guarantee that can certify a DP-SGD run. A sampled-Gaussian accountant must evaluate the specified sampling scheme and adjacency relation at multiple Rényi orders, compose the resulting per-step bounds across the actual number of steps, and then convert the result to $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-DP. Production implementations should use a tested accountant such as Google DP Accounting, TensorFlow Privacy, or Opacus and record its version and sampling convention with the released model.</p>\n<p>This RDP guarantee supplies a valid $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-DP bound using the following conversion; the result is an upper bound on privacy loss, not generally its exact value: $$ \\PrivacyBudget= \\RenyiPrivacyBudget+ \\frac{\\ln(1/\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)}{\\RenyiOrder- 1} $$</p>\n<p>optimized over $\\RenyiOrder$. The moments-accountant and RDP views are closely related; which implementation gives the tightest reported bound depends on the mechanism, orders, and numerical accountant rather than a universal multiplier.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-42\" class=\"cs-h4\">DP-SGD accountant inputs for image classification</h4>\n<p>Consider training a CNN on <code>[PracticalDPBudget.n_records_str]</code> CIFAR-10 images with $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack) = ($ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.eps_target_str]</code> $, 10^{-5})$ target privacy. DP-SGD groups examples into <strong>lots</strong>, the randomly sampled sets whose clipped gradients are summed and share one noise draw per step; with Poisson sampling the lot size varies from step to step around its expected value. The run uses DP-SGD with:</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>Poisson sampling with expected lot size $\\BatchSize=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.batch_size_str]</code> (inclusion probability $\\LotSamplingProbability= \\BatchSize/\\DatasetSize =$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.q_str]</code>)</li>\n<li>Per-example gradient clipping norm $\\GradientClippingNorm=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.clip_norm_str]</code></li>\n<li>Noise multiplier $\\PrivacyNoiseMultiplier=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.sigma_str]</code> (noise scale relative to clipping)</li>\n<li>Training for $\\EpochCount=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.n_epochs_str]</code> epochs ($\\ComposedMechanismCount= \\EpochCount\\cdot \\DatasetSize/\\BatchSize=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.k_steps_str]</code> steps)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These inputs do not determine a privacy guarantee through the shortcut $\\LotSamplingProbability^2\\RenyiOrder/(2\\PrivacyNoiseMultiplier^2)$. Before release, a tested sampled-Gaussian accountant must evaluate Poisson sampling with add/remove adjacency, compose all <code>[PracticalDPBudget.k_steps_str]</code> steps, report the $\\PrivacyBudget$ it certifies at the specified $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$, and verify whether that value meets target $\\PrivacyBudget=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.eps_target_str]</code>. The release record must pin the accountant library and version, sampling convention, adjacency relation, and evaluated Rényi orders.</p>\n<p>The adjacency relation also decides what the bound protects. Per-example clipping bounds the contribution of one record, so when one person contributes many records, as a repeat customer does to a review corpus, a record-level guarantee does not protect that person as a unit. Person-level protection requires bounding each person's total contribution and accounting under person-level adjacency.</p>\n<p>Training for $\\EpochCount=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.alt_epochs_str]</code> reduces the number of composed steps, while reducing the expected lot size to $\\BatchSize=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.alt_batch_size_str]</code> changes the Poisson inclusion probability to $\\LotSamplingProbability=$ <code>[PracticalDPBudget.alt_q_str]</code>. The accountant must be rerun for either change; neither direction should be assigned a numerical $\\PrivacyBudget$ through the leading-order shortcut. If the accountant reports a budget above the target, the operator raises the noise multiplier, trains for fewer epochs, or lowers the sampling rate, and then reruns it. Each change has a utility and compute cost that must be measured. The guarantee itself comes from clipping, calibrated noise, and the privacy accountant; @lst-security-privacy-dp-sgd-step shows the mechanism sequence without implementing an accountant.</p>\n<pre class=\"cs-code\"><code>fix a public normalization B for the expected lot size under the sampling design\ncheck prospectively that the accountant permits the next step within the budget\nindependently sample each record with probability q (Poisson sampling)\nclip each sampled per-example gradient to norm C\nsum clipped gradients; use the zero vector for an empty lot\nadd noise N(0, sigma^2 C^2 I), then divide by fixed B\ntake one optimizer step with the privatized gradient\nrecord the step in the matching privacy accountant\nstop before a step that would exceed the release budget\naccount for private-data-dependent selection; retuning does not erase prior releases</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"sec-42\" class=\"cs-h3\">Quantifying the privacy-utility trade-off</h3>\n<p>Measuring the accuracy cost of those clipping and noise adjustments across benchmarks reveals that the penalty scales sharply with task difficulty. Tightening differential privacy to single-digit $\\PrivacyBudget$ keeps MNIST within <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.mnist_gap_max_str]</code> percentage points of its <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.mnist_base_str]</code> percent nonprivate baseline, yet drops CIFAR-10 trained from scratch by roughly <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_gap_lo_str]</code> to <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_gap_hi_str]</code> percentage points even at $\\PrivacyBudget =$ <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_eps_str]</code> [@abadi2016deep; @bu2020gaussian; @de2022dp]. That gap shows how strongly the utility cost of differential privacy depends on task complexity, architecture, privacy accountant, pretraining, and whether extra public data are allowed.</p>\n<p>@Fig-privacy-utility-frontier collects those published MNIST and CIFAR-10 results on a logarithmic axis [@abadi2016deep; @bu2020gaussian; @de2022dp]. These comparisons show setting-specific utility costs; differences in architectures and training procedures prevent isolating task complexity alone. The plotted MNIST results stay within <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.mnist_gap_max_str]</code> percentage points of the <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.mnist_base_str]</code> percent nonprivate baseline at every plotted single-digit budget, the smallest of which is $\\PrivacyBudget =$ <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.single_digit_eps_min_str]</code>. For CIFAR-10 without extra data, the plotted results reach <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_abadi_str]</code> percent in @abadi2016deep and <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_de_str]</code> percent in @de2022dp at $\\PrivacyBudget=$ <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_eps_str]</code>, roughly <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_gap_lo_str]</code> to <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_gap_hi_str]</code> points below the displayed <code>[PrivacyUtilityGaps.cifar_base_str]</code> percent nonprivate reference. These CIFAR-10 settings therefore pay several times the MNIST accuracy cost at a budget four times looser. That reference is a comparison point, not a ceiling on achievable accuracy. Selecting a budget therefore requires the full training regime, adjacency relation, additive slack, accountant, and utility requirements rather than the horizontal coordinate alone.</p>\n<p>These empirical results show why a privacy budget alone does not predict utility. The original DP-SGD experiments achieved higher accuracy on MNIST than on CIFAR-10, while later training methods improved the CIFAR-10 result under the same budget. Public pretraining changes the learning problem again. With an NFNet-F3 pretrained on JFT-4B, @de2022dp report 86.7 percent ImageNet accuracy at $(8, 8 \\times 10^{-7})$-DP, so comparisons must state which data receive protection and which data support pretraining. The figure supplies selected operating points rather than a complete privacy-accuracy curve. A deployment needs its own sweep over $\\PrivacyBudget$ with the other privacy assumptions and acceptance criteria held explicit.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-43\" class=\"cs-h3\">Decision framework for differential privacy deployment trade-offs</h3>\n<p>Committing to $\\PrivacyBudget = 1$ before checking rare-subgroup accuracy or 8-GPU training budgets can leave a clinical classifier too inaccurate or too expensive to ship. The quantified trade-offs turn differential privacy from a mathematical guarantee into a deployment decision: specify the required protection first, then test whether a mechanism can deliver it at acceptable utility and systems cost. A mandatory privacy bound is a feasibility constraint; an accuracy improvement does not by itself justify exceeding it.</p>\n<p>Start with the regulatory and legal requirement. DP becomes especially valuable when policy, contracts, audits, or risk assessments require demonstrable privacy bounds. Regulations rarely mandate DP by name, but GDPR's data-protection-by-design principle [@gdpr2016] and privacy-by-design framing [@cavoukian2012] motivate privacy-preserving system design. HIPAA's technical safeguards, in turn, require controls such as access control, audit controls, integrity protections, authentication, and transmission security [@hhs2005hipaasecurity]. DP can complement those controls by documenting a mathematical privacy-loss bound, but it does not replace HIPAA safeguard implementation. Organizations handling EU health data, US medical records, or California personal data subject to California state privacy laws (CCPA/CPRA) should evaluate DP as one possible compliance-supporting mechanism.</p>\n<p>Next, match the threat model to the mechanism. DP bounds how much any one record can change the released distribution, even when an adversary chooses queries adaptively and combines outputs with auxiliary information. This property can limit membership inference and record-level extraction, but only under the stated adjacency relation and composed budget. When the primary threats are data breaches of stored data rather than inference from a release, encryption and access controls address the relevant boundary; DP does not substitute for them.</p>\n<p>The dataset and task then determine whether the guarantee is usable. Effective DP training depends on whether the clipped signal remains learnable after adding calibrated noise. Effective sample size, the number of independent examples that actually inform learning for a class or subgroup, captures why repeated observations, class imbalance, and subgroup rarity can limit learning even in a large table. Task complexity also matters, but class count alone does not determine noise tolerance; representation, label quality, and the training regime affect the result. The data sensitivity distribution raises a separate question about whose errors carry the greatest consequences; sensitivity here means the cost of an error, not the mathematical sensitivity used to calibrate noise. Rare subgroups may suffer disproportionate utility loss even when the same formal privacy bound covers every protected contribution. As the salary notebook showed, the noise that hides one contribution is the same for every group, while a small group supplies little signal to outweigh it.</p>\n<p>The utility budget must also be explicit. Before deployment, the team must define task-specific acceptance criteria for overall performance, rare subgroups, calibration, and safety-relevant errors. An average accuracy change that appears small can still be unacceptable if DP noise concentrates errors on a protected subgroup or a high-cost clinical class. Published results supply comparison points on the privacy-utility frontier (@fig-privacy-utility-frontier), but the release decision requires an experiment on the actual model and data.</p>\n<p>Finally, the computational budget decides whether the design can ship. DP-SGD adds clipping, noise generation, and accounting work, while attaining acceptable private utility can change the batch size and training duration. The resulting runtime and memory cost depends on the model and implementation. Deployment planning needs measured time to the required utility under the privacy budget, rather than a universal compute multiplier.</p>\n<h4 id=\"sec-44\" class=\"cs-h4\">Decision matrix summary</h4>\n<p>Use DP when a formal participation or contribution bound addresses the stated threat model, a tested accountant covers the relevant data-dependent releases, measured utility satisfies the task's acceptance criteria, and the training system can afford the mechanism's compute and memory cost. Use different controls when the primary boundary is unauthorized access to stored data. If a required DP bound cannot coexist with acceptable utility, redesign the mechanism, task, or data strategy, or withhold the release. Federated learning, encryption, access control, and audit logging do not substitute for a required release guarantee merely because they are easier to deploy.</p>\n<p>A wearable cardiac monitor classifies arrhythmias from raw ECG signals collected on-device for 5,000 enrolled patients. The product is sold in the United States and is HIPAA-regulated; the training pipeline runs on a cloud cluster of 8 GPUs, and a competing vendor has incentive to extract the model. Apply each of the five decision criteria to determine whether DP-SGD belongs in the training pipeline.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Criterion 1 (regulatory)</strong>: decide whether HIPAA supports a DP guarantee here and identify the documentation DP supplies that ad-hoc de-identification cannot.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Criterion 2 (threat model)</strong>: identify the adversary populations that matter for a cardiac-monitor model and evaluate membership inference as a credible attack.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Criterion 3 (dataset characteristics)</strong>: evaluate effective sample size, class imbalance, repeated measurements, and rare clinical subgroups before selecting a mechanism.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Criterion 4 (acceptable utility loss)</strong>: define safety-critical error, calibration, and subgroup acceptance criteria, then measure whether the private model satisfies them.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Criterion 5 (compute)</strong>: estimate the runtime and memory cost of private training on the stated cloud allocation, measure utility under the selected privacy budget, and identify the binding deployment constraint.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This framework treats DP as one tool in a privacy-preserving toolkit rather than a universal solution. Production deployments often combine an accounted DP mechanism with data minimization, federated execution where raw-data centralization is out of scope, secure aggregation for contribution confidentiality, and access controls for deployment security.</p>\n<p>Returning to the data-layer techniques of @sec-security-privacy-privacypreserving-data-techniques-64f8, differential privacy sits beside federated learning, homomorphic encryption, secure multiparty computation, and synthetic data generation, and each strikes its own balance among privacy strength, accuracy, and computational cost (@tbl-privacy-technique-comparison). The maturity labels are a deployment snapshot for the regimes discussed here, not a permanent ranking of the underlying techniques. Cryptographic guarantees depend on key custody and the stated adversary model; they do not prevent an authorized output from revealing sensitive information. Differential privacy instead bounds disclosure through the released result under a specified adjacency relation.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Technique</strong></th><th><strong>Privacy guarantee</strong></th><th><strong>Computational overhead</strong></th><th><strong>Deployment snapshot</strong></th><th><strong>Typical use case</strong></th><th><strong>Trade-offs</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Differential Privacy</strong></td><td>Formal $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$-DP</td><td>Moderate to High</td><td>Production</td><td>Training with sensitive or regulated data</td><td>Reduced accuracy; careful tuning of $\\PrivacyBudget$/noise required to balance utility and protection</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Federated Learning</strong></td><td>Local data retention only</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Production</td><td>Cross-device or cross-org collaborative learning</td><td>Gradient leakage risk; requires secure aggregation and orchestration infrastructure</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Homomorphic Encryption</strong></td><td>Ciphertext confidentiality</td><td>High</td><td>Experimental</td><td>Inference in untrusted cloud environments</td><td>High latency and memory usage; suitable for limited-scope inference on fixed-function models</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Secure MPC</strong></td><td>Protocol-dependent input privacy</td><td>Very High</td><td>Experimental</td><td>Joint training across mutually untrusted parties</td><td>Expensive communication; challenging to scale to many participants or deep models</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Synthetic Data</strong></td><td>None from synthesis alone</td><td>Low to Moderate</td><td>Emerging</td><td>Data sharing, benchmarking without direct access to raw data</td><td>May leak sensitive patterns if training process is not differentially private or audited for fidelity</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Privacy-Preserving Technique Comparison</strong>: Data privacy techniques impose varying computational costs and offer different levels of formal privacy guarantees, requiring practitioners to balance privacy strength with model utility and deployment constraints. Weighing privacy guarantees, computational overhead, maturity, typical use cases, and trade-offs together guides the design of privacy-aware machine learning systems. {#tbl-privacy-technique-comparison}</p>\n<p>Because increasing the noise to tighten that $\\PrivacyBudget$ release bound may degrade model accuracy in low-data regimes or fine-grained classification tasks, DP is often applied selectively (either during training on sensitive datasets or at inference when returning aggregate statistics) to balance privacy with performance goals [@dwork2014].</p>\n<p>Differential privacy completes the data layer of the defense stack. With the mechanisms of every layer now in view, the remaining engineering questions are which of them a given deployment needs and in what order to adopt them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-44\" class=\"cs-h2\">Selecting and Sequencing Defenses</h2>\n<p>Moving from mathematical guarantees to production controls requires more than setting an epsilon value. A common mistake is attempting to deploy every defense at once, including differential privacy, trusted execution environments, adversarial training, model watermarking, and red-team automation, while basic controls such as access boundaries and encrypted transport remain weak. Two decisions prevent that mistake. The first selects the defenses a deployment context requires; the second sequences them so that each control rests on the ones beneath it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-45\" class=\"cs-h3\">Defense selection by deployment context</h3>\n<p>A hospital training a federated diagnostic model on patient records and a public-facing customer chatbot face completely different attackers, latency budgets, and legal mandates. The hardware mechanisms of @tbl-hw-security-comparison, the deployment and runtime controls, and the cryptographic and statistical privacy techniques of the data layer therefore form a menu rather than a fixed recipe. The appropriate defense for any given deployment depends on three interacting factors: the threat model (who attacks and with what capabilities), the deployment context (what computational and latency budgets exist), and the regulatory environment (what legal mandates constrain design). A defense selection framework maps seven common deployment contexts to their primary threats, recommended defense combinations, and the trade-offs each combination requires evaluating (@tbl-defense-selection-framework). The rows that include differential privacy require an accounted mechanism, built from the sensitivity and composition machinery of @sec-security-privacy-differential-privacy-8c2b, rather than a universal privacy-budget value.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Deployment context</strong></th><th><strong>Primary threats</strong></th><th><strong>Recommended defenses</strong></th><th><strong>Key trade-offs</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>Healthcare ML</strong> (federated diagnostic models)</td><td>Data leakage (HIPAA violation); membership inference; unauthorized access</td><td>Accounted differential privacy for training when the threat model requires it; federated learning across hospitals; TEE for inference on sensitive data; audit logging and RBAC</td><td>Privacy and TEE parameters require task-specific utility and latency measurement</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Financial ML</strong> (fraud detection API)</td><td>Model theft (IP loss); adversarial evasion; data poisoning</td><td>Model encryption (AES-256) at rest; HSM for cryptographic key management; adversarial training on perturbed transactions; input validation and per-principal rate limiting; output confidence monitoring</td><td>HSM units cost roughly <code>[HsmGpuCrypto.hsm_cost_low_str]</code>--<code>[HsmGpuCrypto.hsm_cost_high_str]</code> each before integration and certification; rate limiting may impact legitimate high-frequency users</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Edge ML</strong> (mobile/IoT devices)</td><td>Physical access; side-channel attacks; model extraction</td><td>Secure boot; a suitable TEE; authenticated encrypted model storage; PUF-backed device identity</td><td>Protected working set, paging support, power cost, and physical-attack resistance depend on the platform</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Cloud ML Training</strong> (multi-tenant platform)</td><td>Data poisoning; backdoor injection; gradient leakage</td><td>Secure data pipelines with provenance; accounted DP-SGD when participation leakage is in scope; gradient verification and anomaly detection; secure aggregation for federated training; model watermarking for IP protection</td><td>DP and verification overhead depend on implementation and workload; federated aggregation requires authenticated, confidential communication</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Public-Facing LLM</strong> (chatbot/API)</td><td>Prompt injection; data extraction (training leakage); abuse/overuse</td><td>Instruction-data separation; input/output policy checks; isolated execution; authentication and rate budgets</td><td>Measure latency and false-positive cost; filtering and confidence scores do not guarantee resistance to injection</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Multi-Party ML</strong> (cross-organizational training)</td><td>Data sharing restrictions; curious or malicious participants; regulatory duties</td><td>Federated learning (no raw data sharing); SMPC for secure aggregation; accounted differential privacy when participation leakage is in scope; homomorphic encryption for selected operations</td><td>Communication and cryptographic overhead depend on topology and protocol; utility must be measured for the selected privacy mechanism; requires agreements for liability</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Critical Infrastructure</strong> (autonomous vehicles, power grids)</td><td>Supply chain compromise; real-time adversarial attacks; safety-critical failures</td><td>Hardware attestation (trusted platform module, or TPM, and PUF); Secure Boot and runtime integrity checks; redundant model validation; fault injection detection; fail-safe fallback mechanisms</td><td>Validate physical threat coverage, latency, fail-safe behavior, and the required assurance process</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>Defense Selection Framework</strong>: Maps deployment contexts to threat-specific defensive strategies and deployment trade-offs. The framework provides starting points for security architecture design, highlighting primary threats, recommended defense combinations, and key implementation trade-offs across seven common ML system deployment scenarios. {#tbl-defense-selection-framework tbl-colwidths=\"[18,18,35,29]\"}</p>\n<p>The chapter introduced six hardware-level attack categories, four hardware-security primitives (@tbl-hw-security-comparison), and a framework mapping seven deployment contexts to full-stack defenses (@tbl-defense-selection-framework). Apply that combined mapping to the scenarios below.</p>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li>[ ] <strong>Primitive matching</strong>: match fault injection on model weights and side-channel extraction from the inference pipeline to the most relevant hardware-security primitives and runtime controls across the critical-infrastructure and edge rows of @tbl-defense-selection-framework.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Edge defense stack</strong>: select a combination of hardware and deployment controls for an edge inference deployment facing physical access, side-channel attacks, and model extraction.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>Supply-chain gap</strong>: identify which supply-chain compromises remain outside the four hardware primitives' guarantees in the selected edge or cloud-training deployment, and specify the provenance or inspection evidence needed to assess those risks.</li>\n<li>[ ] <strong>TEE vs. attestation</strong>: distinguish runtime isolation from cryptographic evidence about an execution environment, and identify which fault-injection, counterfeit-hardware, and output-leakage risks remain outside those guarantees.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A recurring theme across @tbl-defense-selection-framework is the cost of privacy. Differential privacy appears in three of seven deployment contexts, and each such row exposes an accuracy or systems cost that must be weighed against regulatory mandates and risk tolerance. The table is therefore a deployment triage tool rather than a universal recipe, because serialization, artifact integrity, access control, runtime isolation, and privacy budgets each defend a different boundary. Selecting the right defenses for a context settles what to build; the maturity model settles the order in which to build it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sec-46\" class=\"cs-h3\">Security and privacy maturity model</h3>\n<p>Deploying homomorphic encryption or a red-team harness while model checkpoints sit in an unauthenticated bucket and API keys lack per-principal budgets leaves the easiest attack paths wide open. A maturity model asks which system property is currently unprotected: access boundary, data privacy, model integrity, adversarial robustness, or governance evidence. The dependency structure in @tbl-security-privacy-maturity-model keeps that choice tied to the boundary being defended.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"cipher-table\">\n<thead><tr><th><strong>Maturity layer</strong></th><th><strong>Primary question</strong></th><th><strong>Control family</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Access and configuration boundary</td><td>Who can touch data, weights, deployments, and logs?</td><td>Least privilege, service identity, encrypted transport, audit logging</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Data and privacy boundary</td><td>What can be learned from training, fine-tuning, or output?</td><td>Privacy accounting, output limiting, secure aggregation, sensitive-data isolation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Model integrity boundary</td><td>How is the validated model and data path authenticated?</td><td>Signed artifacts, registry controls, hash checks, release gates, rollback paths</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adversarial and abuse boundary</td><td>What can an adaptive attacker extract, infer, or induce?</td><td>Query monitoring, rate limiting, robustness evaluation, red-team exercises</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Governance and evidence boundary</td><td>Which obligations must be proven after deployment?</td><td>Compliance mapping, incident records, retention policy, reproducible control checks</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>: <strong>ML Security and Privacy Maturity Model</strong>: Security controls should be added according to the threat model and deployment context. The order is not a calendar; it is a dependency structure from basic access boundaries to stronger privacy, integrity, adversarial, and governance controls. {#tbl-security-privacy-maturity-model tbl-colwidths=\"[25,35,40]\"}</p>\n<p>The maturity layers are cumulative. Access controls and encrypted channels close paths that model-interface defenses do not cover, such as an exposed registry. Privacy analysis begins with the original training data and continues through fine-tuning, personalization, and telemetry feedback loops. When DP is required, the release budget becomes a system resource covering the relevant training, selection, and evaluation mechanisms that access protected data. Model-integrity controls protect the artifact path: signed manifests, key management, hash checks, and release gates connect the loaded model to the approved version, subject to the enforcement boundary remaining intact.</p>\n<p>Adversarial evaluation tests deliberate, adaptive behavior in addition to accidental failures. Query monitoring and output limiting constrain extraction attempts, while robustness evaluation and red-team exercises test whether an attacker can induce unsafe behavior. Trusted execution environments and certified robustness defenses protect different boundaries, so each needs a threat model that justifies its costs and assumptions. Governance defines obligations and evidence requirements from the start, rather than entering only after technical controls. Audit logs, compliance mappings, incident records, and reproducible checks then help assess whether those obligations remain satisfied as the model, data, and traffic change.</p>\n<p>Different domains traverse the model differently. A healthcare deployment prioritizes privacy accounting and governance evidence before broad patient-facing use. A financial fraud system emphasizes access control, model integrity, and abuse monitoring because extraction and evasion directly change loss exposure. An autonomous system emphasizes adversarial robustness and incident evidence because safety depends on behavior under distribution shift and physical-world perturbations. The point is not to follow a universal implementation calendar; it is to make the threat model determine which control boundary must mature next.</p>\n<p>The evidence produced by these controls supports a separate governance decision: which uses are permitted, who accepts residual risk, and who can require remediation. @Sec-responsible-ai-governance-assurance-lifecycle develops those institutional responsibilities. Here the engineering obligation is to make access decisions, release budgets, and incident histories verifiable. By sequencing controls around those boundaries, the maturity model moves security from reactive patching toward proactive threat control, though even well-designed systems can still fail when teams mistake a local mechanism for a complete guarantee.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-47\" class=\"cs-h2\">Fallacies and Pitfalls</h2>\n<p>A common ML security failure is treating a local mechanism as a system guarantee. Obscurity, differential privacy libraries, federated learning, or encrypted storage each solve one part of the threat model, but none substitutes for lifecycle-wide reasoning about data, models, interfaces, hardware, and distributed scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Security through obscurity provides adequate protection for machine learning models.</em></p>\n<p>Hiding architectures or parameters does not establish security when an attacker can learn functionality through an exposed API. The extraction examples in @sec-security-privacy-model-extraction-defenses-8f3b show why query budgets, returned information, and the target task must be evaluated together; no universal query count or accuracy threshold applies. Adversarial examples can also transfer from surrogate models, although success depends on the models, data, and perturbation constraints [@papernot2016transferability]. Kerckhoffs's principle motivates defenses that remain useful when the mechanism is known. Access controls and query limits constrain exposure, output restrictions reduce available information, and monitoring supports investigation; watermarking serves attribution rather than preventing copying.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Assuming that differential privacy automatically ensures privacy without considering implementation details.</em></p>\n<p>Treating differential privacy as a universal solution without specifying adjacency, parameter selection, and budget tracking leaves the release claim incomplete. The same $\\PrivacyBudget$ can describe different protections under different adjacency relations, values of $\\PrivacyAdditiveSlack$, sampling schemes, and compositions, while utility depends on the model, task, and data. Privacy budgets compose across additional data-dependent releases: releasing 10 separately trained models at $\\PrivacyBudget=1.0$ each gives a basic-composition bound of $\\PrivacyBudget=10$, not $\\PrivacyBudget=1.0$, although a mechanism-specific accountant may provide a tighter bound. Retraining, tuning, and evaluation mechanisms that access protected data must be included in the accounting boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Federated learning inherently provides privacy protection without additional safeguards.</em></p>\n<p>This misconception assumes decentralization automatically ensures privacy, but individual updates can expose membership or reconstruct features of local training data. As examined in @sec-security-privacy-federated-learning-3834, effective federated privacy requires controls matched to the adversary: secure aggregation hides individual contributions from the coordinator, an accounted DP mechanism bounds record- or user-level participation leakage under its stated adjacency relation, and authenticated encryption protects updates in transit. Organizations cannot claim privacy merely because raw records remain on devices; the protocol still releases information derived from those records.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Planning poisoning defenses only for large training-data compromise.</em></p>\n<p>A small, strategically chosen set of poisoned examples can target a decision without causing a conspicuous drop in overall validation accuracy [@shafahi2018poison]. Backdoor attacks make this distinction explicit. A model may behave normally on clean inputs while producing attacker-chosen behavior when a trigger appears [@gu2017badnets]. The required poisoning fraction and resulting attack success depend on attacker access, the training procedure, and the trigger; a universal percentage would conceal those dependencies. Defenses must therefore evaluate targeted behavior and provenance, including user-generated ingestion paths, rather than treating a low estimated contamination rate as a safety guarantee; @sec-robust-ai-data-poisoning-defenses-d070 develops them. Retraining or transfer learning also requires renewed testing instead of an assumption that the trigger has disappeared.</p>\n<p><strong>Fallacy</strong>: <em>Security can be isolated from the rest of the ML system.</em></p>\n<p>Component defenses leave gaps when the same asset remains exposed through another path. Per-user query quotas, output perturbation, and top-$\\TopClassCount$ filtering at a public API do not protect weights in an unauthenticated registry or query-response pairs in exposed logs. As established in @sec-security-privacy-security-threat-modeling-ml-systems-f60d, threat modeling must follow the asset through collection, training, storage, deployment, and monitoring. A failed boundary can defeat controls that depend on it, while independent controls may still limit the compromise. The engineering task is to identify both the bypass path and the protections that remain effective, rather than assume either that one protected interface secures the whole system or that every local failure defeats every guarantee.</p>\n<p><strong>Pitfall</strong>: <em>Underestimating the attack surface expansion in distributed ML systems.</em></p>\n<p>Organizations secure single-node ML systems effectively but fail to recognize that distributed architectures introduce additional trust boundaries and communication channels. Each link is a channel whose endpoints must authenticate each other and protect the traffic between them, even when they reuse node-level credentials. An all-to-all topology has $\\mathcal{O}(\\ClusterNodeCount^2)$ links, whereas a ring has $\\mathcal{O}(\\ClusterNodeCount)$ links. Link count alone does not determine vulnerability count, but every additional node, credential, and channel must be included in the threat model.</p>\n<p>In the reference training cluster of <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.total_gpus_str]</code> GPUs (<code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.node_count_str]</code> nodes with <code>[ReferenceClusterAttackSurface.gpus_per_node_str]</code> GPUs each), every node is at once a gradient contributor, a fabric endpoint, and a key holder. @Sec-security-privacy-fleet-trust-boundaries therefore treats worker admission, collective traffic, shared accelerators, and attestation as boundaries of their own. Federated learning with 10,000 clients creates 10,000 separately managed endpoints whose attestation support and coverage may differ. Aggregation methods that inspect individual updates also need reconciliation with protocols that hide those updates; combining the two requires a design that preserves both guarantees. Effective distributed ML security therefore requires threat modeling the added nodes and boundaries: securing inter-node channels, managing identity across security domains, and coordinating policies across heterogeneous infrastructure.</p>\n<p>Recognizing that the shift from a single node to a distributed cluster adds nodes, channels, credentials, and trust boundaries rules out perimeter-only thinking. Security for the machine learning fleet must be layered, measured, and tied to the full lifecycle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sec-48\" class=\"cs-h2\">Summary</h2>\n<p>Privacy and security protect the machine learning fleet's models, data, hardware, and service boundaries from extraction, poisoning, manipulation, and unauthorized access. The breach cases establish the boundaries a fleet must defend: Strava shows that an aggregate release can disclose behavior without leaking a single record, Stuxnet exposes supply-chain trust, the Jeep Cherokee hack exposes insufficient isolation, and Mirai exposes weak endpoint identity. Learned behavior adds further boundaries because poisoned data, extraction queries, and prompt injection can compromise a system through otherwise legitimate interfaces. The fleet adds boundaries of its own that no API gateway sees: the gradient each worker contributes, the fabric that carries it, the accelerators tenants share, and the evidence that each node runs approved code. Hardware trust anchors protect execution, provenance connects artifacts to release decisions, runtime controls contain abuse, and differential privacy bounds participation leakage. These mechanisms compose only when each is tied to the actor and exposure it addresses.</p>\n<p>Their costs span all three C$^3$ axes and can enter different terms of the Fleet Law (@eq-fleet-law). Output shaping, input validation, redundant gradient checks, DP-SGD clipping and noise, and protected execution can add work to $\\IronLawComputeTime$. Fabric encryption, gathering individual updates for some robust aggregation methods, and masked federated uploads can increase $\\CommunicationTime(\\DeviceCount)$; offload and overlap determine how much reaches the critical path. Admission checks, key rotation, privacy-budget checks, and aggregation thresholds can add to $\\SynchronizationTime(\\DeviceCount)$ when the workflow waits for them. Federated protocols also need deadlines and dropout handling rather than an assumption that every client responds. Capacity planning must measure these costs in the deployed path so that operating targets do not depend on bypassing a control under load.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Takeaways</div>\n<ul class=\"cs-list\">\n<li><strong>The model is the attack surface</strong>: ML systems inherit ordinary server threats but add attacks against the learned boundary itself. Poisoned data, model extraction, adversarial inputs, and prompt injection can compromise behavior without exploiting a traditional software vulnerability.</li>\n<li><strong>Extraction is bounded by cumulative budgets</strong>: A daily rate limit slows a query-based extractor without capping what it collects. A lifetime per-principal budget priced at break-even with retraining, paired with an output contract that limits what each response reveals, removes the cost advantage of copying, but only as far as principals map to real actors.</li>\n<li><strong>Privacy is an accounted budget</strong>: Because models can encode information about sensitive data, a correctly executing model can disclose training data through memorization and membership inference, so privacy cannot be treated as anonymization at the perimeter. Differential privacy (principle \\ref{pri-information-leakage}) offers bounded $(\\PrivacyBudget, \\PrivacyAdditiveSlack)$ claims only when all data-dependent DP mechanisms are composed correctly; querying a fixed DP-trained model is post-processing and does not add training privacy loss.</li>\n<li><strong>Generative systems need enforceable control boundaries</strong>: Prompt injection, jailbreaks, and PII leakage pass through normal text channels. Treat model output as untrusted, keep instructions separate from untrusted content, and keep authorization in an external policy layer.</li>\n<li><strong>The fleet's own interfaces are trust boundaries</strong>: An unbounded malicious update can dominate a mean; the examined gather-based implementation of robust aggregation adds about $\\DataParallelDegree/2$ times the traffic, an implementation cost rather than a universal lower bound. Worker admission, collective traffic, and shared accelerators need separate controls, and admission does not replace update checks, because an admitted worker can still submit a malicious update.</li>\n<li><strong>Provenance bounds trust and enables recovery</strong>: A model can be trusted only as far as its record of data, code, weights, and hardware reaches (principle \\ref{pri-provenance-bounds-trust}). Signed datasets, locked dependencies, registry permissions, and deployment attestations let operators answer which data, code, principal, node, and checkpoint produced a suspect model. Without that chain, rollback and forensics become guesswork at fleet speed.</li>\n<li><strong>Hardware trust has explicit boundaries</strong>: TEEs isolate execution, secure boot verifies an authorized boot chain, and HSMs protect keys and cryptographic operations. These mechanisms address different trust boundaries; they do not establish that model behavior is correct or that an authorized output preserves privacy.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<p>A fleet can execute every authorized operation correctly and still disclose sensitive information through an approved release. The information leakage invariant (principle \\ref{pri-information-leakage}) therefore makes the release boundary as important as the execution boundary. A deployment decision must establish both who can access the computation and what its outputs allow an observer to learn.</p>\n<div class=\"cs-callout\"><div class=\"cs-callout-title\">Chapter Connection</div>\n<p>Security and privacy controls address adversarial intent and control, establishing who can infer, manipulate, disable, or authorize what across data, model, interface, and infrastructure boundaries. Those controls decide who may reach the model and what its releases may disclose. They cannot show that its answers stay valid once inputs, data distributions, or hardware depart from the conditions under which it was validated. A fleet can pass every access check and every health signal while its predictions degrade without raising an error, whether from road conditions its training data never covered, from a crafted input that satisfies the interface contract, or from a faulty accelerator that keeps returning plausible numbers. Detecting and bounding that silent failure requires behavioral evidence about accuracy, calibration, and fallback under environmental shift, input-level attack, and system faults, which is the robustness problem the next chapter takes up.</p>\n</div>\n<p>↪ Drift detection, adversarial-defense evaluation, and poisoning defenses are developed in @sec-robust-ai.</p>", "quizzes": [{"section": "The Expanded Attack Surface", "type": "MCQ", "question": "A security team deploys an enterprise ML inference service behind a web application firewall with strict mutual TLS (mTLS) authentication and rate limiting. Despite zero network-level intrusions, an external adversary reconstructs the model's proprietary decision boundaries and extracts memorized training records. What fundamental characteristic of ML systems explains this vulnerability?", "choices": ["ML models act on and compress learned data, exposing an algorithmic attack surface through properly authenticated inputs and valid response signals.", "mTLS certificates fail to encrypt floating-point tensors at the network transport layer.", "Traditional firewalls cannot inspect JSON payloads formatted as numerical arrays.", "Token-bucket rate limiting in standard firewalls is incapable of tracking HTTP/2 request streams."], "answer": "The correct answer is A. Traditional IT security protects the infrastructure perimeter through access control, transport encryption, and firewalls. However, ML systems introduce an orthogonal algorithmic attack surface: properly authenticated API queries containing crafted inputs leverage the model's learned parameters directly to elicit memorized data or reconstruct decision boundaries. The assertion regarding unencrypted tensors confuses transport-layer security with model-layer information leakage. The claim about JSON payload inspection confuses firewall protocol parsing with semantic model exploitation. The assertion regarding rate limiting confuses stream accounting with algorithmic leakage."}, {"section": "The Expanded Attack Surface", "type": "MCQ", "question": "An ML platform engineer must classify two distinct failure modes: (1) a malicious actor submits crafted audio noise to cause an emergency dispatch classifier to miss distress calls, and (2) an observer examines a public salary prediction model and infers whether a specific executive was included in the training set. How should these two issues be categorized according to threat model and objective?", "choices": ["(1) is a privacy violation targeting confidentiality; (2) is a security breach targeting system availability.", "(1) is an adversarial security attack targeting model integrity; (2) is a privacy leakage issue targeting participation inference.", "Both (1) and (2) are pure infrastructure security incidents requiring network-layer firewall rule updates.", "(1) is a stochastic robustness failure; (2) is an unauthorized access control violation requiring credential rotation."], "answer": "The correct answer is B. Failure (1) represents a security attack where an active adversary intentionally crafts inputs to compromise system integrity (evasion/misclassification). Failure (2) represents a privacy issue where an observer infers sensitive individual participation from model outputs even without attacking system availability or exploiting software vulnerabilities. Classifying the audio perturbation as a privacy breach confuses confidentiality with operational integrity. Treating both as network infrastructure incidents ignores the algorithmic nature of the attacks. Classifying evasion as stochastic robustness overlooks the intentional worst-case adversary."}, {"section": "Learning from Security Breaches", "type": "MCQ", "question": "In 2018, an aggregated activity heatmap revealed sensitive military locations through distinctive activity patterns in sparsely populated areas. What fundamental systems privacy vulnerability did this incident demonstrate?", "choices": ["The platform suffered an unencrypted SQL database breach that leaked user account names and plaintext GPS coordinates.", "An adversary executed a physical side-channel power analysis attack against user GPS hardware chips.", "Aggregation can expose sensitive locations in sparse environments where high-dimensional behavioral traces retain distinct structural patterns.", "The web application failed to validate TLS 1.3 certificates on incoming client telemetry streams."], "answer": "The correct answer is C. The Strava incident showed that aggregation alone can expose sensitive locations when behavioral structure is distinctive. An individual-level DP guarantee would not automatically hide an institution or population-level activity pattern; its protected contribution and release objective must be stated. Even without user identifiers, unique coordinate patterns in low-density regions directly reveal sensitive operational locations. A database breach hypothesis is incorrect because no raw database or identity records were stolen; the visualization itself leaked the information. A power analysis hypothesis confuses physical side channels with macro-level telemetry visualization. A TLS certificate hypothesis confuses transport encryption with semantic information leakage in published aggregates."}, {"section": "Learning from Security Breaches", "type": "SHORT", "question": "The Stuxnet worm modified industrial centrifuge control parameters while continuing to report normal operational telemetry to facility operators. Explain the direct architectural parallel between this attack mechanism and a data poisoning backdoor attack on an ML model.", "choices": [], "answer": "In both cases, the compromise subverts operational integrity while silently evading standard health monitoring. Just as Stuxnet altered physical controller commands while spoofing healthy telemetry, a backdoored ML model maintains high validation accuracy on clean benchmark data while silently triggering malicious misclassifications only when an attacker's specific trigger pattern is present."}, {"section": "Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment", "type": "MCQ", "question": "A public image-classification API admits untrusted annotations. Its threat assessment finds poisoning both likely and severely damaging, while accessible behavioral extraction has limited competitive impact in this deployment. How should the team prioritize these threats?", "choices": ["Data poisoning is High-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring automated dataset validation; model extraction is High-Likelihood / Low-to-Moderate-Impact addressed via rate limiting and output truncation.", "Both threats are Low-Likelihood / Low-Impact and should be deferred until a physical hardware breach occurs.", "Model extraction is Low-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring full homomorphic encryption; data poisoning is High-Likelihood / Low-Impact requiring only API key rotation.", "Data poisoning is Low-Likelihood / High-Impact requiring physical air-gaps; model extraction is Low-Likelihood / Low-Impact requiring no action."], "answer": "The correct answer is A. Under the supplied likelihood and impact assessments, poisoning warrants priority controls on data admission and provenance; query budgets and output restrictions are proportionate starting points for extraction. Their effectiveness still requires evaluation. These placements are deployment assumptions, not permanent ranks of attack families; sensitive data or greater copying harm could change them."}, {"section": "Systematic Threat Analysis and Risk Assessment", "type": "SHORT", "question": "A security audit notes that an ML platform's risk assessment consists only of a qualitative list of potential failure modes without actionable boundaries. Name the four essential fields required for a complete, operational ML threat model and explain the role of the boundary field.", "choices": [], "answer": "The four essential fields of an operational ML threat model are Asset (what must remain confidential, intact, or available), Boundary (where trust levels change), Adversary (permitted capabilities, knowledge, and goal), and Control (the permission, mechanism, or observable signal that constrains the attack or supports a response). The Boundary field defines the exact interface where trust transitions, such as between external user prompts and system instructions, raw training data and the model registry, or shared tenant processes on an accelerator, determining where enforcement must occur."}, {"section": "Model-Specific Attack Vectors", "type": "MCQ", "question": "A security architect must deploy defenses against two distinct model theft vectors: (1) an unauthorized party copying serialized model checkpoints from a cloud bucket, and (2) a competitor sending millions of synthetic queries to a public REST endpoint to distill a student model. Which combination of defenses correctly targets each vector?", "choices": ["Vector (1) requires output rounding and top-$k$ truncation; Vector (2) requires hardware security modules for model weights at rest.", "Vector (1) requires differential privacy during training; Vector (2) requires air-gapping the inference server from the internet.", "Vector (1) requires encrypted storage, signed model artifacts, and strict IAM/RBAC permissions; Vector (2) requires rate limiting, query anomaly monitoring, and confidence-score truncation.", "Both vectors are mitigated exclusively by converting model checkpoints to Python pickle files."], "answer": "The correct answer is C. Exact artifact theft (Vector 1) is a storage/registry security failure constrained by encrypting artifacts at rest and enforcing strict IAM/RBAC access controls. Signing supplies artifact-authenticity evidence rather than preventing an observer from copying weights. Approximate behavioral theft (Vector 2) is an API interface leakage failure mitigated by rate limiting, monitoring for systematic query probing (e.g. KL divergence anomalies), truncating confidence scores, and returning only top-$k$ predictions. Applying output truncation to checkpoint theft misplaces API controls on storage assets. Air-gapping an inference server disables public API functionality rather than managing query information leakage. Using Python pickle serialization increases vulnerability by introducing arbitrary code execution risks."}, {"section": "Model-Specific Attack Vectors", "type": "SHORT", "question": "Data poisoning is formalized as a bilevel optimization problem. State the attacker's untargeted outer objective and the defender's inner training objective, and explain why evaluating a poison's learning effect can be computationally demanding.", "choices": [], "answer": "The untargeted outer objective chooses poisoning data to increase the trained model's loss on the target test set; the inner objective trains on the combined clean and poisoned data. Evaluating that learning effect may require repeated training runs, approximations, or surrogate feedback. The nested attack objective does not imply that every poisoning defense must simulate full training to convergence."}, {"section": "Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries", "type": "MCQ", "question": "A 64-worker data-parallel job replaces ring AllReduce with a gather-based coordinate-wise median implementation: every worker gathers every individual gradient before computing the median. Approximately how does per-worker gradient traffic change under this schedule?", "choices": ["It rises by about 32 times, because this implementation gathers the 63 peer gradients instead of moving about twice the gradient size in the ring.", "It is unchanged, because this median implementation uses only the sum returned by ring AllReduce.", "It roughly doubles, because gathering all individual gradients costs one extra ring pass.", "It falls, because the implementation discards outlier gradients before receiving them."], "answer": "The correct answer is A. The specified gather receives $(N-1)M$ bytes per worker, compared with $2(N-1)M/N$ for ring AllReduce, giving $N/2=32$. Ring summation does not expose individual gradients. This is the cost of the stated gather schedule, not a lower bound for every robust aggregation protocol."}, {"section": "Fleet-Internal Trust Boundaries", "type": "SHORT", "question": "Two hypothetical serving configurations use the same allocated accelerator capacity, models, request mix, and latency target. The baseline sustains 1,000 tokens/s and the configuration with additional security controls sustains 850 tokens/s. Explain what this 15% capacity loss establishes and why comparing a whole GPU against a smaller MIG partition would not isolate security overhead.", "choices": [], "answer": "The matched scenario requires capacity planning around a 15% throughput loss; it does not measure a universal isolation cost or identify a particular mechanism. A whole-GPU versus smaller-partition comparison also changes available compute and memory capacity. MIG partitioning can affect pooling and fragmentation, which must be distinguished from execution overhead; it does not inherently imply secure context switching. Measure the actual configuration under its stated security boundary."}, {"section": "Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities", "type": "MCQ", "question": "Vulnerabilities like Meltdown and Spectre exploit transient execution in modern processors. What microarchitectural mechanism can let an attacker infer protected data across an intended isolation boundary?", "choices": ["Transient instructions can encode protected data in cache state that remains measurable after the processor discards their architectural effects.", "Speculative execution permanently overwrites physical DRAM cells with ciphertext keys without notifying the OS kernel.", "The CPU floating-point unit truncates FP32 tensor activations to INT8 during branch prediction.", "Speculative branch predictors disable hardware-level AES encryption engines on PCIe accelerator interconnects."], "answer": "The correct answer is A. Transient execution can leave cache effects even when its architectural results are discarded. Spectre exploits prediction to induce a victim to access data along a transient path; Meltdown exploits a permission-enforcement weakness during transient execution. Their prerequisites differ, but both can turn residual microarchitectural state into an observation channel. The other choices concern memory corruption, numerical representation, or bus encryption rather than this transient-execution channel."}, {"section": "Hardware-Level Security Vulnerabilities", "type": "SHORT", "question": "In laser fault injection experiments on embedded neural network microcontrollers (e.g., Breier et al., 2018), researchers targeted the assembly instructions implementing the ReLU activation function. Explain the assembly-level mechanism of this attack and its effect on the model's output.", "choices": [], "answer": "In the illustrated ReLU implementation, a laser-induced instruction skip targets `jmp end`, the jump that normally bypasses the zeroing block after the activation value has been retained. Skipping that jump lets execution enter the zeroing block, forcing the activation to zero and potentially corrupting downstream classification. The demonstrated mechanism is a control-flow fault, not extraction or modification of stored weights."}]};
